var SQ_LESSONS={a1:{title:"Ask Me Anything",tagline:"Build a chatbot that listens and talks back.",mission:"Build a chatbot that asks questions and answers back.",minutes:"45",skill:"Input, Join and Text Comparisons",goal:"By the end of this quest you will ask the user questions, keep their answer in a variable, build sentences with join, and use if else to reply to different answers.",vocab:[{term:"Input",def:"Information the user gives the program, like typing an answer.",icon:"⌨️"},{term:"answer",def:"A Sensing block that holds what the user typed last.",icon:"📥"},{term:"join",def:"An Operators block that puts two pieces of text together.",icon:"🔗"},{term:"Chatbot",def:"A program that has a conversation with a person.",icon:"🤖"},{term:"Text",def:"Letters and words. Variables can hold text, not just numbers.",icon:"🔤"}],warmup:"If you built a robot helper for our school, what three questions would it ask a new student on the first day? Think alone or share with a neighbor.",needs:["random"],project:{name:"Quiz App",part:"Part 1 of 2"},steps:[{title:"Meet your bot",short:"Start a new project and pick or paint a friendly bot.",text:"Start a new project. Pick or paint a friendly character to be your bot. Rename the sprite to <b>Bot</b>. You will turn this chatbot into a quiz app next quest.",shots:["choose-sprite"]},{title:"Ask a question",short:"Use ask and wait to ask the user for their name.",text:"In <b>Sensing</b>, find <b>ask and wait</b>. When it runs, a box appears at the bottom of the Stage and the script waits. Whatever the user types goes into the round <b>answer</b> block. Build this and click the green flag. Type your name and press Enter.",where:"Bot",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nask [Hi! What is your name?] and wait\nsay (answer) for (2) seconds",shots:["ask"],tip:"The script waits until the user presses Enter or clicks the check mark. That is the wait part of ask and wait."},{title:"Join the words",short:"Use join to say hello with their name in a sentence.",text:"Saying only a name is strange. In <b>Operators</b>, use <b>join</b> to stick two pieces of text together. Put a space after the comma in Nice to meet you, so the words do not touch.",where:"Bot",change:"replace",changeNote:"Replaces the say block in your step 2 script. Drag answer into the second slot of join.",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nask [Hi! What is your name?] and wait\nsay (join [Nice to meet you, ] (answer)) for (2) seconds",shots:["cat-operators"],tip:"join does not add spaces for you. Without the space you get Nice to meet you,Sam."},{title:"Remember the name",short:"Keep their name in a variable called name.",text:"The <b>answer</b> block changes every time you ask something new. To keep the name for later, make a variable <b>name</b> and set it to answer right after the question. Then use name in the join. Put the blocks in order.",where:"Bot",change:"replace",changeNote:"Replaces your step 3 script.",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nask [Hi! What is your name?] and wait\nset [name v] to (answer)\nsay (join [Nice to meet you, ] (name)) for (2) seconds",shots:["make-variable"],tip:"Variables can hold words, not only numbers. Uncheck the name box in the palette if you do not want it showing on the Stage."},{title:"Make decisions",short:"Ask a yes or no question and reply with if else.",text:"Ask a yes or no question. Use <b>if else</b> and <b>=</b> to reply in two different ways. Put the blocks in order and add them to the bottom of your script.",where:"Bot",change:"add",changeNote:"Add to the bottom of your step 4 script, under the say block.",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"ask [Do you like pizza? Type yes or no.] and wait\nif <(answer) = [yes]> then\nsay [Me too! Pizza is the best.] for (2) seconds\nelse\nsay [Really? More for me then.] for (2) seconds\nend",tip:"= ignores capital letters, so YES and yes both match. But = does not ignore extra spaces: yes with a space at the end is not the same as yes, so it goes to else."},{title:"Ask for a number",short:"Ask for a number and reply one way if it is bigger than five.",text:"Build this yourself. Ask the user to pick a number from 1 to 10. If the number is more than 5, the bot says one thing. If not, it says something else. Use the person's name in one reply.<br>Blocks that help: ask and wait, if else, the greater than block from Operators, join, say for seconds.",where:"Bot",change:"add",changeNote:"Add to the bottom of your Bot's green flag script.",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"ask [Pick a number from 1 to 10.] and wait\nif <(answer) > [5]> then\nsay (join [That is a big one, ] (name)) for (2) seconds\nelse\nsay [Small but strong!] for (2) seconds\nend",tip:"The greater than block compares numbers. If the user types seven in letters, Scratch compares it as text, and the reply may surprise you."},{title:"Your own question",short:"Write one more question of your own and give two replies.",text:"Add one more question about anything you like: favorite animal, game, or food. Use ask and wait, then if else to give two replies. Write it with the frame first, then build it.",where:"Bot",change:"add",changeNote:"Add to the bottom of your Bot's green flag script.",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"ask [What is your favorite animal?] and wait\nif <(answer) = [cat]> then\nsay [Cats are my favorite too!] for (2) seconds\nelse\nsay (join (answer) [ is a great animal!]) for (2) seconds\nend",frame:"My question: ____?\nIf they type ____, the bot says: ____\nIf they type anything else, the bot says: ____",tip:"Do not ask for private information like a full name, address, or phone number. A first name is enough.",stretch:!0},{title:"Test with surprise answers",short:"Let someone chat with your bot, then fix the surprises.",text:"Have a classmate chat with your bot while you watch. Do not help them. Did they type something you did not expect, like yeah instead of yes? Make your else replies friendly for surprises. Then save and share.",solo:"No one free? Test it yourself as if you had never seen it. Type surprise answers: YES, yeah, yes with a space at the end, seven, and 7. Write down what the bot did for each one, then fix one thing.",tip:"Your else reply catches everything that is not a match. Make it something like I did not understand, but that is OK!"}],quiz:[{q:"The user types Sam. What does the bot say?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nask [What is your name?] and wait\nsay (join [Hi] (answer)) for (2) seconds",choices:["Hi Sam","HiSam","Hi answer","join Hi Sam"],answer:1,explain:"join does not add a space. There is no space after Hi, so the words touch: HiSam."},{q:"The user types YES in capital letters. What does the bot say?",blocks:"ask [Do you like pizza?] and wait\nif <(answer) = [yes]> then\nsay [Me too!] for (2) seconds\nelse\nsay [More for me!] for (2) seconds\nend",choices:["More for me!","Nothing","Me too!","Both lines"],answer:2,explain:"= ignores capital letters, so YES matches yes and the if part runs."}],challenge:{title:"Guess my number",short:"The bot picks a secret number and gives higher or lower clues.",text:"The bot picks a secret number from 1 to 10 and keeps it in a variable. The user guesses. If the guess is too low, the bot says Higher. If it is too high, it says Lower. The user keeps guessing until they get it, then the bot says You got it. Look at pick random in Operators and repeat until in Control.",answer:"when green flag clicked\nset [secret v] to (pick random (1) to (10))\nask [Guess my number from 1 to 10!] and wait\nrepeat until <(answer) = (secret)>\nif <(answer) < (secret)> then\nsay [Higher!] for (1) seconds\nelse\nsay [Lower!] for (1) seconds\nend\nask [Guess again!] and wait\nend\nsay [You got it!] for (2) seconds"},checklist:["My bot asks for a name and uses it with join","The name is kept in a variable","At least one yes or no question with if else","At least 3 questions in total","I tested surprise answers"],submit:'Share your chatbot. Name your project anything you like (idea: "Robo Buddy"). Paste the link. You will turn it into a quiz app next quest.',teacherNotes:"Pacing (45 min): warmup 3, meet bot 3, ask 5, join 5, remember 6, decisions 7, number 8, test 6. Your own question is a stretch.<br>This is the first lesson that teaches ask and wait, answer, join, and text in variables (Apps path). The challenge uses pick random (Games teaches it), so Apps students see the random primer.<br><b>Common misconceptions</b>: missing space inside join (HiSam) is very common and a great teachable moment; thinking answer remembers every reply (it only holds the latest one); = ignores capitals but not extra spaces; comparing a number typed in words with greater than.<br><b>Accommodations</b>: typing is moderate. Students may copy question text from the step cards. Allow speech-to-text for the frame. For students who type slowly, the core is 3 questions; the stretch question is optional. English learners can write bot replies in their home language. Remind everyone not to ask users for personal information beyond a first name."},a2:{title:"Quiz Builder",tagline:"Turn your chatbot into a quiz app that keeps score.",mission:"Build a quiz app that checks answers and keeps score.",minutes:"45",skill:"App Logic, Score and Feedback",goal:"By the end of this quest you will build a quiz that asks questions, checks right and wrong answers with clickable buttons or typed answers, keeps a score, and shows a results message.",vocab:[{term:"Button",def:"Something on the screen you click to make the app do something.",icon:"🔘"},{term:"Feedback",def:"How the app tells the user what happened, like Correct or Try again.",icon:"✅"},{term:"Score",def:"A variable that counts how many answers were right.",icon:"🏆"},{term:"State",def:"Where the app is right now, like which question it is on.",icon:"📍"}],warmup:"Think of any app or game you use. Name three buttons on it. How do you know they are buttons: shape, color, or words? Think alone or share with a neighbor.",needs:[],starter:{name:"Quiz App starter",contains:"A sprite named Bot. Its chatbot script from a1 (ask, join, if else) may be there, but its hat block is changed to when c key pressed so it does not run on the green flag. No score or question variables yet."},project:{name:"Quiz App",part:"Part 2 of 2"},steps:[{title:"Plan your quiz",short:"Open your app, pick a topic, and write two questions.",text:"Open your Quiz App from last quest (or the starter project). Your Bot will be the quiz host. First, move the chatbot out of the way: drag a <b>when key pressed</b> hat (set to c) onto the chat script and remove its green flag hat, so it does not run during the quiz. Then pick a topic you know well and write <b>2 questions</b>. Each has 3 choices: A, B, and C. You can add questions 3 to 5 later.",frame:"Topic: ____\n\nQ1: ____?\n  A: ____   B: ____   C: ____\n  Right answer: ____\n\nQ2: ____?\n  A: ____   B: ____   C: ____\n  Right answer: ____",tip:"Short questions are easier to read on the Stage. Try to keep each question under 12 words."},{title:"Set up score and question",short:"Make the score and question variables. Reset them on the green flag.",text:"Make two variables, For all sprites: <b>score</b> counts right answers, and <b>question</b> remembers which question the quiz is on. On the green flag, score goes to 0 and question goes to 1. Every new quiz starts fresh. Show the score on the Stage so the player can see it.",where:"Bot",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [score v] to (0)\nset [question v] to (1)\nshow variable [score v]",shots:["make-variable"],tip:"Check the box next to question too while you build. It helps you see what the app is doing. Uncheck it when you are done."},{title:"Question 1: pick how players answer",short:"Pick buttons or typed answers. Ask question one and check it.",text:"Pick one way for players to answer. Both check the answer, add 1 to score if it is right, give feedback if it is wrong, and then add 1 to question.<br><b>Clickable buttons</b>: make a variable <b>right answer</b>. The Bot sets it to the right letter, shows the question, and waits until a button is clicked. Paint a sprite with the letter A and name it <b>Button A</b>. Give it the bottom script. Then duplicate it to make Button B and Button C, and change the letter in each copy.<br><b>Typed answers</b>: the Bot asks and the player types the answer. Everything is on the Bot.",where:"Bot (and Button A if you pick buttons)",change:"add",changeNote:"The Bot blocks go at the bottom of the Bot's green flag script from step 2. The when this sprite clicked script is a new script on Button A.",scaffold:"full",options:[{label:"Clickable buttons",short:"Bot shows Q1 and waits. Button A checks if A is the right answer.",blocks:"set [right answer v] to [A]\nsay [Q1: Which planet is closest to the Sun? A: Mercury B: Mars C: Venus]\nwait until <(question) = [2]>\n\nwhen this sprite clicked\nif <(right answer) = [A]> then\nchange [score v] by (1)\nsay [Correct!] for (1) seconds\nelse\nsay [Not this one.] for (1) seconds\nend\nchange [question v] by (1)"},{label:"Typed answers",short:"Bot asks Q1, checks the typed answer, and gives feedback.",blocks:"ask [Q1: Which planet is closest to the Sun?] and wait\nif <(answer) = [Mercury]> then\nchange [score v] by (1)\nsay [Correct!] for (1) seconds\nelse\nsay [Not quite. It is Mercury.] for (2) seconds\nend\nchange [question v] by (1)"}],shots:["make-variable","ask"],tip:"Test the wrong path too! Click a wrong button or type a wrong answer: the score must stay the same, and the quiz must still go on. Buttons: in Button B, change the A in the if block to B."},{title:"Question 2",short:"Add question two the same way. Use the same tab as before.",text:"Add question 2 to the bottom of the Bot's green flag script. Pick the same tab you picked in step 3. The pattern is the same, only the words and the right answer change. Put the blocks in order.<br><b>Buttons</b>: you do not need to change the buttons at all. They check the right answer variable, and the Bot changes it for each question.",where:"Bot",change:"add",changeNote:"Add to the bottom of the Bot's green flag script, under your question 1 blocks.",scaffold:"parsons",options:[{label:"Clickable buttons",short:"Set the right answer, show Q2, and wait for question 3.",blocks:"set [right answer v] to [B]\nsay [Q2: How many legs does a spider have? A: 6 B: 8 C: 10]\nwait until <(question) = [3]>"},{label:"Typed answers",short:"Ask Q2, check it, give feedback, and add 1 to question.",blocks:"ask [Q2: How many legs does a spider have?] and wait\nif <(answer) = [8]> then\nchange [score v] by (1)\nsay [Correct!] for (1) seconds\nelse\nsay [Not quite. It is 8.] for (2) seconds\nend\nchange [question v] by (1)"}],tip:"Typed answers: = ignores capital letters but not extra spaces, so 8 with a space after it is wrong. Tell the player to type just the answer."},{title:"Results screen",short:"After the last question, show the score and a message.",text:"Build this yourself at the bottom of the Bot's script. Show the score in a sentence with join. If the score is more than 1, say a happy message. If not, say an encouraging one. If you used buttons, they should hide at the end: broadcast a message like <b>quiz over</b>, and give each button a when I receive script that hides it. The buttons also need <b>show</b> on the green flag.<br>Blocks that help: join, say for seconds, if else, the greater than block, broadcast, when I receive, hide, show.",where:"Bot (and each button if you used buttons)",change:"add",changeNote:"Add to the bottom of the Bot's green flag script. Buttons get two new scripts.",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"broadcast (quiz over v)\nsay (join [You scored: ] (score)) for (3) seconds\nif <(score) > [1]> then\nsay [Quiz master!] for (2) seconds\nelse\nsay [Nice try! Play again?] for (2) seconds\nend\n\nwhen green flag clicked\nshow\n\nwhen I receive [quiz over v]\nhide",tip:"Remember the space after You scored: inside join. If you add more questions, change the number in the greater than block, like more than 3 for 5 questions."},{title:"Questions 3 to 5",short:"If you have time, add three more questions with the same pattern.",text:"Write questions 3 to 5 with the frame. Then right-click the blocks for question 2, choose <b>duplicate</b>, and change the words, the right answer, and the number. Put them before the results blocks. Buttons: the wait until number goes up by one each time.",where:"Bot",change:"add",changeNote:"Add between your question 2 blocks and your results blocks.",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"set [right answer v] to [C]\nsay [Q3: What do bees make? A: Milk B: Bread C: Honey]\nwait until <(question) = [4]>",frame:"Q3: ____?  A: ____  B: ____  C: ____  Right: ____\nQ4: ____?  A: ____  B: ____  C: ____  Right: ____\nQ5: ____?  A: ____  B: ____  C: ____  Right: ____",stretch:!0},{title:"Test every path and share",short:"Test right and wrong answers for every question, then share.",text:"Play the quiz three times: once with all right answers, once with all wrong answers, and once mixed. Check the score each time. Then have a classmate try it without your help. Save and share.",frame:"All right: score was ____ (should be ____)\nAll wrong: score was ____ (should be 0)\nMixed: score was ____ (should be ____)\nWhat I fixed: ____",solo:"No one free? Play it yourself as if you had never seen it. Look only at the Stage. Click fast, click the wrong button, or type an answer with a capital letter. Write down anything that surprised you.",tip:"Buttons: click only once per question. If you click a button again before it finishes talking, the score can go up twice."}],quiz:[{q:"right answer is B and score is 0. The player clicks Button A. What happens?",blocks:"when this sprite clicked\nif <(right answer) = [A]> then\nchange [score v] by (1)\nsay [Correct!] for (1) seconds\nelse\nsay [Not this one.] for (1) seconds\nend\nchange [question v] by (1)",choices:["It says Correct and score becomes 1","Nothing happens","It says Not this one, and the quiz stays on the same question","It says Not this one, score stays 0, and question goes up by 1"],answer:3,explain:"B is not A, so the else part runs. The last block is outside the if, so question still goes up."},{q:"This is the Bot. Nobody clicks any button. What does the Bot do?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [question v] to (1)\nsay [Q1: Which planet is closest to the Sun?]\nwait until <(question) = [2]>\nsay [Q2: How many legs does a spider have?]",choices:["Shows Q1 and keeps waiting","Shows Q1, then Q2 after 2 seconds","Shows Q2 right away","Stops with an error"],answer:0,explain:"wait until pauses the script until question is 2. Only a button click changes question."}],challenge:{title:"Start menu",short:"Add a title screen and a Start button before the quiz begins.",text:"Add a backdrop named <b>title</b> and a Start button sprite. On the green flag, show the title backdrop and the Start button. The quiz must not begin until Start is clicked. When it is clicked, the Start button hides, the backdrop changes, and the Bot begins the quiz. Answer buttons stay hidden until the quiz begins.",answer:"when green flag clicked\nshow\nswitch backdrop to (title v)\n\nwhen this sprite clicked\nhide\nswitch backdrop to (quiz v)\nbroadcast (start quiz v)"},checklist:["score and question reset on the green flag","At least 2 questions that check the answer","A right answer adds 1 to score, and a wrong answer gives feedback","A results message that shows the score","I tested right and wrong answers"],submit:'Share your quiz app. Name your project anything you like (idea: "Space Quiz"). Paste the link.',teacherNotes:"Pacing (45 min): warmup 3, plan 6, setup 5, question 1 10, question 2 6, results 8, test 5. Questions 3 to 5 are a stretch. Absent students start from the starter project.<br><b>Logic</b>: both variables are made and reset in step 2, so question is 1 before any answer is checked. Buttons option: the Bot sets right answer, shows the question, and waits until question changes; each button only compares its own letter with right answer, so buttons never need editing for new questions. Typed option: everything is on the Bot; question is a progress counter. Both options show the wrong-answer path in the else branch. For the challenge, the Bot's quiz script moves from when green flag clicked to when I receive start quiz.<br><b>Common misconceptions</b>: putting change question by 1 inside the if (wrong answers then freeze the quiz); forgetting to reset score; a missing space in join; buttons left visible on the results screen; with typed answers, = ignores capitals but not extra spaces.<br><b>Accommodations</b>: the core is 2 questions, so writing load stays small; the frame keeps each question to one line. Students may use the sample questions. Typed answers need more typing from players; buttons need painting. Let students choose. Students who need it can follow the typed option, where all code is on one sprite. English learners may write the quiz in their home language."},a3:{title:"List Master",tagline:"Store a whole collection of data and build a study tool with it.",mission:"Build a flashcard study app with lists.",minutes:"45",skill:"Lists & Data",goal:"By the end of this quest you will make lists, add and delete items, read items by their position, and build a flashcard study app.",vocab:[{term:"List",def:"A variable that holds many items in order, like a shopping list.",icon:"📋"},{term:"Item",def:"One thing stored in a list.",icon:"🔖"},{term:"Index",def:"The position number of an item in a list: 1, 2, 3, and so on.",icon:"🔢"},{term:"Length",def:"How many items are in a list.",icon:"📏"}],warmup:"Write down 5 things you need to study for a test: vocab words, math facts, capitals. How could a computer remember all of them? Do this alone or with a neighbor.",needs:["askAnswer","join"],project:{name:"Super App",part:"Part 1 of 2"},steps:[{title:"Start your Super App",short:"Make a new project and pick a study topic.",text:"Make a new project. Your Super App will have a flashcard study mode (this quest) and a sketch mode (next quest). Pick a sprite to be your <b>Host</b> (it asks the questions). Pick a study topic: vocab words, state capitals, Spanish words, times tables, or anything from class.",shots:["choose-sprite"],tip:"Name your project anything you like. One idea: Super App."},{title:"Make two lists",short:"Make a questions list and an answers list with five items each.",text:"Click <b>Variables</b>, then <b>Make a List</b>. Name it <code>questions</code> and choose <b>For all sprites</b>. Do the same for <code>answers</code>. Both lists appear on the Stage.<br>Click the <b>+</b> at the bottom of each list to type 5 items. Item 1 of questions goes with item 1 of answers. For example: questions item 1 is <i>Capital of France?</i> and answers item 1 is <i>Paris</i>.<br>Items you type into a list are saved with the project.",shots:["make-list"],tip:"Choose For all sprites. If you choose For this sprite only, your Add Card button will not be able to reach the lists later."},{title:"Read an item",short:"Use the item of block to show one flashcard.",text:"The <code>item (1) of [questions]</code> block reports the first item in the list. Build this script on the Host and click the green flag. The Host says the first question, then the first answer. Try changing the 1 to a 2.",where:"Host sprite",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nhide list [questions v]\nhide list [answers v]\nsay (item (1) of [questions v]) for (2) seconds\nsay (item (1) of [answers v]) for (2) seconds"},{title:"Loop through every card",short:"Use a card variable and a loop to quiz every card.",text:"Make a variable called <code>card</code>. It holds the index: which card we are on. The loop runs one time for each item, because it uses <code>length of [questions]</code>. Each time, the Host asks a question, checks the answer, then adds 1 to card to go to the next card.<br>Keep the hat and the two <b>hide list</b> blocks from step 3. Drag the two <b>say</b> blocks away and delete them. Add the new blocks under the hide list blocks.",where:"Host sprite",change:"replace",changeNote:"Changes your step 3 script: keep the green flag hat and both hide list blocks; delete the two say blocks; add the rest below.",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nhide list [questions v]\nhide list [answers v]\nset [card v] to (1)\nrepeat (length of [questions v])\nask (item (card) of [questions v]) and wait\nif <(answer) = (item (card) of [answers v])> then\nsay [Correct!] for (1) seconds\nelse\nsay (join [It was: ] (item (card) of [answers v])) for (2) seconds\nend\nchange [card v] by (1)\nend",shots:["make-variable"],tip:"A space at the end of a list item breaks the equals check. Paris with a space after it does NOT equal Paris. Click each item in the list and check for extra spaces."},{title:"Add new cards from the app",short:"Make an Add Card button so users can add their own cards.",text:"Paint a new sprite that looks like a button and says <b>Add Card</b>. When someone clicks it, it asks for a new question and its answer, and adds them to the END of each list. Now users can add cards without changing the code.",where:"Add Card button (new sprite)",change:"new",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when this sprite clicked\nask [Type a new question:] and wait\nadd (answer) to [questions v]\nask [Type its answer:] and wait\nadd (answer) to [answers v]\nsay [Card added!] for (1) seconds",tip:"The two add blocks must go to different lists: the question goes to questions, the answer goes to answers."},{title:"Missed-it list",short:"Save every missed question in a review list and show it at the end.",text:"Make a third list called <code>review</code> (For all sprites). Change the Host's script so that:<br>1. At the start, the review list is emptied and hidden.<br>2. When the user gets a card WRONG, that question is added to review.<br>3. After the last card, the review list is shown, so the user knows what to practice.<br>Look in <b>Variables</b> for the list blocks: <b>delete all of</b>, <b>add to</b>, <b>hide list</b>, and <b>show list</b>.",where:"Host sprite",change:"add",changeNote:"Adds to your step 4 script: delete all and hide list go under the hat; add to review goes in the ELSE part, under the say It was block; show list goes at the very end, after the repeat.",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nhide list [questions v]\nhide list [answers v]\ndelete all of [review v]\nhide list [review v]\nset [card v] to (1)\nrepeat (length of [questions v])\nask (item (card) of [questions v]) and wait\nif <(answer) = (item (card) of [answers v])> then\nsay [Correct!] for (1) seconds\nelse\nsay (join [It was: ] (item (card) of [answers v])) for (2) seconds\nadd (item (card) of [questions v]) to [review v]\nend\nchange [card v] by (1)\nend\nshow list [review v]",shots:["make-list"],tip:"Trace it: which part runs when the answer is wrong, the if part or the else part? That is where the add block goes."},{title:"Test and share",short:"Test every card, miss one on purpose, add a card, then share.",text:"Click the green flag. Answer every card. Get one WRONG on purpose. Does it show up in the review list at the end? Click Add Card and add a new card. Click the green flag again. Does the new card get asked? Save and share."}],quiz:[{q:"Trace this script. What will the sprite say?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\ndelete all of [pets v]\nadd [dog] to [pets v]\nadd [cat] to [pets v]\nadd [fish] to [pets v]\nsay (item (2) of [pets v])",choices:["dog","fish","cat","2"],answer:2,explain:"Scratch lists start counting at 1. Item 2 is the second item added: cat."},{q:"The questions list has 4 items. Trace this script. What number will the sprite say?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [card v] to (1)\nrepeat (length of [questions v])\nchange [card v] by (1)\nend\nsay (card)",choices:["4","5","1","0"],answer:1,explain:"card starts at 1 and the loop adds 1 four times: 1 + 4 = 5."}],challenge:{title:"Shuffle mode",short:"Ask five random cards instead of going in order.",text:"Make the Host ask 5 cards, but pick a RANDOM card each time instead of going 1, 2, 3. The pick random block in Operators gives a random number between two numbers. The random card must always be a real card, even after users add more cards.",answer:"when green flag clicked\nrepeat (5)\nset [card v] to (pick random (1) to (length of [questions v]))\nask (item (card) of [questions v]) and wait\nif <(answer) = (item (card) of [answers v])> then\nsay [Correct!] for (1) seconds\nelse\nsay (join [It was: ] (item (card) of [answers v])) for (2) seconds\nend\nend"},checklist:["My app has a questions list and an answers list that match","My Host asks every card with a loop and says if I am right","My Add Card button adds a new card to both lists","Missed questions go into a review list"],submit:"Share your Super App and paste the link. Name your project anything you like (idea: Super App). Next quest you will add a sketch mode!",teacherNotes:"Pacing: start 3 min, lists 7, item of 5, loop 10, Add Card 8, review 8, test 4. Students may come from any fork-1 path; ask/answer and join have primers. Misconceptions: (1) off-by-one: students think item 0 is first; model the index with a real numbered list on the board. (2) 'For this sprite only' lists cannot be reached by the Add Card button; check scope first when Add Card 'does nothing'. (3) Trailing spaces in list items make = false even for a correct answer (= ignores upper/lower case but not spaces). (4) In step 6 many put 'add to review' in the if part; ask them to trace a wrong answer. (5) Students forget delete all, so review grows every run. Accommodations: students who find two matching lists hard may use 5 cards and the step 4 script as a full copy; the review list (step 6) can be done with the 'Stuck? Show me' solution. ELLs may write cards in their home language and English (a great study tool). Students with motor needs can type list items instead of dragging."},a4:{title:"Drawing Tools",tagline:"Turn your app into a creative studio with a sketch mode.",mission:"Add a sketch mode and mode buttons to your Super App.",minutes:"45",skill:"Pen Tools & App Modes",goal:"By the end of this quest you will add mode buttons to your Super App, make your flashcards start from the Study button, and build a sketch mode with a brush and color tools.",vocab:[{term:"Mode",def:"One part of an app, like study mode or sketch mode.",icon:"🔀"},{term:"Tool",def:"A button that changes what the user can do, like picking a color.",icon:"🧰"},{term:"Pen",def:"Blocks that let a sprite draw lines on the Stage as it moves.",icon:"🖊️"},{term:"Broadcast",def:"A message one sprite sends so other sprites know to start.",icon:"📣"}],warmup:"What tools does every drawing app have? List as many as you can in 1 minute. Circle the 3 most important. Do this alone or with a neighbor.",needs:["askAnswer","join"],starter:{name:"Super App (from List Master)",contains:"A Host sprite with the green flag flashcard script (card variable, questions and answers lists For all sprites with 5 cards each, review list), and an Add Card button sprite."},project:{name:"Super App",part:"Part 2 of 2"},steps:[{title:"Get ready",short:"Open your Super App, add the Pen, and add three new sprites.",text:"Open your Super App from the last quest. Click the <b>Add Extension</b> button at the bottom-left and choose <b>Pen</b>.<br>Paint three new sprites:<br>1. <b>Study</b> button (a small rectangle with the word Study).<br>2. <b>Sketch</b> button (the same, with the word Sketch).<br>3. <b>Brush</b> (a small dot).<br>Put the two mode buttons in a strip along the top of the Stage. Make sure the backdrop is white, so the eraser will match later.",shots:["extensions","choose-sprite"],tip:"Missed the last quest? Ask your teacher for the Super App starter project and begin here."},{title:"Mode buttons",short:"Make the Study button send study mode when it is clicked.",text:"Each mode button sends a message. The sprites that belong to that mode will listen for it. Build this on the Study button. Use <b>New message</b> and type <code>study mode</code>.<br>Then build the same script on the Sketch button, but choose <b>New message</b> and type <code>sketch mode</code>.",where:"Study button (then the Sketch button)",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when this sprite clicked\nbroadcast (study mode v)",tip:"Message names must match exactly. A typo makes a different message, and nothing will listen to it."},{title:"Flashcards start from Study",short:"Change your flashcard script so it starts when study mode arrives.",text:"Right now your flashcards start on the green flag. Now they should start when someone clicks Study.<br>1. On the Host, drag the <code>when green flag clicked</code> hat off the top of your flashcard script. Put <code>when I receive [study mode]</code> on top instead, and add <code>show</code> under it. Everything else stays the same.<br>2. Add a short green flag script so the Host hides until a mode is picked.<br>3. Add a sketch mode script. <code>stop [other scripts in sprite]</code> stops the flashcard loop, so the Host does not keep asking while you draw.",where:"Host sprite",change:"replace",changeNote:"Changes your List Master flashcard script: only the hat changes, plus one show block. The two short scripts at the bottom are new.",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when I receive [study mode v]\nshow\nhide list [questions v]\nhide list [answers v]\ndelete all of [review v]\nhide list [review v]\nset [card v] to (1)\nrepeat (length of [questions v])\nask (item (card) of [questions v]) and wait\nif <(answer) = (item (card) of [answers v])> then\nsay [Correct!] for (1) seconds\nelse\nsay (join [It was: ] (item (card) of [answers v])) for (2) seconds\nadd (item (card) of [questions v]) to [review v]\nend\nchange [card v] by (1)\nend\nshow list [review v]\n\nwhen green flag clicked\nhide\n\nwhen I receive [sketch mode v]\nstop [other scripts in sprite v]\nhide",tip:"If you forget to remove the green flag hat, the flashcards start right away, before anyone clicks Study."},{title:"Draw with the mouse",short:"Make the brush follow the mouse and draw while the mouse is down.",text:"When sketch mode starts, the Brush shows and follows the mouse forever. If the mouse button is down, the pen is down. If not, the pen is up. Put the blocks in order on the Brush.",where:"Brush",change:"new",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when I receive [sketch mode v]\nshow\nforever\ngo to (mouse-pointer v)\nif <mouse down?> then\npen down\nelse\npen up\nend\nend",tip:"Pen lines are drawn behind all sprites, so your buttons always stay on top of the drawing."},{title:"Stop drawing in study mode",short:"Make the brush stop, lift the pen, and hide when study mode starts.",text:"Click Sketch, then Study. The brush still follows the mouse and draws! Fix it, using what you did on the Host in step 3.<br>Make TWO scripts on the Brush:<br>1. When <b>study mode</b> arrives: stop the drawing loop, lift the pen, and hide.<br>2. When the <b>green flag</b> is clicked: lift the pen, hide, clear the drawing, and set the pen color and size to a starting value.<br>Helpful blocks are in <b>Events</b>, <b>Control</b>, <b>Looks</b>, and <b>Pen</b>.",where:"Brush",change:"new",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"when I receive [study mode v]\nstop [other scripts in sprite v]\npen up\nhide\n\nwhen green flag clicked\npen up\nhide\nerase all\nset pen color to [#000000]\nset pen size to (3)",tip:"Test it: click Sketch, draw, click Study, move the mouse. Nothing should draw now."},{title:"Color buttons",short:"Make color buttons that send a message to change the brush color.",text:"Only the Brush can change its own pen color, so each color button sends a message to the Brush.<br>Paint a small red square sprite called <b>Red</b>. Its script sends <code>pick red</code>. On the Brush, add a script that sets the pen color when <code>pick red</code> arrives. Click the color circle in the set pen color block to choose red.<br>Then duplicate the Red button to make more colors. Change the message and the color for each one.",where:"Red button, then the Brush",change:"new",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when this sprite clicked\nbroadcast (pick red v)\n\nwhen I receive [pick red v]\nset pen color to [#ff0000]",shots:["choose-sprite"],tip:"The first script goes on the Red button. The when I receive script goes on the Brush."},{title:"Every sprite in its mode",short:"Show each button only in its own mode, then test switching modes.",text:"Right now the color buttons show in study mode, and the Add Card button shows while you draw. Fix it:<br>1. Each color button shows on <b>sketch mode</b> and hides on <b>study mode</b> and on the <b>green flag</b>.<br>2. The Add Card button shows on <b>study mode</b> and hides on <b>sketch mode</b> and on the <b>green flag</b>.<br>Then test: click the green flag, then switch between Study and Sketch three times. Only the right sprites should show each time.",where:"Each color button (and the Add Card button)",change:"new",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nhide\n\nwhen I receive [sketch mode v]\nshow\n\nwhen I receive [study mode v]\nhide",tip:"Make the three scripts on the Red button first. Then drag each script onto another color button to copy it there."},{title:"Eraser, Clear, and brush size",short:"Add an eraser, a clear button, and keys for brush size.",stretch:!0,text:"An eraser is a pen with the same color as the background. Add an <b>Eraser</b> button that sends <code>eraser</code> and a <b>Clear</b> button that sends <code>clear</code>. On the Brush: eraser makes the pen white and big; clear erases everything. Use the up and down arrow keys to make the brush bigger and smaller.",where:"Brush",change:"new",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"when I receive [eraser v]\nset pen color to [#ffffff]\nset pen size to (20)\n\nwhen I receive [clear v]\nerase all\n\nwhen [up arrow v] key pressed\nchange pen size by (2)\n\nwhen [down arrow v] key pressed\nchange pen size by (-2)",tip:"Eraser leaves white marks? Your backdrop is not white. Use a white backdrop for sketch mode."}],quiz:[{q:"Someone clicks Sketch, holds the mouse down, and moves it around. Trace this Brush script. What happens?",blocks:"when I receive [sketch mode v]\nshow\ngo to (mouse-pointer v)\nif <mouse down?> then\npen down\nend",choices:["The brush draws lines everywhere the mouse goes","The brush jumps to the mouse one time and then stops following it","The brush draws only red lines","Nothing shows at all"],answer:1,explain:"There is no forever loop, so go to and the if run only once."},{q:"Why does the Host use stop other scripts in sprite when sketch mode arrives?",choices:["To erase the drawing","To change the pen color","To delete the lists","So the flashcard loop stops asking questions while you draw"],answer:3,explain:"The flashcard repeat loop is still running. Stopping it ends the questions until Study is clicked again."}],challenge:{title:"Stamp tool",short:"Add a Stamp button that stamps a star where you click.",text:"Add a Stamp button. After it is clicked, each mouse click puts ONE star picture on the drawing where the mouse is. Holding the mouse down must not make a long line of stars. Clicking Sketch again goes back to normal drawing. Hint: the Pen extension has a stamp block, and the Brush can have a star costume.",answer:"when this sprite clicked\nbroadcast (stamp mode v)\n\nwhen I receive [stamp mode v]\nstop [other scripts in sprite v]\npen up\nswitch costume to (star v)\nforever\ngo to (mouse-pointer v)\nif <mouse down?> then\nstamp\nwait until <not <mouse down?>>\nend\nend"},checklist:["My app has a Study button and a Sketch button","My flashcards start when I click Study, not on the green flag","In sketch mode I can draw with the mouse and pick a color","Each sprite shows only in its own mode"],submit:"Draw a picture in sketch mode, then share your finished Super App and paste the link. Name your project anything you like (idea: Super App).",teacherNotes:"Pacing: get ready 6 min, mode buttons 4, flashcards from Study 7, draw 6, stop drawing goal 7, color buttons 7, modes goal 8. Eraser/Clear/size is a stretch. Have the Super App starter (end of a3) ready for absent students. Misconceptions: (1) students leave the green flag hat on the flashcard script, so study mode starts at once; only the hat changes. (2) A hidden sprite's forever loop keeps running; hide does not stop scripts. That is why both the Host and the Brush use stop other scripts in sprite. (3) Students try to set pen color on the color button itself; pen settings belong to the sprite that draws. (4) Message typos create a second message. If an ask box is still on screen after switching to sketch mode, clicking the green flag resets it. Accommodations: step 7 can be done for just ONE color button plus Add Card; students who need fewer steps can use only the Red button. The 'Stuck? Show me' solutions are fine to use on goal steps for students with IEP reading or writing goals. Students with motor needs can draw with arrow keys instead of the mouse (change the brush to move with key presses)."},f1:{title:"Playtest & Polish",tagline:"Real creators test with real users. Get feedback and level up your project.",mission:"Test a project, get feedback, and polish your own project.",minutes:"45",skill:"Feedback & Iteration",goal:"By the end of this quest you will playtest a project, give kind and useful feedback, make a fix list, and polish your own project with a title screen, instructions, and credits.",vocab:[{term:"Playtest",def:"Someone uses your project while you watch and learn.",icon:"🎮"},{term:"Glow",def:"Feedback about something that works really well.",icon:"🌟"},{term:"Grow",def:"Kind, specific feedback about something that could be better.",icon:"🌱"},{term:"Iterate",def:"Make a change, test it, and repeat.",icon:"🔁"},{term:"Credits",def:"Saying who made the art, sounds, or ideas you used.",icon:"🙏"}],warmup:'Read these two comments: "This is bad." and "The jump was hard to time. Maybe make it a little higher?" Which one helps the creator more? Why? Think alone or talk with a neighbor.',needs:[],starter:{name:"Polish Starter",contains:"A finished catch game from the teacher: a Cat player that moves with the arrow keys, an Apple that falls from the top, a score variable, and green flag scripts that reset everything (score to 0, positions, costumes). It has NO title screen, NO instructions, and one small bug (the Apple sometimes starts too low). Students who missed a project can polish this one."},steps:[{title:"Pick your project",short:"Pick your best project. Make sure it is saved and shared.",text:"Choose your best project so far (from either branch project). Open it, click the green flag, and play it once. Then click <b>Share</b> so other people can open it. If you do not have a finished project, open the teacher's <b>Polish Starter</b> instead.",tip:"Pick a project that works from the green flag. You will polish it today, not start over."},{title:"Silent playtest",short:"Watch someone use your project. Do not talk or help.",text:"Swap seats with a partner who is also ready. They use your project for 5 minutes <b>without you explaining anything</b>. You watch quietly and take notes:<br>• Where did they get stuck or confused?<br>• What made them smile?<br>Then switch: you test their project in silence.",tip:"Creators: NO helping! If the tester is stuck, that is useful information. Write it down.",solo:"No partner free? Pick one: <b>1.</b> Ask your teacher to try it while you watch. <b>2.</b> Record a short screen video of yourself playing, then watch it. <b>3.</b> Test it yourself as if you have never seen it: click the full screen button, keep your eyes on the Stage only, and answer these: Do I know what to do in the first 5 seconds? Do I know which keys to press? Do I know how to win or finish? Does it start the same way every time I click the green flag? Write down every time you feel confused.",frame:"Playtest notes\nThe tester got stuck when ...\nThe tester smiled when ...\nThe tester did not know ..."},{title:"Glow & Grow",short:"Write two Glows and one Grow. Say what and why.",text:"Testers write 2 <b>Glows</b> (things that work well) and 1 <b>Grow</b> (one thing that could be better) for the creator. Be specific: say <b>what</b> happened and <b>why</b> it matters. A Grow is kind: it gives an idea, not just a problem. Give your notes to the creator.",tip:'"It\'s good" does not help. "I liked how the score went up with a sound" helps a lot.',solo:"If you tested alone or your teacher tested it, write the Glows and the Grow for your own project, using your playtest notes. You can also ask your teacher for one Grow.",frame:"Glow 1: I really liked how ... because ...\nGlow 2: It was fun when ... because ...\nGrow: I was confused when ... Maybe you could ..."},{title:"Make a fix list",short:"Write three fixes in order, biggest problem first.",text:"Read your feedback and your notes. Write a fix list of 3 things, in this order:<br><b>1. Biggest problem</b>: something broken or very confusing.<br><b>2. Easier to understand</b>: something that helps a new user.<br><b>3. Cooler</b>: something that makes it more fun.<br>Fix number 1 first.",tip:'Keep each fix small. "Add 5 new levels" is too big for today. "Make the jump higher" is just right.',frame:"My fix list\n1. Biggest problem: ... I will fix it by ...\n2. Easier to understand: ... I will fix it by ...\n3. Cooler: ... I will fix it by ..."},{title:"Make a Title sprite",short:"Paint a Title sprite that shows at start and hides when space is pressed.",text:'A polished project starts with a title. We will use a simple, safe way: a <b>Title</b> sprite that covers the Stage.<br>1. Hover over Choose a Sprite and click <b>Paint</b>. Name the sprite <b>Title</b>.<br>2. In the Costumes tab, draw a big rectangle that covers the Stage. Use the Text tool to write your project\'s name and "Press SPACE to start".<br>3. Add this script to the Title sprite.<br>You do NOT change any of your old green flag scripts. They still reset everything like before.',where:"Title sprite",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (0) y: (0)\ngo to [front v] layer\nshow\nwait until <key (space v) pressed?>\nhide",shots:["choose-sprite","costumes-tab"],tip:"If your project already uses the space key (for example, to jump), pick a different key like s. Use the same key here and in the next step."},{title:"Wait for the start key",short:"Make your main scripts wait until the start key is pressed.",text:"Right now your game might start moving behind the title. Fix that: in each green flag script that <b>moves things or starts a timer</b>, keep the reset blocks at the top (set score to 0, go to x and y, switch costume). Right <b>after</b> the reset blocks, before the forever or repeat loop, add a block that waits until the start key is pressed. Use the same key as your Title sprite. Look in the Control category.",where:"Your main sprite (and any other sprite or the Stage with a moving green flag script)",change:"add",changeNote:"In each green flag script that moves things: add one block after the reset blocks, just above the forever or repeat loop.",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [score v] to (0)\ngo to x: (0) y: (-150)\nwait until <key (space v) pressed?>\nforever\nif <key (right arrow v) pressed?> then\nchange x by (10)\nend\nif <key (left arrow v) pressed?> then\nchange x by (-10)\nend\nend",tip:"Put the wait AFTER the reset blocks, not before. Then everything resets on the green flag even while the title is showing. Story or app? Add it before your first say or scene block."},{title:"Instructions and credits",short:"Write instructions and credits on your project page.",text:"Click <b>See Project Page</b>. Fill in two boxes:<br>• <b>Instructions</b>: how to play or use it, and which keys or buttons to press.<br>• <b>Notes and Credits</b>: who made it, who tested it, and credit for any art, sounds, music, or ideas you did not make yourself. If you remixed a project or followed a tutorial, say so.<br>Giving credit is honest and kind. It is a rule of the Scratch community.",tip:"Use your first name only. Never put your last name, school, or address online.",frame:"Instructions:\nYour goal is to ...\nPress ... to ...\nYou win (or finish) when ...\n\nNotes and Credits:\nMade by (first name) ...\nTested by ...\nThe ... sound is from the Scratch library.\nThe idea for ... came from ..."},{title:"Fix, retest, share",short:"Fix your list, test again, then save and share.",text:"Fix as many items on your fix list as you can, starting with number 1. After each fix, click the green flag and test. Then ask your tester to try the new version. Save and share.",tip:"Test from the green flag after every fix. One fix can sometimes break something else.",solo:'If your tester is busy, retest it yourself with the same "never seen it" checklist from the Silent playtest step, or ask your teacher for a quick look.'}],quiz:[{q:"You click the green flag but do NOT press any key. What do you see?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (0) y: (0)\ngo to [front v] layer\nshow\nwait until <key (space v) pressed?>\nhide",choices:["The Title sprite shows and hides right away","The Title sprite stays on the Stage until space is pressed","Nothing shows at all","The Title sprite hides and never comes back"],answer:1,explain:"The script stops at wait until. It only goes on to hide when space is pressed."},{q:"Which is the most helpful Grow feedback?",choices:["It's boring.","Make it better.","I did not know which key jumps. Maybe add it to the title screen.","I like it."],answer:2,explain:"Helpful feedback is specific and kind, and it gives an idea to make the project better."}],challenge:{title:"Fancy exit",short:"Make the title glide up off the Stage when the start key is pressed.",text:"Change your Title sprite so that when the start key is pressed, the title glides up and off the top of the Stage in 1 second, then hides. It must come back to the middle and show again every time you click the green flag.",answer:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (0) y: (0)\ngo to [front v] layer\nshow\nwait until <key (space v) pressed?>\nglide (1) secs to x: (0) y: (360)\nhide"},checklist:["My project has a fix list with 3 fixes, and fix number 1 is done.","My project has a Title sprite that hides when the start key is pressed.","My project still resets correctly every time I click the green flag.","My project page has Instructions and Notes and Credits filled in."],submit:'Save and share your polished project and paste the link. Put your fix list in Notes and Credits. Name your project anything you like (idea: add "Polished" to the old name).',teacherNotes:'PACING: warm-up 3 min, pick project 3, silent playtest 10 (5 each way), Glow and Grow 5, fix list 4, Title sprite 6, wait step 5, instructions 4, fix and retest in the time left. Model a silent playtest and Glow and Grow with a teacher project first (2 min). Pair students across branch types when possible; fresh eyes find more. Because students are self-paced, keep a "ready to playtest" spot on the board: students write their name there and pair with the next name. If nobody is there, they use the solo checklist, a screen video, or you.\nWHY THIS TITLE SCREEN: we do NOT ask students to re-wire every green flag script to a new broadcast. That refactor often breaks resets. The Title sprite plus one wait until block after the resets is safe: resets still run on the green flag. Projects that already use space can use another key.\nMISCONCEPTIONS: (1) Putting wait until above the reset blocks, so old positions show behind the title; it still works but resets late. (2) Forgetting the Title sprite needs go to front layer, so it hides behind other sprites. (3) Stories with "when this sprite clicked" scripts can still be clicked under the title; that is fine. (4) Grows that are only complaints; point to the frame.\nACCOMMODATIONS: all writing has sentence frames; students may dictate feedback or fix lists to you or use speech-to-text. The silent playtest can be done as a teacher test or a screen video for students who find partner work stressful. Students with fine motor needs can type the title with the Text tool on a plain colored rectangle; no drawing needed.'},f2:{title:"Capstone Build",tagline:"Your biggest quest yet: plan and build a project that is all yours.",mission:"Break a big idea into small features, then build them one at a time.",minutes:"45",skill:"Decomposition, Planning & Building",goal:"By the end of this quest you will break a project idea into features, match each feature to the blocks it needs, plan a small first version (MVP) with 3 must-have features, and build and test it one feature at a time.",vocab:[{term:"Decompose",def:"Break a big problem into small parts you can build one by one.",icon:"🧩"},{term:"Feature",def:"One thing your project can do, like jump or keep score.",icon:"⭐"},{term:"MVP",def:"The smallest version that works. Build this first, add extras later.",icon:"🛠️"},{term:"Capstone",def:"A final project that shows what you learned.",icon:"🏆"},{term:"Build loop",def:"Build one feature, test it, check it off, then start the next.",icon:"🔄"}],warmup:"Look back at your projects. Which 3 skills were your favorite (for example loops, variables, broadcast, custom blocks, Pen)? Which one do you want to use in a new way? Write them down or tell a neighbor.",needs:[],starter:{name:"Capstone Skeleton",contains:'For option C or absent students. The teacher\'s skeleton has: a Player sprite with a green flag script that goes to a start spot and moves with the arrow keys; an empty Title sprite with the safe title script from Playtest & Polish (show, wait until space is pressed, hide); a score variable and a time variable (For all sprites) set to 0 on the green flag; two backdrops named level 1 and level 2; and empty "when I receive game over" and "when I receive next level" scripts on the Stage. It uses only trunk skills, so it works for every path.'},steps:[{title:"Break down a game together",short:"Break a flying game into features and the blocks each one needs.",text:"Before you plan your own project, we practice on one together. Imagine a game called <b>Flappy Bat</b>:<br><i>A bat is in the middle of the Stage. It falls down all the time. When you press space, it flaps up. Walls with a gap slide in from the right. If the bat flies through a gap, you get 1 point. If the bat touches a wall or the ground, the game ends.</i><br>Big problems are hard. Small parts are easy. Split the game into <b>features</b>. For each feature, ask: <b>What does the player see?</b> and <b>Which blocks or skills does it need?</b> The first two rows are done for you. Fill in the rest with your teacher or by yourself.",tip:"One feature = one small job. If a row needs more than about 6 blocks, split it into two rows.",frame:"Feature | What the player sees | Blocks or skills it needs\n1. Bat falls | The bat drops down slowly | forever, change y by -3 (Motion, Control)\n2. Flap | Press space, the bat jumps up | when space key pressed, change y by 40\n3. Walls move | ... | ...\n4. Score | ... | ...\n5. Game over | ... | ..."},{title:"Choose your path",short:"Pick one path: level up, mash up, or start fresh.",text:"Pick ONE option:<br><b>A. Level up</b>: open one of your branch projects and add a big new feature or mode (a new level, a timer, a hard mode, a new ending).<br><b>B. Mash-up</b>: combine two kinds of projects (a story with a mini game inside, a quiz that unlocks a platformer level, a drawing app with a score).<br><b>C. Start fresh</b>: open the teacher's <b>Capstone Skeleton</b>. It already has a Player that moves with the arrow keys, a Title sprite, score and time variables, two level backdrops, and empty game over and next level messages. You add your own idea on top.",tip:"For A or B, use File then Save as a copy first, so your old project stays safe."},{title:"Break down your idea",short:"Break your own idea into features, just like Flappy Bat.",text:"Now do the same thing for <b>your</b> project. Write one sentence about it. Then list 4 to 6 features. For each one, write what the user sees and which blocks or skills it needs. Use only skills you know. If you are not sure how to build a feature, write a question mark. You will ask about it in the size check.",tip:'A story or app has features too: "Scene 2 starts when the cat finishes talking" (broadcast) or "Button shows the next card" (when this sprite clicked, change a variable).',frame:"My idea in one sentence: A ... where ...\n\nFeature | What the user sees | Blocks or skills it needs\n1. ... | ... | ...\n2. ... | ... | ...\n3. ... | ... | ...\n4. ... | ... | ...\n5. (extra) ... | ... | ..."},{title:"Plan your MVP",short:"Choose your three must have features. Everything else is an extra.",text:"Look at your feature table. Circle the <b>3 must-have features</b>: without them, the project does not work. That is your <b>MVP</b>. Put the rest on the Extras list. You build extras only after all 3 must-haves work.",tip:'Good MVP features are small: "Player moves", "Score goes up", "Game ends". "10 levels with a boss" is an extra.',frame:"Title: ...\nPitch: A ... where ...\nPath: A (level up) / B (mash-up) / C (start fresh)\nSprites I need: ...\n\nMust-have 1: ...\nMust-have 2: ...\nMust-have 3: ...\n\nExtras (only if I have time):\n- ...\n- ..."},{title:"Size check",short:"Check that each must have feature fits in about ten minutes.",text:"Show your plan to your teacher. Together, check: Can you build each must-have in about 10 minutes? Do you know which blocks each one needs? If a feature is too big, split it or move it to Extras.",tip:"Making your plan smaller is smart, not giving up. A small project that works is better than a big one that does not.",solo:"If your teacher is busy, check it yourself. For each must-have, answer: Do I know at least 2 blocks it needs? Is it 8 blocks or fewer? If you answer no, make it smaller or move it to Extras. Then show your teacher later."},{title:"Build loop: feature 1",short:"Build must have one, test it from the green flag, then check it off.",text:'Work in a <b>build loop</b>: <b>build</b> one feature, <b>test</b> it from the green flag, <b>check it off</b> on your plan. Then start the next one. Build must-have 1 now, using the blocks you listed in your table. Many projects need a <b>score</b> or a <b>timer</b>. If yours does and you get stuck, open "Stuck? Show me" for an example.',where:"The sprite or Stage named in your plan for this feature",change:"new",scaffold:"goal",options:[{label:"Score",short:"The score starts at zero and goes up when the player touches the goal.",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [score v] to (0)\nshow variable [score v]\nforever\nif <touching (Goal v)?> then\nchange [score v] by (1)\nwait (0.5) seconds\nend\nend"},{label:"Timer",short:"The time counts down from thirty, then the game over message is sent.",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [time v] to (30)\nshow variable [time v]\nrepeat (30)\nwait (1) seconds\nchange [time v] by (-1)\nend\nbroadcast (game over v)"}],tip:"Make the variable first with Make a Variable (For all sprites). Reuse scripts from old projects: drag them into the Backpack at the bottom of the screen, then drag them out here.",shots:["make-variable"]},{title:"Build loop: features 2 and 3",short:"Build your next two must haves. Test and check off each one.",text:'Keep looping: build must-have 2, test, check it off. Then must-have 3, test, check it off. Many projects need a <b>title screen</b> or a <b>level or scene switch</b>. If yours does and you get stuck, open "Stuck? Show me". For the level switch, also add a block in your Player script that sends the next level message when the player touches the door.',where:"The sprite or Stage named in your plan for this feature",change:"new",scaffold:"goal",options:[{label:"Title screen",short:"A Title sprite shows at the start and hides when space is pressed.",blocks:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (0) y: (0)\ngo to [front v] layer\nshow\nwait until <key (space v) pressed?>\nhide"},{label:"Level or scene switch",short:"The Stage starts on level one and goes to the next backdrop on a message.",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [level v] to (1)\nswitch backdrop to (level 1 v)\n\nwhen I receive [next level v]\nchange [level v] by (1)\nnext backdrop"}],tip:"Stuck for more than 5 minutes? Write your question on your plan, move on to a different feature, and ask your teacher. Do not stay stuck."},{title:"Checkpoint and share",short:"Play from the green flag, update your plan, then save and share.",text:"Click the green flag and play your whole project. Is every must-have checked off? Does it reset every time? If your MVP works, pick one feature from your Extras list and do one more build loop. Then save and share. In <b>Notes and Credits</b>, write which features are done and which you will finish next time.",tip:"If something broke, use your Bug Hunter skills: read the script slowly and click one block at a time to see what it does.",frame:"Done: ...\nNot done yet: ...\nNext time I will ..."}],quiz:[{q:"You click the green flag. What is the value of time after 3 seconds?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [time v] to (30)\nrepeat (30)\nwait (1) seconds\nchange [time v] by (-1)\nend\nbroadcast (game over v)",choices:["27","3","30","33"],answer:0,explain:"Time starts at 30. Each second it goes down by 1. After 3 seconds it is 27."},{q:'Which is the best way to break down "a quiz game with a timer"?',choices:["Build it all at once and test at the end","Start with the coolest sound effect","Make a bigger plan with 10 levels","Ask a question, check the answer, keep score, count down the time"],answer:3,explain:"Small features with clear jobs are easy to build and test one at a time."}],challenge:{title:"One reset block",short:"Make a custom block that resets your whole game, and use it.",text:"Make a custom block named <b>reset game</b> on your main sprite. It should set the score to 0, set the time to 30, and move the sprite to its start spot. Then use it as the first block in your green flag script, instead of the separate reset blocks.",answer:"define reset game\nset [score v] to (0)\nset [time v] to (30)\ngo to x: (-200) y: (0)\n\nwhen green flag clicked\nreset game"},checklist:["My project has a feature table that matches features to blocks.","My project has a plan with 3 must-have features.","My project has at least one must-have feature that works and is checked off.","My project resets when I click the green flag."],submit:'Save and share your capstone and paste the link. In Notes and Credits, type your one-sentence pitch and your done and not done list. Name your project anything you like (idea: "Capstone" plus your title).',teacherNotes:'PACING: warm-up 3 min, Flappy Bat breakdown 7 (do it together on the board as a "we do"; students fill rows 3 to 5), choose path 3, own breakdown 6, MVP plan 4, size check 3, build loops 17, checkpoint 2. If you can, give a second build session before Showcase Day.\nWORKED ANSWER for Flappy Bat rows: 3. Walls move: walls start at the right and glide or move left (go to x, change x in a forever loop; clones if they know them). 4. Score: score goes up when the wall passes the bat (variable, change score by 1). 5. Game over: touching wall or ground stops the game (if touching, broadcast game over or stop all).\nSCOPE: approve plans fast and push students to shrink them. Students who finish early do Extras, not a new project. The build loop step options only show common features; students build the feature in their own plan.\nALL PATHS: the plan uses only trunk skills (variables, broadcast, if, wait until). Students who know clones, lists, ask, or Pen may use them, but nobody needs them.\nMISCONCEPTIONS: (1) Building everything before testing anything. (2) The MVP being the "cool" feature instead of the core one. (3) Making a variable "For this sprite only" and then using it from the Stage. (4) Copying a Show me script without changing sprite names like Goal or Door to their own sprite names.\nACCOMMODATIONS: feature tables can be drawn, typed, or dictated; students may use 3 features instead of 4 to 6 in the breakdown. Offer path C (the skeleton) to anyone who feels stuck at a blank page. Students with IEP extended time may count only 2 must-haves as their MVP for today.'},f3:{title:"Showcase Day",tagline:"The final quest: show what you built, and prove what you can do.",mission:"Finish your capstone, share it, reflect, and solve one mystery mission.",minutes:"45",skill:"Presenting, Reflecting & Transfer",goal:"By the end of this quest you will finish and share your capstone, pitch it in one minute, give kind and specific comments, reflect on how you grew, and build a brand-new mini project on your own.",vocab:[{term:"Showcase",def:"An event where creators share their finished work.",icon:"🎪"},{term:"Pitch",def:"A short talk that tells people what your project is and why it is cool.",icon:"🎤"},{term:"Gallery walk",def:"Everyone tries projects, like visiting a museum.",icon:"🖼️"},{term:"Netiquette",def:"Being kind, safe, and respectful when you post online.",icon:"💬"},{term:"Reflection",def:"Thinking about what you learned and how you grew.",icon:"🪞"}],warmup:"Think back to your first quest, when you made your first sprite move. What can you do now that you could not do then? Write down three things, alone or with a neighbor.",needs:[],starter:{name:"Capstone Skeleton",contains:"For absent students: the same skeleton as Capstone Build (a Player that moves with the arrow keys, a Title sprite, score and time variables, two level backdrops). A student who missed Capstone Build adds one feature to it and showcases that."},steps:[{title:"Final fixes",short:"Fix last bugs and play your capstone from start to finish.",text:"Open your capstone. You have about 8 minutes. Fix the biggest bug first. Then play it start to finish. Click the green flag 2 times in a row: does it start the same way both times? Do not add new features today. Finish what you have.",tip:"If a feature still does not work, hide it or remove it. A small project that works is better than a big one that breaks."},{title:"Project page",short:"Write instructions and credits on your project page.",text:"Click <b>See Project Page</b> and fill in:<br>• <b>Instructions</b>: how to play or use it, which keys or buttons.<br>• <b>Notes and Credits</b>: your first name, the skills you used, who tested it, and credit for any art, sounds, or ideas you did not make.<br>• <b>Thumbnail</b>: the picture on the Stage when you save becomes the thumbnail, so save on a cool screen.<br>Then click <b>Share</b> and add the project to the class studio your teacher made.",tip:"First name only. Never post your last name, school, or address.",frame:"Instructions:\nYour goal is to ...\nPress ... to ...\n\nNotes and Credits:\nMade by ...\nSkills I used: ...\nTested by ...\nThe ... is from ..."},{title:"Plan your pitch",short:"Plan a one minute pitch about your project.",text:"Get ready to present. A pitch is short: about <b>1 minute</b>. Fill in the frame. Pick one script to show, the one you are most proud of. Practice once, quietly, with a timer.",tip:"One minute is about 4 or 5 sentences. You do not need to explain every block.",frame:"My project is called ... It is a ... where ...\nTo use it, you ...\nThe coolest part of my code is ... (show the script)\nOne problem I had was ... I fixed it by ..."},{title:"Present",short:"Present your pitch and show your favorite script.",text:"Present your 1-minute pitch to your small group or the class. Show your project on the Stage and your favorite script. Listeners: look at the speaker, and save questions for the end.<br>Feeling nervous? You can choose another way. Any way counts the same.",tip:"Read from your frame if you want to. That is allowed.",solo:"If talking to a group feels too hard, pick one: <b>1.</b> Present only to your teacher. <b>2.</b> Record a short screen video of your project while you read your pitch, and share it with your teacher. <b>3.</b> Type your pitch into Notes and Credits so people can read it."},{title:"Gallery walk",short:"Try four projects and leave a kind, specific comment on each.",text:"Put your project in full screen. Walk around (or open the class studio) and try at least <b>4 classmates' projects</b>. Try different kinds: a game, a story, and an app. For each one, leave a comment in the <b>Scratch class studio</b> that is <b>kind</b> and <b>specific</b>.<br><b>Netiquette rules:</b> say what you liked and why; give ideas, not insults; never post personal information; if you see a mean comment, tell your teacher.",tip:'"Cool" is kind but not specific. "The score sound made me want to play again" is kind AND specific.',solo:"If the room is quiet or nobody is at their seat, open the class studio on your own computer and try 4 projects there. You can also try projects from another class studio your teacher shares.",frame:"I liked how ... because ...\nMy favorite part was ...\nOne idea: maybe you could ..."},{title:"Reflect",short:"Write three sentences about how you grew as a coder.",text:"Write a short reflection with 3 sentences. Think about the whole course, not just today. Paste it into Notes and Credits or turn it in to your teacher.",frame:"I used to ... (something that was hard or new for you)\nNow I can ... (a skill you are proud of)\nNext I want to ... (something you want to learn or build)"},{title:"Mystery Mission",short:"Build a brand new mini project alone in ten minutes.",text:'This is your exit ticket. It is a new problem you have never seen. Work alone, with no help and no Show me. You have <b>10 minutes</b>.<br><b>The mission:</b> Start a new project. Make a sprite that counts how many times it is clicked. The count must start at 0 every time you click the green flag, and you must be able to see the count on the Stage. When the count reaches 10, the sprite says a message, like "You did it!".<br><b>First, decompose it</b>: what are the parts? (Something to remember the count. Something that happens on a click. Something that checks for 10.) Then build each part and test it.',where:"Any sprite in a new project",change:"new",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [clicks v] to (0)\nshow variable [clicks v]\n\nwhen this sprite clicked\nchange [clicks v] by (1)\nif <(clicks) = (10)> then\nsay [You did it!] for (2) seconds\nend",tip:"Look in the Variables, Events, Control, and Operators categories. Everything you need, you have used before.",frame:"Parts of the mission:\n1. Remember the count: ...\n2. When clicked: ...\n3. Check for 10: ..."}],quiz:[{q:"You click the green flag, then click the sprite again and again. On which click does it say Wow?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [clicks v] to (0)\n\nwhen this sprite clicked\nchange [clicks v] by (2)\nif <(clicks) > (5)> then\nsay [Wow!]\nend",choices:["The 2nd click","The 3rd click","The 5th click","The 6th click"],answer:1,explain:"Each click adds 2: then 2, 4, 6. Six is more than 5, so it says Wow on the 3rd click."},{q:"Which gallery comment follows netiquette best?",choices:["This is too easy lol.","Cool.","Mine is better, check out my project.","The timer made it exciting. Maybe add a sound when time runs out?"],answer:3,explain:"It is kind, it says what worked and why, and it gives a helpful idea."}],challenge:{title:"Click race",short:"Count clicks for ten seconds only, then say the total.",text:"Make a new mini project: when the green flag is clicked, the player has 10 seconds to click a sprite as many times as possible. Clicks after the 10 seconds must NOT count. When time is up, the sprite says the total number of clicks. It must reset every time you click the green flag.",answer:"when green flag clicked\nset [clicks v] to (0)\nset [playing v] to (1)\nwait (10) seconds\nset [playing v] to (0)\nsay (clicks)\n\nwhen this sprite clicked\nif <(playing) = (1)> then\nchange [clicks v] by (1)\nend"},checklist:["My project has Instructions and Notes and Credits, and it is shared in the class studio.","My project starts the same way every time I click the green flag.","I wrote my 3-sentence reflection.","I tried the Mystery Mission and shared what I built."],submit:'Paste the link to your finished capstone and your 3-sentence reflection. Then share your Mystery Mission project and paste that link too. Name your projects anything you like (idea: "Mystery Mission" plus your first name).',teacherNotes:'PACING: warm-up 3 min, final fixes 8, project page 4, pitch plan 4, presentations 10 (small groups of 4 run in parallel and save time), gallery walk 6, reflection 3, Mystery Mission 10 (exit ticket; last 10 minutes, timed). Set up a class Scratch Studio before class and turn on comments; check it after class.\nMYSTERY MISSION: it is a transfer check, not a grade on the capstone. Do not help, but you may read the mission aloud. Score it simply: count resets on the green flag; count goes up on click; message at 10; count is visible. Students who know broadcasts or custom blocks may use them; any working way counts.\nMISCONCEPTIONS: (1) Putting set clicks to 0 inside "when this sprite clicked", so the count never goes past 1. (2) Using a forever loop with if clicks = 10 that says the message again and again; this works but is not needed. (3) Gallery comments that are kind but not specific.\nACCOMMODATIONS: presenting to the whole class is never required; a teacher-only talk, a screen video, or a typed pitch counts the same. Reflection and pitch may be dictated or typed with speech-to-text. For the Mystery Mission, students with extended time get the time in their plan; you may read the mission aloud, and ELL students may use a picture card of the categories (Variables, Events, Control, Operators).'},g1:{title:"Score Keeper",tagline:"Every great game needs a score. Time to build yours.",mission:"Build a game where things fall and your score changes.",minutes:"45",skill:"Random Numbers & Scoring",goal:"By the end of this quest you will use pick random to drop objects from new spots, and use a score variable to build a catch or dodge game.",vocab:[{term:"Random",def:"A number the computer picks by chance, so it is different each time.",icon:"🎲"},{term:"pick random",def:"A green Operators block that gives a random number between two numbers.",icon:"🔢"},{term:"Score",def:"A variable that counts points. It goes up or down during the game.",icon:"🏆"},{term:"Mechanic",def:"The main action a player does in a game, like catch, dodge, or jump.",icon:"🕹️"}],warmup:"Think of a game where things fall or fly at you. Do you catch them or dodge them? What makes your score go up? What makes it go down? Tell a neighbor, or write two sentences.",needs:[],project:{name:"Catch Game",part:"Part 1 of 2"},steps:[{title:"Set up your game",short:"Start a new project. Add a Player sprite and a Faller sprite.",text:"Start a new project. Delete the cat: right-click it in the sprite list and choose <b>delete</b>. Add a sprite for the player at the bottom (a Bowl, a person, or paint one). Add a sprite that will fall from the sky (Apple, Star, Rock, Donut...). Pick a backdrop.<br><br><b>Rename</b> your sprites in the box above the sprite list: call the bottom one <b>Player</b> and the falling one <b>Faller</b>. The code in this quest uses those names.",shots:["delete-sprite","choose-sprite"],tip:"Renaming matters. A <b>touching</b> block looks for the exact sprite name. If your sprite is still called Bowl, pick Bowl in the dropdown instead."},{title:"Move the Player",short:"Make the Player slide left and right with the arrow keys.",text:"The Player stays at the bottom of the Stage. Each time the forever loop runs, it checks the arrow keys and moves a little. You learned <b>key pressed?</b> in t4.",where:"Player",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (0) y: (-150)\nforever\nif <key (right arrow v) pressed?> then\nchange x by (8)\nend\nif <key (left arrow v) pressed?> then\nchange x by (-8)\nend\nend",tip:"Keys do nothing? Click on the Stage first, so Scratch knows you are playing and not typing."},{title:"Drop from a random spot",short:"Make the Faller start at a random spot at the top and fall down.",text:"New block! <b>pick random (1) to (10)</b> is in <b>Operators</b> (green). It gives a different number each time it runs. We use it for x, so the Faller starts at a new spot across the top each time. The Stage goes from x = -240 on the left to x = 240 on the right, so -220 to 220 keeps it on screen.<br><br>When the Faller gets near the bottom (y less than -170), it jumps back to the top at a new random spot.",where:"Faller",change:"new",scaffold:"full",shots:["cat-operators"],blocks:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (pick random (-220) to (220)) y: (170)\nforever\nchange y by (-5)\nif <(y position) < [-170]> then\ngo to x: (pick random (-220) to (220)) y: (170)\nend\nend",tip:"Drag the <b>pick random</b> block right into the white x slot of <b>go to x: y:</b>. Drop it when the slot glows white."},{title:"Make the score",short:"Make a score variable and set it to zero when the game starts.",text:"You made variables in t5. Click <b>Make a Variable</b> in <b>Variables</b> (orange). Name it <code>score</code> and choose <b>For all sprites</b>. Then add <b>set score to 0</b> to the top of your Faller script, right under the hat block.",where:"Faller",change:"add",changeNote:"Add set score to 0 at the very top of your Faller green flag script, right under the hat. Do not make a new script.",scaffold:"full",shots:["make-variable"],blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [score v] to (0)\ngo to x: (pick random (-220) to (220)) y: (170)\nforever\nchange y by (-5)\nif <(y position) < [-170]> then\ngo to x: (pick random (-220) to (220)) y: (170)\nend\nend",tip:"Use <b>set</b>, not <b>change</b>, to reset. Set makes it exactly 0. Change would add to the old score from last game."},{title:"Catch it or dodge it",short:"Pick your game type. Then add the touching check to the Faller.",text:"Choose the kind of game you want. <b>Catching</b>: the Player tries to touch the Faller to earn points. <b>Dodging</b>: the Player tries to stay away from it, and getting hit costs points. Either way, the Faller checks if it is touching the Player. Put the blocks together in the right order.",where:"Faller",change:"add",changeNote:"Add this if block inside the forever loop of your Faller script, right under change y by -5. Keep the bottom check that is already there.",scaffold:"parsons",options:[{label:"Falling (catch it)",short:"Touching the Player gives one point, then the Faller goes back to the top.",blocks:"if <touching (Player v)?> then\nchange [score v] by (1)\nstart sound (pop v)\ngo to x: (pick random (-220) to (220)) y: (170)\nend"},{label:"Dodging (avoid it)",short:"Touching the Player takes away three points, then the Faller goes back up.",blocks:"if <touching (Player v)?> then\nchange [score v] by (-3)\nstart sound (pop v)\ngo to x: (pick random (-220) to (220)) y: (170)\nend"}],tip:"This if goes INSIDE the forever loop of the script you already have. Do not start a second green flag script for the Faller. Two scripts would move it down twice as fast. No pop sound? Pick any sound your sprite has."},{title:"Reaching the bottom",short:"Change the bottom check so the score changes when the Faller lands.",text:"Right now, when the Faller reaches the bottom it just goes back to the top. Make it count!<br><b>If you chose Catching:</b> a miss takes away 1 point.<br><b>If you chose Dodging:</b> every object that lands without hitting you gives 1 point.<br><br>You only need one block from <b>Variables</b>. Think about where it goes: inside the if that checks <b>y position</b>, before the Faller jumps back to the top.",where:"Faller",change:"add",changeNote:"Add one block inside the if y position less than -170 block of your Faller script, above the go to block.",scaffold:"goal",options:[{label:"Falling (catch it)",short:"A miss takes away one point.",blocks:"if <(y position) < [-170]> then\nchange [score v] by (-1)\ngo to x: (pick random (-220) to (220)) y: (170)\nend"},{label:"Dodging (avoid it)",short:"A safe landing gives one point.",blocks:"if <(y position) < [-170]> then\nchange [score v] by (1)\ngo to x: (pick random (-220) to (220)) y: (170)\nend"}],tip:"Score not changing? Check that the block is inside the if, not under the forever loop."},{title:"Two at once",short:"Duplicate the Faller so two objects fall at the same time.",text:"Right-click the Faller in the sprite list and choose <b>duplicate</b>. The copy, Faller2, gets all the same code. Now two objects fall at once! Change the copy's <b>change y by</b> number so it falls at a different speed.",stretch:!0,tip:"Both copies have <b>set score to 0</b>. That is OK, because both run at the green flag and both set 0."},{title:"Playtest and save",short:"Play three rounds, change the fall speed, then save and share.",text:"Play three rounds. Is it too easy or too hard? Change <b>-5</b> in the Faller script: -3 is slower, -8 is faster. Change <b>8</b> in the Player script to make the Player faster or slower. Then save and share.",tip:"Change one number at a time, then test. That way you know which change made the difference."}],quiz:[{q:"What will the score show after you click the green flag?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [score v] to (5)\nchange [score v] by (2)\nset [score v] to (0)\nchange [score v] by (1)",choices:["8","7","1","0"],answer:2,explain:"Set to 0 throws away the 7. Then change by 1 makes it 1."},{q:"This block runs many times. Which number can it NEVER pick?",blocks:"go to x: (pick random (-220) to (220)) y: (170)",choices:["0","-200","220","250"],answer:3,explain:"pick random only gives numbers from -220 to 220. 250 is too big."}],challenge:{title:"The opposite object",short:"Add a second falling object that does the opposite of your first one.",text:"Add a new sprite that also falls from a random spot. Give it a very different look so players can tell them apart.<br><b>Catching game:</b> it is a bad object. Touching it takes away 2 points.<br><b>Dodging game:</b> it is a bonus object. Touching it gives 5 points.<br>When it reaches the bottom, it just goes back to the top with no points. Make it fall at a different speed than the first one.",answer:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (pick random (-220) to (220)) y: (170)\nforever\nchange y by (-7)\nif <touching (Player v)?> then\nchange [score v] by (-2)\ngo to x: (pick random (-220) to (220)) y: (170)\nend\nif <(y position) < [-170]> then\ngo to x: (pick random (-220) to (220)) y: (170)\nend\nend"},checklist:["A Player that moves left and right with the arrow keys","A Faller that drops from a random spot at the top","A score that starts at 0 when I click the green flag","The score changes when the Faller touches the Player","The score changes when the Faller reaches the bottom"],submit:'Save and share your game, then paste the link. Name your project anything you like (idea: "Fruit Catch" or "Rock Dodge"). You will keep building this same project in the next quest, so remember its name!',teacherNotes:"PACING: steps 1-3 about 15 min, steps 4-6 about 15 min, playtest 5 min, quiz 5 min. Step 7 is stretch. Variables come from t5, so step 4 should be quick; do not reteach. MISCONCEPTIONS: (1) The biggest bug is a second green flag script on the Faller instead of adding into the first one. The Faller then moves twice as fast or jumps around. Check the Faller has only ONE green flag script. (2) The touching dropdown must name the Player sprite; if students did not rename, they pick the old name. (3) Students use change instead of set to reset the score. (4) pick random goes in the x slot, not the y slot. (5) Dodging students sometimes add points when touching; ask them to say the rule out loud. g2 builds on both versions (catch or dodge). ACCOMMODATIONS: For students who need less reading, turn on Core only and read aloud; steps 2-4 can be built side by side with the teacher. For the goal step (6), a support option is to say: you need ONE change score block, and it goes inside the bottom if. Let students who struggle with fine motor dragging use a bigger zoom (the plus button in the code area). ELL: point to the block colors (Operators = green, Variables = orange) along with the words. Fast finishers: stretch step 7, then the challenge."},g2:{title:"Clone Army",tagline:"One falling object? Boring. Let's make it rain.",mission:"Use clones, a timer, and a Game Over screen in your game.",minutes:"45",skill:"Clones, Timers & Game Over",goal:"By the end of this quest you will use clones to make many falling objects, add a countdown timer, and end the game with a Game Over screen.",vocab:[{term:"Clone",def:"A copy of a sprite that Scratch makes while the project runs.",icon:"👯"},{term:"Parent",def:"The original sprite. It makes the clones. Clones copy how it looks.",icon:"🏭"},{term:"delete this clone",def:"A block that removes a clone when its job is done.",icon:"🗑️"},{term:"Timer",def:"A variable that counts down, so the game has an end.",icon:"⏱️"}],warmup:"Look at the sky in a rain game: one drop, or many? If you had to code 50 raindrops one by one, how long would it take? Talk about it with a neighbor, or write your guess.",needs:[],starter:{name:"Catch Game starter",contains:"A Player sprite that moves left and right with the arrow keys at y -150. A Faller sprite with ONE green flag script: set score to 0, go to a random x at y 170, then a forever loop with change y by -5, an if touching Player that adds 1 to score and goes back to the top, and an if y position less than -170 that takes away 1 point and goes back to the top. A score variable For all sprites."},project:{name:"Catch Game",part:"Part 2 of 2"},steps:[{title:"Open your Catch Game",short:"Open your Catch Game from My Stuff. Save a copy to be safe.",text:"Open your Catch Game from last quest in <b>My Stuff</b>. Click <b>File</b>, then <b>Save as a copy</b>, so your old version is safe. Missed last quest? Open the starter project at the top of this quest and click <b>Remix</b>.<br><br>Today the Faller becomes a <b>clone factory</b>: one hidden parent sprite that makes a new falling copy every second.",tip:"Only one Faller today. If you made Faller2 in the stretch step, delete it. Clones will do that job now."},{title:"Teach the clones",short:"Build a when I start as a clone script that makes each clone fall.",text:"Find <b>when I start as a clone</b> in <b>Control</b> (orange). Every clone runs this script on its own. Each clone starts at a random spot, shows itself, and falls until it reaches the bottom or touches the Player. Then it uses <b>delete this clone</b>, because a clone that is done should go away. You can drag the blocks you need out of your old green flag script.<br><br>Pick the same game type you chose last quest.",where:"Faller",change:"new",scaffold:"full",shots:["cat-control"],options:[{label:"Falling (catch it)",short:"Touch the Player for one point. A miss takes away one point.",blocks:"when I start as a clone\ngo to x: (pick random (-220) to (220)) y: (170)\nshow\nrepeat until <(y position) < [-170]>\nchange y by (-5)\nif <touching (Player v)?> then\nchange [score v] by (1)\nstart sound (pop v)\ndelete this clone\nend\nend\nchange [score v] by (-1)\ndelete this clone"},{label:"Dodging (avoid it)",short:"Getting hit takes away three points. A safe landing gives one point.",blocks:"when I start as a clone\ngo to x: (pick random (-220) to (220)) y: (170)\nshow\nrepeat until <(y position) < [-170]>\nchange y by (-5)\nif <touching (Player v)?> then\nchange [score v] by (-3)\nstart sound (pop v)\ndelete this clone\nend\nend\nchange [score v] by (1)\ndelete this clone"}],tip:"Why <b>show</b>? A clone copies everything about its parent: costume, size, and if it is hidden. The parent will be hidden, so each clone must show itself."},{title:"Build the clone factory",short:"Make the parent hide and create a new clone every second.",text:"Now change the green flag script. The parent does not fall any more. It resets the score, hides, and makes a clone every second. Put the blocks in the right order.",where:"Faller",change:"replace",changeNote:"Replaces your whole old Faller green flag script. Delete any old blocks that are left under it, so the Faller has only this green flag script.",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [score v] to (0)\nhide\nforever\ncreate clone of (myself v)\nwait (1) seconds\nend",tip:"Put <b>create clone</b> ONLY in this green flag script. If it is inside <b>when I start as a clone</b>, every clone makes more clones, and the game fills up and freezes. Scratch stops at 300 clones."},{title:"Add a countdown timer",short:"Make a time variable on the Stage that counts down from thirty.",text:"Click the <b>Stage</b> (bottom right). Make a variable called <code>time</code>. On the Stage, every variable is <b>For all sprites</b>, so all sprites can read it. Rename your play backdrop to <b>Play</b> in the <b>Backdrops</b> tab. This script starts on the Play backdrop, counts down one second at a time, and sends <b>game over</b> when time is 0. Choose <b>New message</b> in the broadcast dropdown to make the game over message.",where:"Stage",change:"new",scaffold:"parsons",shots:["select-stage","make-variable"],blocks:"when green flag clicked\nswitch backdrop to (Play v)\nset [time v] to (30)\nrepeat until <(time) = [0]>\nwait (1) seconds\nchange [time v] by (-1)\nend\nbroadcast (game over v)",tip:"Make sure the Stage is selected (it has a blue outline) before you add these blocks. If you build this on a sprite, it still works, but it is harder to find later."},{title:"Game Over screen",short:"Paint a Game Over backdrop. Show it and stop the game when time ends.",text:"In the Stage's <b>Backdrops</b> tab, paint a new backdrop. Use the text tool to write GAME OVER. Name it <b>Game Over</b>.<br><br>Now build a script on the Stage, with no blocks shown: when the Stage receives the <b>game over</b> message, it switches to the Game Over backdrop, then stops everything. You know all of these blocks: one from <b>Events</b>, one from <b>Looks</b>, one from <b>Control</b>.",where:"Stage",change:"new",scaffold:"goal",shots:["backdrops"],blocks:"when I receive [game over v]\nswitch backdrop to (Game Over v)\nstop [all v]",tip:"<b>stop all</b> also deletes every clone, so the screen clears and only your Player and score stay. Test it: set time to 5 for a quick check, then put it back to 30."},{title:"Make a speed variable",short:"Make a speed variable that grows by one every five seconds.",text:"Good games get harder slowly. Click the Stage and make a variable called <code>speed</code>. It starts at 3 and goes up by 1 every 5 seconds. Like <code>time</code>, it is <b>For all sprites</b>, so every clone can read it.",where:"Stage",change:"new",scaffold:"parsons",stretch:!0,shots:["make-variable"],blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [speed v] to (3)\nforever\nwait (5) seconds\nchange [speed v] by (1)\nend",tip:"Nothing gets faster yet. The clones still use -5. The next step connects them to speed."},{title:"Clones use the speed",short:"Make the clones fall at the speed from your speed variable.",text:"Clones need to move DOWN by <code>speed</code>. Down means a negative number. Scratch has no minus sign block for a variable, so we use <b>0 minus speed</b> from <b>Operators</b>. If speed is 3, then 0 - 3 = -3, so the clone moves down 3.",where:"Faller",change:"replace",changeNote:"In your when I start as a clone script, take out change y by -5 and put this block in the same place, inside the repeat until loop.",scaffold:"full",stretch:!0,shots:["cat-operators"],blocks:"change y by ((0) - (speed))",tip:"Put the <b>minus</b> block in the slot first. Type 0 in its left side. Then drag the <b>speed</b> reporter into its right side."},{title:"Playtest and share",short:"Play a full round, change your numbers, then save and share.",text:"Play a full 30 second round. Is the start fun? Is the end exciting? Try changing the wait in the factory (1 second) or the timer (30). Then save and share.",tip:"Too many clones at once? Make the factory wait longer, like 1.5 seconds."}],quiz:[{q:"You click the green flag. How many sprites on the Stage say hi?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nhide\nrepeat (3)\ncreate clone of (myself v)\nend\n\nwhen I start as a clone\nshow\nsay [hi]",choices:["0","1","3","4"],answer:2,explain:"Three clones are made, and each one runs its own script. The parent stays hidden and never says hi."},{q:"Mia's game freezes after a few seconds. What is the bug?",blocks:"when I start as a clone\nshow\nforever\ncreate clone of (myself v)\nchange y by (-5)\nend",choices:["The clone should say hello first","Every clone makes more clones, so there are soon too many","The show block should be hide","Clones cannot move"],answer:1,explain:"Create clone belongs in the parent's script. Here each clone makes copies forever, until Scratch hits 300."}],challenge:{title:"Golden bonus",short:"Make one clone out of ten golden and worth five points.",text:"Paint a second gold costume for the Faller and name it <b>gold</b>. Name the first costume <b>normal</b>. When a clone starts, it picks a random number from 1 to 10. If it is 10, the clone wears the gold costume and is worth 5 points. If not, it wears the normal costume and is worth 1. Hint: each clone needs its OWN worth, so make a variable called <code>worth</code> with <b>For this sprite only</b>. Then each clone gets its own copy. Use <b>change score by worth</b> when it is caught.",answer:"when I start as a clone\nif <(pick random (1) to (10)) = [10]> then\nswitch costume to (gold v)\nset [worth v] to (5)\nelse\nswitch costume to (normal v)\nset [worth v] to (1)\nend\ngo to x: (pick random (-220) to (220)) y: (170)\nshow\nrepeat until <(y position) < [-170]>\nchange y by (-5)\nif <touching (Player v)?> then\nchange [score v] by (worth)\ndelete this clone\nend\nend\ndelete this clone"},checklist:["The parent Faller hides and makes a new clone every second","Clones fall from random spots and delete themselves when done","A time variable counts down from 30","A Game Over backdrop shows when time runs out, and the game stops","The score still works with clones"],submit:'Save and share your finished game, then paste the link. Name your project anything you like (idea: "Rain of Donuts" or "Meteor Dodge").',teacherNotes:"PACING: step 1 about 3 min, steps 2-3 (clones) about 15 min, steps 4-5 (timer and Game Over) about 12 min, playtest 5 min, quiz 5 min. Steps 6-7 (difficulty) are stretch. Clones are the key idea; give them the most time. MISCONCEPTIONS: (1) Clone movement code left under the green flag: the parent then falls too and is visible. The parent should only hide and make clones. (2) Parent not hidden: a frozen object sits at the top. (3) Forgetting show: clones copy the hidden parent, so nothing appears. (4) create clone inside when I start as a clone: clones multiply until Scratch hits 300 and the project lags. (5) Missing delete this clone: clones pile up at the bottom. (6) Variable scope: time and speed live on the Stage, so they are For all sprites; score is For all sprites too. If a student made score For this sprite only on the Faller, each clone gets its own copy and the score monitor will not add up. Fix by deleting it and making it again For all sprites. (7) Wrong backdrop shows: check the backdrop names Play and Game Over match the dropdowns exactly. STARTER: a student who missed g1 can Remix the teacher's starter (catch version with score) and begin at step 1. ACCOMMODATIONS: Core only mode hides steps 6-7. For the goal step (5), a support option is to give the three block names on a sticky note. For students who need more support, steps 2 and 3 can be done together with the teacher using the class screen. ELL: act out parent and clone with a rubber stamp or by copying a drawing. Fast finishers: stretch steps, then the Golden bonus challenge (it uses For this sprite only on purpose, a great scope example)."},g3:{title:"Gravity & Jumping",tagline:"What goes up must come down. Build a real platformer hero.",mission:"Make a hero that falls, lands on platforms, and jumps.",minutes:"45",skill:"Velocity, Gravity & Platforms",goal:"By the end of this quest you will use a y speed variable to make gravity, land on platforms, and jump only when standing on the ground.",vocab:[{term:"Velocity",def:"How fast something moves, and which way. Here: up and down speed.",icon:"💨"},{term:"Gravity",def:"A pull downward. In code, we make y speed a little smaller every time.",icon:"🌍"},{term:"Platform",def:"A floor or ledge the player can stand on.",icon:"🧱"},{term:"Model",def:"A simple copy of something real. It keeps some parts and leaves others out.",icon:"🧪"}],warmup:"Toss a ball or a balled-up paper gently straight up and catch it. Is its speed the same the whole time? When is it slowest? You can do this alone, or watch a neighbor do it and talk about it.",needs:["math"],project:{name:"Platformer",part:"Part 1 of 2"},steps:[{title:"Paint the level",short:"Paint a backdrop with a ground and platforms, all in one color.",text:"Start a new project. Click the <b>Stage</b>, open the <b>Backdrops</b> tab, and paint a backdrop. Use the rectangle tool to draw a ground along the bottom and three or four floating platforms. Make ALL of them the same color, like green. Leave gaps the player can jump across. Name this backdrop <b>level1</b>.",shots:["select-stage","backdrops"],tip:"Use your platform color ONLY for platforms and ground. If the background, coins, or another sprite have that same color, touching it will count as ground. Use the same fill color for every rectangle; do not pick a slightly different green."},{title:"Make the player walk",short:"Add a small Player sprite. Make it walk with the arrow keys.",text:"Add or paint a small player sprite (about as tall as a platform is thick, times two). Rename it <b>Player</b>. Then give it this script to walk left and right.",where:"Player",change:"new",scaffold:"full",shots:["choose-sprite","costumes-tab"],blocks:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (-200) y: (0)\nforever\nif <key (right arrow v) pressed?> then\nchange x by (5)\nend\nif <key (left arrow v) pressed?> then\nchange x by (-5)\nend\nend",tip:"Check the costume center: in the Costumes tab, the drawing should sit right on the small crosshair in the middle. If it is off to the side, the Player wobbles and lands in strange places. Use the arrow tool to drag the whole drawing onto the crosshair."},{title:"Add gravity",short:"Make a y speed variable. Gravity makes the Player fall faster and faster.",text:"Make a variable named <code>y speed</code> and choose <b>For this sprite only</b>. Then build a SECOND green flag script on the Player (keep the walk script). Every time the loop runs, gravity takes 1 away from y speed, and the Player moves by y speed. Click the green flag: the Player falls faster and faster, right through the ground. That is OK for now!",where:"Player",change:"new",scaffold:"full",shots:["make-variable"],blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [y speed v] to (0)\nforever\nchange [y speed v] by (-1)\nchange y by (y speed)\nend",tip:"Why <b>For this sprite only</b>? Only the Player uses this speed. If you add an enemy that also falls later, it can have its own y speed with the same name, and the two speeds will not mix up."},{title:"Land on the ground",short:"Stop falling when the Player touches the platform color.",text:"Now the Player needs to stop when it hits a platform. When it touches the platform color, it moves back by the last step (0 minus y speed), so it stands on top, not inside. Then y speed becomes 0. Put the blocks in order. Use the eyedropper in <b>touching color</b> to click your platform on the Stage.",where:"Player",change:"replace",changeNote:"Replaces your step 3 gravity script (the one with set y speed to 0). Keep the walk script as it is.",scaffold:"parsons",shots:["touching-color"],blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [y speed v] to (0)\nforever\nchange [y speed v] by (-1)\nchange y by (y speed)\nif <touching color [#22c55e]?> then\nchange y by ((0) - (y speed))\nset [y speed v] to (0)\nend\nend",tip:"Sinking into the ground? You are missing <b>change y by 0 minus y speed</b>. That block undoes the last move, which pulls the Player back out of the platform."},{title:"Jump!",short:"Let the Player jump, but only while standing on a platform.",text:"A jump is just a big UP speed. Set y speed to 14 and gravity will slow it down and pull the Player back. The check for the up arrow goes INSIDE the touching color if. That way the Player can only jump when it is on a platform, not in the air.",where:"Player",change:"add",changeNote:"Add this if block inside the if touching color block of your gravity script, right after set y speed to 0. It is an if inside an if.",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"if <key (up arrow v) pressed?> then\nset [y speed v] to (14)\nend",tip:"If the Player can fly by holding the up arrow, the jump if is outside the touching color if. Drag it in, under set y speed to 0."},{title:"Fall off, start again",short:"If the Player falls off the bottom, send it back to the start.",text:"Build this one yourself. If the Player falls below the bottom of the Stage (y position less than -170), it goes back to the start (x: -200, y: 0) and its y speed becomes 0, so it does not keep its falling speed. Hint: this is an <b>if</b> inside the forever loop of the gravity script. You used <b>y position</b> and <b>less than</b> in the Catch Game or in t5.",where:"Player",change:"add",changeNote:"Add this if block inside the forever loop of your gravity script, at the bottom, after the touching color if.",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"if <(y position) < [-170]> then\ngo to x: (-200) y: (0)\nset [y speed v] to (0)\nend",tip:"Not working? Check that your level has a gap, and that the if is inside the forever loop."},{title:"Tune the physics",short:"Try new gravity and jump numbers until the game feels good.",text:"Change one number at a time and test. Gravity: try -1 and -2. Jump: try 10, 14, and 18. Walk: try 4 and 7. Can you reach every platform? Is any platform too easy? Move platforms in the paint editor if you need to. Then save and share.",tip:"If gravity is very strong, the Player can fall through thin platforms. Make platforms thicker, or use a smaller gravity number."},{title:"Face the way you walk",short:"Make the Player turn to face left or right as it walks.",text:"Add <b>set rotation style left-right</b> at the top of the walk script. Then add <b>point in direction 90</b> when right is pressed and <b>point in direction -90</b> when left is pressed. Now the Player faces the way it walks, without turning upside down.",where:"Player",change:"replace",changeNote:"Replaces your walk script (the one with the arrow keys).",scaffold:"full",stretch:!0,blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset rotation style [left-right v]\ngo to x: (-200) y: (0)\nforever\nif <key (right arrow v) pressed?> then\npoint in direction (90)\nchange x by (5)\nend\nif <key (left arrow v) pressed?> then\npoint in direction (-90)\nchange x by (-5)\nend\nend",tip:"Without left-right rotation style, pointing in direction -90 turns the Player upside down."}],quiz:[{q:"The Player starts at y: 0. What is its y position after this runs?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (0) y: (0)\nset [y speed v] to (3)\nrepeat (3)\nchange [y speed v] by (-1)\nchange y by (y speed)\nend",choices:["0","3","-3","6"],answer:1,explain:"y speed becomes 2, then 1, then 0. The Player moves up 2, then 1, then 0. So y is 3."},{q:"Why is the up arrow check inside the touching color if?",choices:["So jumps go higher","So the game runs faster","It does not matter where it goes","So the Player can only jump while standing on a platform"],answer:3,explain:"Outside that if, the Player could keep jumping in the air and fly away."}],challenge:{title:"Bonk your head",short:"Make the Player bump its head on the bottom of platforms.",text:"Paint a thin strip of a NEW color (like red) under some platforms. If the Player touches red while moving up (y speed more than 0), it should move back out of the red and stop going up, so it bumps its head and falls back down. Do not use your platform color for this strip.",answer:"if <touching color [#ef4444]?> then\nif <(y speed) > [0]> then\nchange y by ((0) - (y speed))\nset [y speed v] to (0)\nend\nend"},checklist:["A level backdrop with a ground and platforms in one color","A Player that walks left and right","Gravity that pulls the Player down and lands it on platforms","The Player can jump only when standing on a platform","Falling off the bottom sends the Player back to the start"],submit:'Save and share your game, then paste the link. Name your project anything you like (idea: "Sky Jumper"). You will add levels to this same project next quest, so remember its name!',teacherNotes:'PACING: steps 1-2 about 12 min (painting can take over; set a timer), steps 3-5 about 15 min, steps 6-7 about 8 min, quiz 5 min. Step 8 is stretch. PATHS: students come from Games, Stories, or Apps, so do not assume they used pick random or clones; this lesson uses neither. Variables, if-else, and comparisons come from t5. MISCONCEPTIONS: (1) Player sinks into the ground: the push-back block is missing. (2) Player stands in the air: the backdrop or another sprite has the platform color somewhere else (touching color ignores the Player\'s own costume, so the costume color does not matter). (3) Player can fly: the jump if is outside the touching color if. (4) Students put the gravity blocks into the walk script, so one loop does everything; that also works, but it is harder to read. (5) y speed made For all sprites still works, but ask why For this sprite only is better. (6) Walking into the side of a platform can make the Player get stuck; that is OK for now. Level design can avoid tall walls. STANDARDS WRAP-UP (CT.e.1): ask "Real objects fall because of gravity, but what did our model leave out?" (air, friction, bouncing, weight). Then ask why it is OK to ignore those things in a game. ACCOMMODATIONS: Core only mode hides step 8. Students with fine motor needs can draw platforms with only the rectangle tool, or use a teacher-made level1 backdrop. For the goal step (6), a support option is to show the blocks needed (if, less than, y position, go to, set) without the order. Read aloud the quiz trace and let students trace with a table of y speed and y.'},g4:{title:"Level Up!",tagline:"Add levels, lives, and a win screen worth bragging about.",mission:"Add three levels, lives, and win and lose screens to your platformer.",minutes:"45",skill:"Levels, Lives & Win/Lose",goal:"By the end of this quest you will use a level variable and messages to run three levels, move a door for each level, give the player lives, and end with a You Win or Game Over screen.",vocab:[{term:"Level",def:"One part of a game. Each level is usually harder than the last.",icon:"🪜"},{term:"Lives",def:"How many more tries the player has.",icon:"❤️"},{term:"Win or lose rule",def:"The rule that decides when the game ends, and if you won.",icon:"🏁"},{term:"Message",def:"A signal one script sends with broadcast. Other scripts start when they receive it.",icon:"📣"}],warmup:"Think of a great level complete moment in a game. What did the game do to make it feel good: a sound, a message, a new place? Tell a neighbor, or write one sentence.",needs:[],starter:{name:"Platformer starter",contains:"A backdrop named level1 with a ground and platforms in one color. A Player sprite with two green flag scripts: a walk script (go to x -200 y 0, then a forever loop with left and right arrow keys) and a gravity script (y speed For this sprite only; change y speed by -1 and change y by y speed; if touching the platform color, move back by 0 minus y speed, set y speed to 0, and jump with the up arrow by setting y speed to 14; if y position less than -170, go to x -200 y 0 and set y speed to 0)."},project:{name:"Platformer",part:"Part 2 of 2"},steps:[{title:"Make your backdrops",short:"Make three level backdrops, a You Win backdrop, and a Game Over backdrop.",text:"Open your Platformer from <b>My Stuff</b> (or Remix the starter project at the top of this quest). Click the <b>Stage</b> and open <b>Backdrops</b>. Right-click <b>level1</b> and choose <b>duplicate</b> two times. Rename the copies <b>level2</b> and <b>level3</b>, and move their platforms to make new levels. Make level3 the hardest.<br><br>Then paint two more backdrops with the text tool: <b>You Win</b> and <b>Game Over</b>.",shots:["select-stage","backdrops"],tip:"Keep the platform color the SAME on every level, so your gravity code still works. Do not use the platform color on the You Win or Game Over backdrops."},{title:"The level manager",short:"Make level and lives variables. The Stage starts the right level.",text:"Click the Stage. Make two variables, <code>level</code> and <code>lives</code> (on the Stage they are For all sprites). The Stage is the <b>level manager</b>. On the green flag, it sets level to 1 and lives to 3, then broadcasts a new message called <b>start level</b>. When it receives start level, it switches to the backdrop that matches the level number.",where:"Stage",change:"new",scaffold:"full",shots:["make-variable"],blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [level v] to (1)\nset [lives v] to (3)\nbroadcast (start level v)\n\nwhen I receive [start level v]\nif <(level) = [1]> then\nswitch backdrop to (level1 v)\nend\nif <(level) = [2]> then\nswitch backdrop to (level2 v)\nend\nif <(level) = [3]> then\nswitch backdrop to (level3 v)\nend",tip:"This step has TWO scripts, both on the Stage. The blank line shows where one script ends and the next begins."},{title:"A door that moves",short:"Add a Door sprite that moves to a new spot on each level.",text:"Add a sprite for the exit, like a door or a flag, and rename it <b>Door</b>. There is only ONE Door, so it must move to a new place for each level. When it receives start level, it shows and goes to the spot for that level. Put the blocks in order, then change the x and y numbers to fit YOUR levels.",where:"Door",change:"new",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when I receive [start level v]\nshow\nif <(level) = [1]> then\ngo to x: (200) y: (-120)\nend\nif <(level) = [2]> then\ngo to x: (190) y: (60)\nend\nif <(level) = [3]> then\ngo to x: (-180) y: (130)\nend",tip:"To find a spot: switch the Stage to that level backdrop, drag the Door there, and read its x and y in the sprite info under the Stage. Type those numbers into the go to block."},{title:"Touch the door",short:"When the Player touches the Door, go back to start and send next level.",text:"On the Player, check for the Door. First move the Player back to the start and stop its fall. Then broadcast a new message, <b>next level</b>. Moving first matters: if the Player stayed on the Door, it would send next level again and skip levels.",where:"Player",change:"add",changeNote:"Add this if block inside the forever loop of your Player gravity script, at the bottom, after the fall-off check.",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"if <touching (Door v)?> then\ngo to x: (-200) y: (0)\nset [y speed v] to (0)\nbroadcast (next level v)\nend",tip:"Make the start spot (x: -200, y: 0) far away from every Door spot."},{title:"Win at level three",short:"The Stage adds one to level. After level three, show You Win.",text:"Click the Stage. Build ONE <b>when I receive next level</b> script, and keep it on the Stage only. It adds 1 to level. If level is now more than 3, the player beat level 3: switch to You Win, broadcast a new message <b>end game</b> and wait, then stop all. Else, broadcast start level, so the backdrop and the Door change. Put the blocks in order.",where:"Stage",change:"new",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when I receive [next level v]\nchange [level v] by (1)\nif <(level) > [3]> then\nswitch backdrop to (You Win v)\nbroadcast (end game v) and wait\nstop [all v]\nelse\nbroadcast (start level v)\nend",tip:"Only ONE script should receive next level. If two scripts both change level, they can run in either order and skip a level. There is no level4, so the game must end after level 3."},{title:"Lives and Game Over",short:"Falling off costs a life. With no lives left, show Game Over.",text:"Build this yourself. Change the fall-off check in the Player gravity script. When the Player falls below -170, it loses 1 life and goes back to the start with y speed 0, like before. Then, if lives is 0, switch the backdrop to <b>Game Over</b>, broadcast <b>end game</b> and wait, and stop all. Look at your Win script for ideas: the ending is almost the same.",where:"Player",change:"replace",changeNote:"Replaces the if y position less than -170 block inside your Player gravity script. Keep the touching color if and the touching Door if.",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"if <(y position) < [-170]> then\nchange [lives v] by (-1)\ngo to x: (-200) y: (0)\nset [y speed v] to (0)\nif <(lives) = [0]> then\nswitch backdrop to (Game Over v)\nbroadcast (end game v) and wait\nstop [all v]\nend\nend",tip:"Sprites can use <b>switch backdrop to</b> too. It is in Looks. Test quickly: set lives to 1 on the Stage, fall once, then set it back to 3."},{title:"Clean ending",short:"Hide the Player and Door at the end. Show the Player at the start.",text:"On the win and lose screens, the Player and the Door should not be there. Build it yourself: the <b>Player</b> and the <b>Door</b> each get a script that hides when they receive <b>end game</b>. Then the Player must show again when the green flag is clicked, or it stays hidden next game. (The Door already shows itself in its start level script.)",where:"Player and Door",change:"new",changeNote:"One new end game script on the Player and one on the Door. Also add show to the top of the Player walk script, right under the hat.",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"when I receive [end game v]\nhide\n\nwhen green flag clicked\nshow\ngo to x: (-200) y: (0)\nforever\nif <key (right arrow v) pressed?> then\nchange x by (5)\nend\nif <key (left arrow v) pressed?> then\nchange x by (-5)\nend\nend",tip:"Why broadcast and wait before stop all? It waits until every end game script is done, so the sprites hide before everything stops."},{title:"Coins and high score",short:"Add a coin for points, and keep the best score ever.",text:"Make two variables on the Stage: <code>score</code> and <code>high score</code>. Add <b>set score to 0</b> to the Stage green flag script. Do NOT reset high score on the green flag, so it stays from game to game.<br><br>Add a <b>Coin</b> sprite. On each level it shows, waits until the Player touches it, then adds 10 points and hides. Pick one spot that works on all three levels (or copy the Door's ifs). When the game ends, the Stage checks for a new high score.",where:"Coin and Stage",change:"new",changeNote:"First script goes on the Coin. Second script goes on the Stage. Also add set score to 0 to the Stage green flag script.",scaffold:"full",stretch:!0,shots:["make-variable"],blocks:"when I receive [start level v]\ngo to x: (0) y: (100)\nshow\nwait until <touching (Player v)?>\nchange [score v] by (10)\nhide\n\nwhen I receive [end game v]\nif <(score) > (high score)> then\nset [high score v] to (score)\nend",tip:"A new start level message restarts the Coin script from the top, so the coin comes back on each level. Give the Coin an end game script that hides it too, like the Player and Door."}],quiz:[{q:"Level is 3. The Player touches the Door. What happens?",blocks:"when I receive [next level v]\nchange [level v] by (1)\nif <(level) > [3]> then\nswitch backdrop to (You Win v)\nbroadcast (end game v) and wait\nstop [all v]\nelse\nbroadcast (start level v)\nend",choices:["The game goes to level 4","The level 3 backdrop shows again","You Win shows and the game stops","Nothing happens"],answer:2,explain:"Level becomes 4. 4 is more than 3, so the if part runs: You Win, then stop all."},{q:"High score is 40. You score 25 this game. What is high score after the game ends?",blocks:"when I receive [end game v]\nif <(score) > (high score)> then\nset [high score v] to (score)\nend",choices:["40","25","65","0"],answer:0,explain:"25 is not more than 40, so the if does not run. High score stays 40."}],challenge:{title:"Enemy patrol",short:"Add an enemy that walks on a platform and costs a life.",text:"Add an <b>Enemy</b> sprite. When a level starts, it goes to a platform and walks back and forth forever. It walks faster on higher levels: use the level variable in its speed. If the Player touches the Enemy, treat it like falling off: lose a life, go back to start, and check for Game Over. Use ONE Enemy sprite (no clones needed).",answer:"when I receive [start level v]\ngo to x: (0) y: (-110)\nshow\nforever\nrepeat (40)\nchange x by ((level) * (2))\nend\nrepeat (40)\nchange x by ((0) - ((level) * (2)))\nend\nend\n\nif <touching (Enemy v)?> then\nchange [lives v] by (-1)\ngo to x: (-200) y: (0)\nset [y speed v] to (0)\nif <(lives) = [0]> then\nswitch backdrop to (Game Over v)\nbroadcast (end game v) and wait\nstop [all v]\nend\nend"},checklist:["Three level backdrops that the game moves through in order","A Door that moves to a new spot on each level","Lives that go down when the Player falls off","A You Win screen after level 3 and a Game Over screen when lives run out","The Player and Door hide at the end and come back on the green flag"],submit:'Play your whole game from level 1 to You Win. Then save, share, and paste the link. Name your project anything you like (idea: "Castle Climb").',teacherNotes:"PACING: step 1 about 8 min (keep backdrop edits quick), steps 2-4 about 12 min, steps 5-7 about 15 min, playtest and quiz 8 min. Step 8 is stretch. PATHS: students may come from Stories or Apps at fork 1, so this lesson avoids clones, join, and pick random. The level manager uses if level = 1, 2, 3 instead of join. A student who missed g3 can Remix the starter. MISCONCEPTIONS: (1) Skipping levels: the Player stays on the Door and broadcasts next level many times. Moving the Player to start BEFORE broadcasting fixes it. (2) Two when I receive next level scripts (for example one on the Door and one on the Stage) both change level and race. Keep only the Stage one. (3) Door does not move: backdrop names or level numbers in the ifs do not match. (4) Player stays hidden after a win: show is missing on the green flag. (5) High score resets: a student added set high score to 0 on the green flag. (6) stop all before hiding: use broadcast end game and wait, then stop all. ACCOMMODATIONS: Core only mode hides step 8. For students who need less building, give a starter with level2 and level3 already painted, so step 1 is just painting two text backdrops. For the goal steps (6 and 7), a support option is to point at the Win script in step 5 and say: copy that ending. ELL: draw the message flow on the board (Player sends next level, Stage receives it, Stage sends start level, Door and Stage receive it)."},m1:{title:"Bug Hunters & Code Readers",tagline:"Every coder makes bugs. Great coders can find them and read any code.",mission:"Find and fix bugs, then read code that someone else wrote.",minutes:"45",skill:"Debugging & Reading Code",goal:"By the end of this quest you will use four bug hunting strategies to fix four real bugs, and explain what a script in someone else's project does.",vocab:[{term:"Bug",def:"A mistake in code that makes the program do something you did not expect.",icon:"🐛"},{term:"Debugging",def:"Finding a bug and fixing it.",icon:"🔍"},{term:"Expected vs. actual",def:"What you wanted to happen, and what really happened. The difference is the bug.",icon:"⚖️"},{term:"Trace",def:"Read code one block at a time, the same way the computer runs it.",icon:"👣"},{term:"Test",def:"Run the program on purpose to check if it works.",icon:"🧪"}],warmup:"Think of one bug you had in a project so far. What did you expect? What really happened? How did you fix it? Write one sentence, or tell a neighbor.",needs:[],steps:[{title:"The Bug Hunter's toolkit",short:"Write the four bug hunting strategies in your notes.",text:"Make a new project. Then write these four strategies in your notes. You will use them on every bug today.<br><b>1. Expected vs. actual:</b> say out loud what SHOULD happen and what DOES happen.<br><b>2. Trace:</b> read the script one block at a time, like the computer.<br><b>3. Test small:</b> click one script by itself and watch what it does.<br><b>4. Watch values:</b> check the box next to a variable so you can see it on the Stage while the project runs.",frame:"Strategy 1: Expected vs. actual means ___\nStrategy 2: Trace means ___\nStrategy 3: Test small means ___\nStrategy 4: Watch values means ___\nThe one I want to try first: ___",tip:"Name your project anything you like. One idea: Bug Hunt."},{title:"Bug #1: The frozen sprite (worked example)",short:"Build the buggy script, find the bug, and watch how we fix it.",text:'Build this buggy script on the cat sprite, exactly as you see it:<pre class="blocks">when green flag clicked\nif &lt;key (right arrow v) pressed?&gt; then\nchange x by (10)\nend</pre>Click the green flag, then press the right arrow. The cat does not move!<br><b>Expected:</b> the cat moves every time I press the arrow. <b>Actual:</b> nothing happens.<br><b>Trace it:</b> the green flag is clicked. The <code>if</code> checks the key ONE time, right away. You were not pressing the key yet, so it skips the inside. Then the script is done. Nothing checks again.<br><b>Fix:</b> put the <code>if</code> inside a <code>forever</code> so the key is checked again and again. The fixed script is below.',where:"Cat sprite (Sprite1)",change:"replace",changeNote:"Fixes the buggy script you just built: drag a forever between the hat and the if.",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nforever\nif <key (right arrow v) pressed?> then\nchange x by (10)\nend\nend",tip:"Click the Stage first so Scratch hears your key presses."},{title:"Bug #2: The runaway score",short:"Build the score bug, then put the fixed blocks in order.",text:'Add the <b>Apple</b> sprite. Make a variable called <code>score</code> (For all sprites). On the cat, build this buggy script:<pre class="blocks">when green flag clicked\nforever\nif &lt;touching (Apple v)?&gt; then\nchange [score v] by (1)\nend\nend</pre>Drag the cat onto the apple, then away. Click the green flag again. Watch the score.<br>This script has <b>TWO</b> bugs. Expected: the score starts at 0, and each touch gives 1 point. Actual: what do you see?<br>Fix both bugs. The fix uses one new block: <code>not</code>, the green block in <b>Operators</b>. <code>not &lt;touching Apple?&gt;</code> is true when the cat is NOT touching the apple.',where:"Cat sprite (Sprite1)",change:"replace",changeNote:"Replaces the buggy score script you built in this step.",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [score v] to (0)\nforever\nif <touching (Apple v)?> then\nchange [score v] by (1)\nwait until <not <touching (Apple v)?>>\nend\nend",shots:["make-variable"],tip:"Use strategy 4: check the box next to score and watch it. How many times does the forever loop run while the cat sits on the apple?"},{title:"Bug #3: The missing hero",short:"Build the hide bug, then fix it without help.",text:'On the cat, build these two scripts:<pre class="blocks">when [h v] key pressed\nhide\n\nwhen green flag clicked\ngo to x: (0) y: (0)\nsay [Here I am!] for (2) seconds</pre>Press <b>h</b>. Then click the green flag. The cat says "Here I am!" but you cannot see it!<br>Fix it so the cat is ALWAYS seen after you click the green flag, even if someone pressed h before.',where:"Cat sprite (Sprite1)",change:"add",changeNote:"Add to the green flag script from this step. Do not change the h key script.",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nshow\ngo to x: (0) y: (0)\nsay [Here I am!] for (2) seconds",tip:"Trace it: what did the sprite do the LAST time the project ran? Does the green flag script undo that?"},{title:"Bug #4: The message that never arrives",short:"Build the message bug, then find out why the apple never talks.",text:'On the cat, build this script. Use <b>New message</b> and type <code>start game</code>:<pre class="blocks">when green flag clicked\nsay [Ready?] for (2) seconds\nbroadcast (start game v)</pre>On the <b>Apple</b>, build this script. Use <b>New message</b> again and type the name with a typo: <code>start gmae</code>.<pre class="blocks">when I receive [start gmae v]\nsay [Let\'s go!] for (2) seconds</pre>Click the green flag. The cat talks, but the apple never says "Let\'s go!". Find the bug and fix it.',where:"Apple sprite",change:"replace",changeNote:"Fixes the when I receive script on the Apple. Do not change the cat.",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"when I receive [start game v]\nsay [Let's go!] for (2) seconds",tip:"Test small: click the Apple script by itself. It works! So the problem is not inside it. Now read the message names on both scripts, letter by letter."},{title:"Code Reader",short:"Open a project from a different branch and explain one script.",text:"Good coders can read code they did not write. Open a classmate's shared project from a <b>different branch</b> than yours (Games, Stories, or Apps). Your teacher's class studio has them.<br>1. Click <b>See Inside</b>.<br>2. Pick ONE script with 4 to 10 blocks. Click each sprite to find one.<br>3. Trace it. Write what it does with the frame.<br>4. Predict: pick one number or word to change. Write what you think will happen.<br>5. Make the change and click the green flag. Were you right?<br>You do not need to save. Changes in See Inside are not saved unless you click <b>Remix</b>.",solo:"No classmate projects yet? Open your teacher's example project, or a Starter Project on the Scratch website: click <b>Ideas</b> at the top, then scroll to <b>Starter Projects</b>.",frame:"Project name: ___   Branch: ___\nSprite: ___\nThis script starts when ___.\nFirst it ___. Then it ___. Last it ___.\nI think the coder made it so that ___.\nMy prediction: if I change ___ to ___, then ___ will happen.\nWhat really happened: ___",tip:"See a block you do not know? Drag it out alone and click it to test it small. Then put it back."},{title:"Write a bug report",short:"Write about one bug you fixed in Notes and Credits.",text:"Click the green flag one more time and test all four fixes. Then click <b>Share</b>. On the project page, find the <b>Notes and Credits</b> box. Write a bug report about ONE bug you fixed today.",frame:"Bug: ___\nI expected ___.\nBut actually ___.\nThe strategy that helped me: ___.\nI fixed it by ___."},{title:"Bug swap",short:"Hide one bug in a copy of your project for a partner.",stretch:!0,text:"Open your branch project. Click <b>File</b>, then <b>Save as a copy</b>, so your working project stays safe. In the copy, add ONE small bug: a wrong number, a missing reset, or a block in the wrong place. Swap computers with a partner who is also on this step. Find and fix their bug with the toolkit. Tell them which strategy helped.",solo:"No partner free? Open your teacher's Mystery Bug project. It has three hidden bugs. Find and fix as many as you can, and write which strategy helped for each one.",tip:"Keep it fair: only one small bug, and you must know how to fix it yourself."}],quiz:[{q:"Trace this script. What happens when you click the green flag?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [score v] to (0)\nwait until <(score) > (5)>\nsay [You win!]",choices:["The sprite says You win! right away","The sprite says You win! after 5 seconds","The sprite never says anything","The score goes up to 6"],answer:2,explain:"Nothing ever changes the score, so it stays 0. The wait until waits forever."},{q:"Trace this script. What will the sprite say?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [lives v] to (3)\nchange [lives v] by (-1)\nif <(lives) = (3)> then\nsay [Full health!]\nelse\nsay [Ouch!]\nend",choices:["Ouch!","Full health!","Both messages","Nothing"],answer:0,explain:"Lives starts at 3, then changes by -1 to 2. 2 is not 3, so the else part runs."}],challenge:{title:"Bug-proof countdown",short:"Build a countdown from ten that ends with Blast off.",text:"Make a variable called count. When the green flag is clicked, the sprite should say 10, then 9, then 8, and so on, one number each second, down to 1. Then it says Blast off! Test it two times in a row. Does it still start at 10 the second time? If not, you have a reset bug to fix.",answer:"when green flag clicked\nset [count v] to (10)\nrepeat (10)\nsay (count) for (1) seconds\nchange [count v] by (-1)\nend\nsay [Blast off!]"},checklist:["All four bugs are fixed and I tested each one","My score starts at 0 and adds only 1 point for each touch","My Notes and Credits has a bug report: expected, actual, and fix","I wrote what one script in someone else's project does"],submit:"Share your bug project and paste the link. Name your project anything you like (idea: Bug Hunt).",teacherNotes:"Pacing: toolkit 4 min, Bug #1 5, Bug #2 8, Bug #3 4, Bug #4 6, Code Reader 10, bug report 5. Bug swap is a stretch. Before class: make a class studio where students add their fork-1 projects (for Code Reader), and make a 'Mystery Bug' project with 3 small bugs (for the solo option). Buggy scripts appear in the step text so students build the bug themselves; the fix is in the step blocks (Bug #1 is a worked example; Bugs #3 and #4 are hidden behind 'Stuck? Show me'). Misconceptions: (1) students think the if 'keeps watching' by itself; it checks once unless it is in a loop. (2) Bug #2: many fix the reset but not the repeat points; have them watch the score monitor while the cat sits on the apple. (3) In Bug #4 Scratch makes 'start gmae' a separate message, so nothing is 'broken' inside either script; the names just do not match. (4) In Code Reader, students describe what a script LOOKS like ('it has a forever') instead of what it DOES; push for 'when ___ happens, the sprite ___'. Accommodations: the frames can be answered out loud or typed as short phrases; students who need less reading can do Bugs #1-#3 plus Code Reader and skip Bug #4. ELLs can label each block of their chosen script with one word in their home language before writing. Pair a strong reader with a student for Code Reader if needed, but each writes their own frame."},m2:{title:"My Blocks Workshop",tagline:"Invent your own blocks and draw with the Pen.",mission:"Make your own Scratch blocks and use them to draw.",minutes:"45",skill:"Custom Blocks, Inputs & Pen",goal:"By the end of this quest you will make custom blocks with Make a Block, give them inputs, draw shapes with the Pen, and use a custom block to clean up your branch project.",vocab:[{term:"Custom block",def:"A block you make. It runs a group of blocks you choose.",icon:"🧩"},{term:"define",def:"The hat block where you build what your custom block does.",icon:"🛠️"},{term:"Input",def:"A blank in your block. You can put a different value in it each time.",icon:"📥"},{term:"Pen",def:"Extra blocks that let a sprite draw lines on the Stage as it moves.",icon:"🖊️"},{term:"Abstraction",def:"Putting many blocks behind one simple name, so code is easier to read.",icon:"📦"}],warmup:"If you could invent ONE new Scratch block, what would it be called and what would it do? Write its name like a real block, for example: do a backflip. Do this alone or with a neighbor.",needs:[],steps:[{title:"Find My Blocks",short:"Click My Blocks, then Make a Block, and name it dance.",text:"Make a new project. Click the <b>My Blocks</b> category (pink, at the bottom of the list). Click <b>Make a Block</b>. Type the name <code>dance</code> and click <b>OK</b>. A <code>define dance</code> hat block appears in the code area, and a new <code>dance</code> block appears in My Blocks.",shots:["make-block"],tip:"Name your project anything you like. One idea: Block Workshop."},{title:"Define your block",short:"Put blocks under define dance, then use dance in a green flag script.",text:"The blocks under <code>define dance</code> are what your block does. Build the define script. Then build a green flag script that uses your new <code>dance</code> block two times. Click the green flag to test.",where:"Sprite1",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"define dance\nturn cw (15) degrees\nwait (0.2) seconds\nturn ccw (30) degrees\nwait (0.2) seconds\nturn cw (15) degrees\n\nwhen green flag clicked\ndance\nsay [Ta-da!] for (1) seconds\ndance",tip:"Custom blocks only work in the sprite where you made them. If you click a different sprite, dance is not there."},{title:"Add an input",short:"Give your dance block a number input called times.",text:"Right-click the <code>define dance</code> hat and choose <b>Edit</b>. Click <b>Add an input (number or text)</b>, type <code>times</code>, and click <b>OK</b>. Now the hat says <code>define dance (times)</code>.<br>Put the dance moves inside a <code>repeat</code>, and drag the pink <code>times</code> bubble into the repeat. In the green flag script, type a number into each dance block.",where:"Sprite1",change:"replace",changeNote:"Replaces both scripts from step 2: the define dance script and the green flag script.",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"define dance (times)\nrepeat (times)\nturn cw (15) degrees\nwait (0.2) seconds\nturn ccw (15) degrees\nwait (0.2) seconds\nend\n\nwhen green flag clicked\ndance (3)",shots:["make-block"],tip:"Drag the pink times bubble from the define hat itself. You cannot find it in the block list."},{title:"A drawing block",short:"Add the Pen extension and make a block that draws any size square.",text:"Click the <b>Add Extension</b> button at the bottom-left corner. Choose <b>Pen</b>. New green Pen blocks appear: <code>pen down</code> draws as the sprite moves, <code>pen up</code> stops drawing, and <code>erase all</code> clears the Stage.<br>Make a new block <code>square</code> with a number input called <code>size</code>. Then build a green flag script that draws three squares.",where:"Sprite1",change:"replace",changeNote:"Replaces your green flag script from step 3. Keep the define dance script.",scaffold:"full",blocks:"define square (size)\npen down\nrepeat (4)\nmove (size) steps\nturn cw (90) degrees\nend\npen up\n\nwhen green flag clicked\nerase all\nsquare (50)\nsquare (100)\nsquare (150)",shots:["extensions"],tip:"Lines left over from last time? That is why erase all goes at the top of the green flag script."},{title:"Turbo mode",short:"Turn on run without screen refresh so the squares draw fast.",text:"Right-click <code>define square (size)</code> and choose <b>Edit</b>. Check the box <b>Run without screen refresh</b> and click <b>OK</b>. Click the green flag. The squares appear all at once! This is good for drawing and for code that should happen in one moment.",shots:["make-block"],tip:"Do not use Run without screen refresh on a block that has a forever or a wait inside. Scratch can freeze. If it freezes, click the red stop sign."},{title:"A block that uses a block",short:"Make a row block that draws many squares in a line.",text:"Make a new block called <code>row</code> with a number input called <code>count</code>. When you use <code>row (5)</code>, the sprite should draw 5 small squares next to each other, in a line from left to right.<br>Ideas: your <code>square</code> block can go INSIDE the define row script. Use a loop from <b>Control</b>. Move the sprite after each square so they do not draw on top of each other. In the green flag script, use <b>erase all</b>, move the sprite to the left side, point it to the right, and then use your row block.",where:"Sprite1",change:"replace",changeNote:"Replaces your green flag script from step 4. Keep define dance and define square.",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"define row (count)\nrepeat (count)\nsquare (40) :: custom\nmove (50) steps\nend\n\nwhen green flag clicked\nerase all\ngo to x: (-200) y: (0)\npoint in direction (90)\nrow (5)",tip:"Test small: click row with a 2 typed in it before you add it to the green flag script."},{title:"Clean up your branch project",short:"Turn repeated blocks in your branch project into one custom block.",text:"Open your project from your first branch. Look for a group of blocks you use two or more times. Turn it into ONE custom block with a clear name. Then replace each copy with your block.<br><b>Games idea:</b> <code>reset player</code> (go to the start spot, set lives, show).<br><b>Stories idea:</b> <code>enter from left</code> (go to the left edge, show, glide in).<br><b>Apps idea:</b> <code>correct answer</code> (play a sound, say Correct!, change score by 1).<br>Click the green flag. The project should work the same as before, but the code is shorter and easier to read.",where:"The sprite in your branch project that repeats blocks",change:"replace",changeNote:"Replaces each repeated group of blocks with one call to your new custom block. The example shows a Stories version.",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"define enter from left\nhide\ngo to x: (-240) y: (-100)\nshow\nglide (1) secs to x: (-100) y: (-100)\n\nwhen green flag clicked\nenter from left\nsay [Hello!] for (2) seconds\n\nwhen I receive [scene 2 v]\nenter from left\nsay [I am back!] for (2) seconds",tip:"Make the block in the SAME sprite as the repeated code. If two sprites repeat the same blocks, each sprite needs its own copy of the block. No branch project? Use your bug project from the last quest."},{title:"Save and share",short:"Save and share both projects.",text:"Click the green flag in each project one more time to test. Save and share your workshop project and your updated branch project. Paste the workshop link."}],quiz:[{q:"Trace this code. The sprite starts at y: 0. What does it say at the end?",blocks:"define jump (height)\nchange y by (height)\nwait (0.3) seconds\nchange y by ((0) - (height))\n\nwhen green flag clicked\ngo to x: (0) y: (0)\njump (50)\nsay (y position)",choices:["50","100","-50","0"],answer:3,explain:"jump (50) moves up 50, waits, then moves down 50. The sprite is back at y 0."},{q:"You made a custom block on the Cat sprite. You click the Dog sprite and the block is gone. Why?",choices:["The block was deleted","Custom blocks only work in the sprite where you made them","You must save first","The Pen extension is off"],answer:1,explain:"Each sprite has its own custom blocks. Make the block again in the Dog sprite."}],challenge:{title:"Polygon machine",short:"Make one block that can draw a shape with any number of sides.",text:"Make a block called polygon with two number inputs: sides and size. polygon with 3 sides draws a triangle, 5 draws a pentagon, 6 draws a hexagon. Every side has the same length. Hint: to get back to the start, the sprite turns 360 degrees in total.",answer:"define polygon (sides) (size)\npen down\nrepeat (sides)\nmove (size) steps\nturn cw ((360) / (sides)) degrees\nend\npen up"},checklist:["My workshop project has at least two custom blocks","One of my blocks has an input that changes what it does","My square block draws with the Pen","My branch project uses a custom block instead of repeated code"],submit:"Share your workshop project and paste the link. Name your project anything you like (idea: Block Workshop).",teacherNotes:"Pacing: Make a Block 3 min, define 5, input 7, Pen square 8, turbo 3, row goal 8, branch cleanup 9, share 2. Pen is introduced here for everyone (Apps fork 2 builds on it). Misconceptions: (1) students drag an input from somewhere other than the define hat; it must come from the hat. (2) custom blocks only exist in the sprite where they were made; the Stage and other sprites do not see them. (3) Run without screen refresh with a forever or wait inside can freeze the editor; the red stop sign fixes it. (4) Students expect editing the define to change behavior without clicking the green flag again. In branch cleanup, Games students may find repeated code in clone scripts; that is fine, but the custom block must be made in that same sprite. Accommodations: students who need less reading can follow the screenshots for steps 1, 3 and 5; the row goal can be reduced to 'use square three times in one green flag script'. ELLs: model 'define' as 'teach Scratch a new word'. For students with motor needs, drawing is done by code, not the mouse, so no fine control is needed."},s1:{title:"Talking Characters",tagline:"Bring two characters to life and make them have a real conversation.",mission:"Make two characters talk and take turns at the right time.",minutes:"45",skill:"Dialogue Timing with Broadcasts",goal:"By the end of this quest you will write a short conversation and use broadcast or broadcast and wait so two characters take turns without talking over each other.",vocab:[{term:"Dialogue",def:"The words characters say to each other in a story.",icon:"💬"},{term:"Cue",def:"A signal that tells a character it is their turn to talk.",icon:"🎬"},{term:"broadcast",def:"Sends a message to all sprites, then keeps going right away.",icon:"📣"},{term:"broadcast and wait",def:"Sends a message, then waits until every sprite that got it is finished.",icon:"⏳"},{term:"Overlap",def:"Two characters talking at the same time, so the story is hard to follow.",icon:"🔀"}],warmup:"Pick two characters who would never meet, like a pirate and a robot, or a dragon and a dentist. What is the first thing one would say to the other? Say it out loud or write it down. You can do this alone or with a neighbor.",needs:[],project:{name:"Animated Story",part:"Part 1 of 2"},steps:[{title:"Cast your characters",short:"Start a new project. Add two characters and a backdrop.",text:"Start a new project. Add two character sprites and one backdrop. Rename each sprite to its character name: click the sprite, then type in the <b>Sprite</b> name box. Drag them so they face each other. In this quest we call them <b>Character A</b> (talks first) and <b>Character B</b>.",shots:["choose-sprite","backdrops"],tip:"Is a character facing the wrong way? Go to its <b>Costumes</b> tab and click the flip horizontal button. That is easier than turning the sprite."},{title:"Write your script",short:"Write six short lines of dialogue before you code.",text:"A movie starts with a script. Write 6 lines: who says what, in order. A says lines 1, 3 and 5. B says lines 2, 4 and 6. Keep each line short so it fits in a speech bubble. Write it in a doc so you can copy and paste each line into Scratch later.<br>Example:<br>A: Hi! Who are you?<br>B: I am a robot from the future.<br>A: Whoa! Why are you here?<br>B: I came to find the last slice of pizza.<br>A: I ate it this morning.<br>B: Oh no!",frame:"My characters: A is ____. B is ____.\nWhere are they? ____\n\nLine 1  A: ____\nLine 2  B: ____\nLine 3  A: ____\nLine 4  B: ____\nLine 5  A: ____\nLine 6  B: ____",tip:"Short lines are better. Try to keep each line under 10 words. You may use the example lines and change just a few words."},{title:"Try timing with wait",short:"Use wait blocks so B answers after A finishes talking.",text:"First, try the simple way. Each character starts on the green flag. B waits 2 seconds while A talks, then answers. Put the <b>top script on Character A</b> and the <b>bottom script on Character B</b>. Use your own lines and your own x and y numbers. Click the green flag to test.",where:"Character A (top script) and Character B (bottom script)",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (-120) y: (-60)\nsay [Hi! Who are you?] for (2) seconds\nwait (2) seconds\nsay [Whoa! Why are you here?] for (2) seconds\n\nwhen green flag clicked\ngo to x: (120) y: (-60)\nwait (2) seconds\nsay [I am a robot from the future.] for (2) seconds",tip:"Now change line 1 to say for <b>4</b> seconds and click the green flag. B talks on top of A! With wait, every time you change one line, you must fix the wait times too. Broadcasts fix this. Change it back to 2."},{title:"Choose your timing method",short:"Pick relay or leader. Make Character A send B a cue.",text:"Instead of guessing the time, A will send B a <b>cue</b> (a broadcast message). Pick one way to do it. Both ways work.<br><b>Relay</b>: each character sends the next cue, like passing a ball.<br><b>Leader</b>: A uses <b>broadcast and wait</b>, so A waits until B is done, then says the next line.<br>To make a message, open the broadcast dropdown and choose <b>New message</b>. Name it after the line it starts, like <b>line 2</b>.",where:"Character A",change:"replace",changeNote:"Replaces Character A's green flag script from step 3.",scaffold:"full",options:[{label:"Relay: broadcast",short:"A says line 1, sends line 2, then waits for line 3.",blocks:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (-120) y: (-60)\nsay [Hi! Who are you?] for (2) seconds\nbroadcast (line 2 v)\n\nwhen I receive [line 3 v]\nsay [Whoa! Why are you here?] for (2) seconds\nbroadcast (line 4 v)"},{label:"Leader: broadcast and wait",short:"A keeps the whole order in one script and waits for B each time.",blocks:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (-120) y: (-60)\nsay [Hi! Who are you?] for (2) seconds\nbroadcast (line 2 v) and wait\nsay [Whoa! Why are you here?] for (2) seconds\nbroadcast (line 4 v) and wait"}],shots:["cat-events"],tip:"Choose the message from the dropdown every time. If you type a new message with a different spelling, like <b>Line 2</b> and <b>line 2</b>, you get two different messages."},{title:"Character B answers",short:"Pick the same method as before. Make B answer its cue.",text:"Now B listens for its cue with <b>when I receive</b>. Pick the same tab you picked in step 4. Put the blocks together, then click the green flag. You should hear three lines in the right order.<br><b>Relay</b>: B must send the next cue back to A.<br><b>Leader</b>: B does not send anything. A is already waiting for B to finish.",where:"Character B",change:"replace",changeNote:"Replaces Character B's green flag script from step 3. Keep go to x: y:, but delete the wait and say blocks.",scaffold:"parsons",options:[{label:"Relay: broadcast",short:"B answers line 2, then sends the line 3 cue back to A.",blocks:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (120) y: (-60)\n\nwhen I receive [line 2 v]\nsay [I am a robot from the future.] for (2) seconds\nbroadcast (line 3 v)"},{label:"Leader: broadcast and wait",short:"B answers line 2. A waits until B is finished.",blocks:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (120) y: (-60)\n\nwhen I receive [line 2 v]\nsay [I am a robot from the future.] for (2) seconds"}],tip:"Test the overlap again: change B's line to 4 seconds. Nobody talks on top of anyone now, because the next line waits for the cue."},{title:"Finish the conversation",short:"Add lines four to six yourself, using the same method.",text:"Build the rest of your 6 lines on your own. Use the same method you picked. You already know every block you need: <b>when I receive</b>, <b>say for seconds</b>, and <b>broadcast</b> or <b>broadcast and wait</b>.<br><b>Relay</b>: every line is a when I receive script that ends by sending the next cue.<br><b>Leader</b>: add more say and broadcast and wait blocks to the bottom of A's green flag script, and give B a when I receive script for each of its lines.<br>Test after each new line.",where:"Character A and Character B",change:"add",changeNote:"Adds new when I receive scripts. Leader method: also adds blocks to the bottom of Character A's green flag script.",scaffold:"goal",options:[{label:"Relay: broadcast",short:"B gets line 4, A gets line 5, B gets line 6.",blocks:"when I receive [line 4 v]\nsay [I came to find the last slice of pizza.] for (2) seconds\nbroadcast (line 5 v)\n\nwhen I receive [line 5 v]\nsay [I ate it this morning.] for (2) seconds\nbroadcast (line 6 v)\n\nwhen I receive [line 6 v]\nsay [Oh no!] for (2) seconds"},{label:"Leader: broadcast and wait",short:"A's script grows. B gets one when I receive script per line.",blocks:"say [I ate it this morning.] for (2) seconds\nbroadcast (line 6 v) and wait\n\nwhen I receive [line 4 v]\nsay [I came to find the last slice of pizza.] for (2) seconds\n\nwhen I receive [line 6 v]\nsay [Oh no!] for (2) seconds"}],tip:"Relay method: the line 5 script goes on Character A. The line 4 and line 6 scripts go on Character B. Leader method: the two loose blocks go at the bottom of A's green flag script."},{title:"Add personality",short:"Add a thought, a costume change, or a sound to one line.",text:"Make your characters feel alive. Use <b>think for seconds</b> for a secret thought, <b>next costume</b> when a character gets excited, or <b>start sound</b> on a funny line. Add them inside any when I receive script.",where:"Either character",change:"add",changeNote:"Add these blocks inside one of your when I receive scripts, before or after its say block.",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when I receive [line 4 v]\nthink [Hmm... should I tell them?] for (2) seconds\nnext costume\nsay [I came to find the last slice of pizza.] for (2) seconds",stretch:!0,tip:"If you change costumes, add <b>switch costume to</b> under the green flag too, so the character looks normal every time the story starts."},{title:"Watch, fix, and share",short:"Watch the whole conversation twice. Fix overlaps, then save and share.",text:"Click the green flag and watch with your eyes on the Stage only. Do the lines come in the right order? Does anyone talk at the same time? Does a bubble stay up forever? Click the green flag a second time to check that the characters go back to their places. Then save and share.",tip:"A speech bubble that never goes away means you used <b>say</b> instead of <b>say for seconds</b>. A line that never comes means a message name does not match, or nobody sends that message."}],quiz:[{q:"Character A runs the top script. Character B runs the bottom one. When does A say Done?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nbroadcast (line 2 v) and wait\nsay [Done!] for (2) seconds\n\nwhen I receive [line 2 v]\nsay [Hello!] for (3) seconds",choices:["Right away, at the same time B says Hello","After about 3 seconds, when B is finished","Never","Before B says anything"],answer:1,explain:"broadcast and wait waits until B's when I receive script is finished, so A speaks after B's 3 seconds."},{q:"Now A uses plain broadcast. What happens when you click the green flag?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nbroadcast (line 2 v)\nsay [Done!] for (2) seconds\n\nwhen I receive [line 2 v]\nsay [Hello!] for (3) seconds",choices:["A says Done after B finishes","Only B talks","A and B talk at the same time","Nothing happens"],answer:2,explain:"Plain broadcast does not wait. A keeps going right away, so both bubbles appear at once."}],challenge:{title:"Third character",short:"Add a third character who jumps into the middle of the conversation.",text:"Add a third sprite who is hidden at the start. In the middle of the conversation, it appears, says one surprising line, and hides again. The other characters must wait for it and then keep talking. The story must still reset when you click the green flag again.",answer:"when green flag clicked\nhide\n\nwhen I receive [surprise v]\nshow\nsay [Wait! Nobody move!] for (2) seconds\nhide\n\nwhen I receive [line 4 v]\nbroadcast (surprise v) and wait\nsay [I came to find the last slice of pizza.] for (2) seconds"},checklist:["Two named characters and a backdrop","At least 6 lines of dialogue","Turns use broadcast or broadcast and wait, not only wait blocks","No lines overlap when I watch it","Characters go back to their start places on the green flag"],submit:'Share your conversation with at least 6 lines. Name your project anything you like (idea: "Pirate Meets Robot"). Paste the link. You will add more scenes to this same project next quest.',teacherNotes:"Pacing (45 min): warmup 3, cast 5, script writing 7, wait method 5, choose method 7, B answers 5, finish 8, watch and share 5. The personality step is a stretch.<br><b>Key idea</b>: plain broadcast starts other scripts and keeps going; broadcast and wait pauses until every receiving script ends. The step 3 tip (change a line to 4 seconds) makes students feel why wait timing breaks. Students pick Relay or Leader; both are correct. Leader keeps the whole order in one place and is easier to read; Relay shows clearly how cues pass. Students must pick the same tab in steps 4 to 6 (the app remembers each step's tab separately).<br><b>Common misconceptions</b>: say without seconds stays up forever and looks like overlap; making two messages with different spellings; in Leader, B also broadcasting the next line (it then runs twice); thinking broadcast and wait waits for the sprite to say something, when it waits for the whole receiving script.<br><b>Accommodations</b>: this lesson is typing-heavy. Let students write lines in a doc and copy and paste into say blocks, use speech-to-text in the doc, or use the example dialogue and change only names and a few words. Students with writing goals may do 4 lines instead of 6. English learners may write lines in their home language (Scratch shows any language in speech bubbles) plus one English line. Read-aloud covers the step cards; pair the frame with a quick verbal rehearsal."},s2:{title:"Scene Changer",tagline:"Take your story on a journey: new places, new action.",mission:"Turn your story into three scenes that change by themselves.",minutes:"45",skill:"Backdrops, Scenes and Show or Hide",goal:"By the end of this quest you will build a story with three scenes, use messages to change the backdrop, start action with when backdrop switches to, and make characters enter and exit with show and hide.",vocab:[{term:"Scene",def:"One part of a story in one place. In Scratch, often one backdrop.",icon:"🎞️"},{term:"Backdrop",def:"The background picture on the Stage.",icon:"🏞️"},{term:"Enter and exit",def:"When a character comes into a scene or leaves it.",icon:"🚪"},{term:"Plot",def:"What happens in a story: the beginning, the middle, and the end.",icon:"🗺️"},{term:"Reset",def:"Putting everything back the way it was at the start of the story.",icon:"🔄"}],warmup:"Think of a movie or book you like. Name three places where it happens. How do you know the story moved to a new place? Think alone or share with a neighbor.",needs:[],starter:{name:"Animated Story starter",contains:"Two character sprites named Character A and Character B, one backdrop named scene1, and a 6-line conversation that uses broadcast messages line 2 to line 6. The last line (line 6) is said by Character B in a when I receive [line 6] script. Each character has a green flag script with go to x: y:."},project:{name:"Animated Story",part:"Part 2 of 2"},steps:[{title:"Plan three scenes",short:"Open your story and plan three scenes with the frame.",text:"Open your Animated Story from last quest (or the starter project). A good story has a beginning, a middle, and an end. <b>Scene 1</b> is the conversation you already have. In <b>Scene 2</b> a problem happens somewhere new. In <b>Scene 3</b> the problem is solved. Each new scene only needs 2 to 4 short lines.",frame:"Scene 1 (backdrop scene1): the conversation I already have.\n  Last line of Scene 1: ____\n\nScene 2 (backdrop scene2): Where are we? ____\n  Who is here? ____\n  What problem happens? ____\n  Lines: ____ / ____\n\nScene 3 (backdrop scene3): Where are we? ____\n  How is the problem solved? ____\n  Last line of the story: ____",tip:"Keep it small. Three scenes with a few lines each is a full story. You can add more scenes later if you have time."},{title:"Add and name backdrops",short:"Add two backdrops. Name them scene1, scene2, and scene3.",text:"Click the <b>Stage</b> in the bottom right corner, then the <b>Backdrops</b> tab. Add two more backdrops (choose one or paint your own). Rename all three to <b>scene1</b>, <b>scene2</b>, and <b>scene3</b> in the name box, so they are easy to find in dropdowns.",shots:["select-stage","backdrops"],tip:"Delete the plain white backdrop1 if you do not use it, so it does not show up by mistake."},{title:"Make the Stage the director",short:"On the Stage, start at scene 1 and change scenes when messages arrive.",text:"The Stage will change scenes. On the green flag it always goes back to scene1. When it receives <b>end scene 1</b>, it switches to scene2. When it receives <b>end scene 2</b>, it switches to scene3. Make the two new messages in the dropdown. Nobody sends them yet, so the next steps will do that.",where:"Stage",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nswitch backdrop to (scene1 v)\n\nwhen I receive [end scene 1 v]\nswitch backdrop to (scene2 v)\n\nwhen I receive [end scene 2 v]\nswitch backdrop to (scene3 v)",shots:["select-stage","cat-events"],tip:"A when I receive script only runs if some sprite sends that message. If a scene never changes, check that something broadcasts it."},{title:"Send the end of Scene 1",short:"After your last Scene 1 line, broadcast end scene 1.",text:"Find the script that says the <b>last line</b> of your conversation. Add <b>broadcast (end scene 1)</b> at the very bottom. Click the green flag: after the last line, the backdrop should change to scene2.",where:"The character who says the last line of Scene 1",change:"add",changeNote:"Add the broadcast block at the bottom of the script that says your last Scene 1 line (in the starter, Character B's when I receive [line 6] script).",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when I receive [line 6 v]\nsay [Oh no!] for (2) seconds\nbroadcast (end scene 1 v)",tip:"Pick end scene 1 from the dropdown. Do not make a new message with a different name."},{title:"Enter and exit",short:"Hide characters on the green flag. Show them when their scene starts.",text:"Not every character is in every scene. A character who first appears in Scene 2 must <b>hide</b> on the green flag, then <b>show</b> when the backdrop switches to scene2. A character who is in Scene 1 must <b>show</b> on the green flag, so it is back after the last run. Use <b>when backdrop switches to</b> from Events. You can add a new sprite for Scene 2.",where:"A character who enters in Scene 2",change:"add",changeNote:"Put hide and go to at the top of this character's green flag script (make one if it has none). The backdrop script is a new script.",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nhide\ngo to x: (-250) y: (-80)\n\nwhen backdrop switches to [scene2 v]\nshow\nglide (2) secs to x: (-80) y: (-80)\nsay [Where are we now?] for (2) seconds",shots:["cat-events"],tip:"Every character needs show or hide in its green flag script. If a character hides in Scene 3, it will still be hidden next time unless the green flag shows it again."},{title:"Build Scene 2",short:"Make a short Scene 2 conversation that ends with broadcast end scene 2.",text:"When the backdrop switches to scene2, your characters talk about the problem. Use broadcasts for turns, like last quest. The last line of Scene 2 must send <b>end scene 2</b>, so the Stage moves on to scene3. Put the blocks in order. The other character needs a when I receive [scene 2 line 2] script with its line.",where:"One character in Scene 2",change:"new",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when backdrop switches to [scene2 v]\nwait (2) seconds\nsay [Oh no! The map is gone!] for (2) seconds\nbroadcast (scene 2 line 2 v) and wait\nsay [We have to find it.] for (2) seconds\nbroadcast (end scene 2 v)",tip:"The wait at the top gives the entering character time to glide in first."},{title:"Build Scene 3 and the end",short:"Build Scene 3 yourself. End with a final line, a sound, and stop.",text:"Build Scene 3 on your own. When the backdrop switches to scene3, your characters solve the problem in 2 to 4 lines. After the last line, broadcast a new message <b>the end</b>. Something must receive it: play a happy sound and then stop the story. Other characters can also receive <b>the end</b> to cheer or change costume.<br>Blocks that help: when backdrop switches to, say for seconds, broadcast and wait, when I receive, play sound until done, stop all.",where:"The character who says the last line",change:"new",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"when backdrop switches to [scene3 v]\nsay [We found the map!] for (2) seconds\nbroadcast (scene 3 line 2 v) and wait\nsay [And that is how we saved the day!] for (3) seconds\nbroadcast (the end v) and wait\nstop [all v]\n\nwhen I receive [the end v]\nplay sound (Magic Spell v) until done",shots:["sounds-tab"],tip:"Use play sound until done, not start sound, before stop all. Otherwise the story stops before you hear the sound. Pick any sound from the Sounds tab."},{title:"Watch twice, fix, share",short:"Watch the whole story two times in a row. Fix resets, then share.",text:"Click the green flag and watch the whole story. Then click it <b>again</b>. The second run shows reset bugs: a character still hidden, in the wrong place, or the wrong backdrop. Fix them, then save and share.",tip:"Character in the wrong place at the start? Add go to x: y: and show or hide to its green flag script. Story stuck on one scene? A message is received but never sent."}],quiz:[{q:"This is the Dragon's code. The story goes scene1, then scene2, then scene3. When can you see the Dragon?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nhide\n\nwhen backdrop switches to [scene3 v]\nshow",choices:["In every scene","Only in scene 1","Never","Only in scene 3"],answer:3,explain:"It hides when the story starts and only shows when the backdrop switches to scene3."},{q:"This is on the Stage. No sprite ever broadcasts end scene 1. What happens?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nswitch backdrop to (scene1 v)\n\nwhen I receive [end scene 1 v]\nswitch backdrop to (scene2 v)",choices:["The story moves to scene2 after 2 seconds","The story stays on scene1","Scratch shows an error","It skips to scene3"],answer:1,explain:"A when I receive script waits for its message. If nobody sends it, the script never runs."}],challenge:{title:"Title screen and credits",short:"Add a title screen before Scene 1 and a credits screen at the end.",text:"Add a backdrop named <b>title</b> with your story's name and the words Press space to start. Add a backdrop named <b>credits</b> that lists you as the director. On the green flag, show the title and wait until the space key is pressed, then go to scene1. Scene 1 must now start on when backdrop switches to scene1 instead of the green flag. At the end, show the credits.",answer:"when green flag clicked\nswitch backdrop to (title v)\nwait until <key (space v) pressed?>\nswitch backdrop to (scene1 v)\n\nwhen I receive [the end v]\nswitch backdrop to (credits v)"},checklist:["Three backdrops named scene1, scene2, and scene3","The story moves to each scene by itself","Every character shows or hides on the green flag","An ending with a last line and a sound","It plays correctly two times in a row"],submit:'Share your finished story with three scenes. Name your project anything you like (idea: "The Lost Pizza Map"). Paste the link.',teacherNotes:"Pacing (45 min): warmup 3, plan 6, backdrops 4, Stage director 5, send end scene 1 3, enter and exit 7, Scene 2 7, Scene 3 7, watch twice 3. Absent students start from the starter project.<br><b>Message map</b> (every received message is sent): end scene 1 is sent by the last Scene 1 line (step 4); end scene 2 by the last Scene 2 line (step 6); scene 2 line 2 and scene 3 line 2 are sent by the speaking character and received by the other one; the end is sent and received in step 7.<br><b>Common misconceptions</b>: thinking when I receive runs by itself; missing show on the green flag (characters stay hidden on the second run); using start sound before stop all (sound is cut off); switching backdrops in many sprites at once instead of letting the Stage direct. Note: switch backdrop to also triggers when backdrop switches to, even if it is the same backdrop.<br><b>Accommodations</b>: typing-heavy. Scenes 2 and 3 need only 2 lines each for students with writing goals; allow speech-to-text in a doc and copy and paste; the frame can be filled with single words. English learners may write lines in their home language. Students who struggle with the message map can draw arrows on paper from each broadcast to its when I receive."},s3:{title:"Choose Your Path",tagline:"Your reader is the hero, and the reader decides what happens next.",mission:"Write a story where the reader chooses what happens next.",minutes:"45",skill:"Branching Stories and Choices",goal:"By the end of this quest you will plan a story map, use a scene variable and choice buttons, and use broadcasts to send the story down different paths to at least three endings.",vocab:[{term:"Branching story",def:"A story that splits into different paths based on the reader's choices.",icon:"🌳"},{term:"Story map",def:"A drawing of every scene and the choices that connect them.",icon:"🗺️"},{term:"Choice point",def:"A moment where the reader picks what happens next.",icon:"🔀"},{term:"Ending",def:"One way the story can finish. Branching stories have more than one.",icon:"🏁"},{term:"scene variable",def:"A variable that remembers which scene the story is in right now.",icon:"📍"}],warmup:"You find a strange door in the school library. Choice A: open it. Choice B: go get help. What happens next for each choice? Think alone or tell a neighbor.",needs:["textStrings","askAnswer"],project:{name:"Interactive Adventure",part:"Part 1 of 2"},steps:[{title:"Draw your story map",short:"Plan a small story map with two choices and three endings.",text:"On paper or in a doc, draw a box for each scene and an arrow for each choice. Keep it small: <b>5 to 7 scenes</b>, at least <b>2 choice points</b>, and at least <b>3 endings</b>. Give every box a short one-word name, like <b>cave</b>. You will use these exact names for backdrops and messages.<br>Example map: <b>start</b>: A goes to <b>cave</b>, B goes to <b>forest</b> (an ending). <b>cave</b>: A goes to <b>treasure</b> (an ending), B goes to <b>home</b> (an ending).",frame:"START scene name: ____\n  What happens: ____\n  Choice A: ____  goes to scene ____\n  Choice B: ____  goes to scene ____\n\nScene name: ____\n  What happens: ____\n  Choice A: ____  goes to scene ____\n  Choice B: ____  goes to scene ____\n\nEnding 1 name: ____  What happens: ____\nEnding 2 name: ____  What happens: ____\nEnding 3 name: ____  What happens: ____",tip:"Story maps grow very fast. Two choices in every scene means many scenes. Let some choices go straight to an ending."},{title:"Set up the scenes",short:"Start a new project. Add one backdrop for each box on your map.",text:"Start a new project. Click the Stage, open the <b>Backdrops</b> tab, and add one backdrop for each box on your map. Rename each backdrop to match your map exactly, like <b>start</b>, <b>cave</b>, <b>forest</b>. Add one sprite to be the <b>Narrator</b> and rename it.",shots:["select-stage","backdrops","choose-sprite"],tip:"Use the same names everywhere: backdrop, message, and scene variable. cave and Cave will match, but cave and the cave will not."},{title:"Choice buttons and the scene variable",short:"Paint a Choice A button. Make a scene variable for all sprites.",text:"Paint a new sprite: a rounded rectangle with the letter <b>A</b> on it. Name it <b>Choice A</b>. Make a variable named <b>scene</b> (For all sprites), so every sprite knows where the story is. Add this code so the button hides until a choice point. Then right-click Choice A and pick <b>duplicate</b>. Rename the copy <b>Choice B</b> and change its letter.",where:"Choice A (then duplicate it to make Choice B)",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nhide\n\nwhen I receive [show choices v]\nshow\n\nwhen I receive [hide choices v]\nhide",shots:["choose-sprite","make-variable"],tip:"Variables can hold words, not just numbers. The scene variable will hold names like start or cave. Uncheck its box if you do not want it on the Stage."},{title:"The opening scene",short:"Make the Narrator tell the opening and show the choices.",text:"On the green flag, the Narrator goes to the start backdrop, sets <b>scene</b> to <b>start</b>, tells the opening, and shows the buttons. The say block here has <b>no seconds</b>, so the words stay on screen while the reader decides.",where:"Narrator",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nswitch backdrop to (start v)\nset [scene v] to [start]\nsay [You find a glowing door. A: open it. B: walk into the forest.]\nbroadcast (show choices v)",tip:"Type start into the set block exactly as you named it. The buttons will compare against these words."},{title:"Wire the buttons",short:"Make Choice A check the scene and send the right message.",text:"One button is used at every choice point, so it must check which scene it is in. Put the blocks together on <b>Choice A</b>. Then build the same kind of script on <b>Choice B</b>, with B's messages from your map (in the example: start goes to forest, cave goes to home).",where:"Choice A",change:"new",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when this sprite clicked\nbroadcast (hide choices v)\nif <(scene) = [start]> then\nbroadcast (go cave v)\nend\nif <(scene) = [cave]> then\nbroadcast (go treasure v)\nend",tip:"Use plain broadcast here, not broadcast and wait. With and wait, the scene changes to cave before the second if checks, and the story jumps two scenes."},{title:"Build each path",short:"For each message, switch the backdrop, set scene, and tell that part.",text:"Now build every scene on your map on the Narrator. For each go message: switch to that backdrop, set <b>scene</b> to its name, and say what happens. A scene with a choice ends by showing the choices. An ending does <b>not</b> show the choices: it says THE END instead.<br>Blocks that help: when I receive, switch backdrop to, set scene to, say, broadcast.",where:"Narrator",change:"new",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"when I receive [go cave v]\nswitch backdrop to (cave v)\nset [scene v] to [cave]\nsay [It is dark. You hear a growl. A: light a torch. B: run home.]\nbroadcast (show choices v)\n\nwhen I receive [go treasure v]\nswitch backdrop to (treasure v)\nset [scene v] to [treasure]\nsay [You found the treasure! THE END]",tip:"Build one scene, test it, then build the next. Every message a button sends needs one when I receive script on the Narrator."},{title:"Test every path",short:"Play the story once for each ending. Fix any path that gets stuck.",text:"Click the green flag and play until you reach an ending. Do it again for <b>every</b> ending on your map. Also click the green flag in the middle of a story: it should go back to the start with the buttons hidden. Write down each test. Save and share.",frame:"Path: start, then ____, then ____\nEnding I reached: ____\nDid it work? yes / no\nWhat went wrong: ____\nHow I fixed it: ____",tip:"A button that does nothing usually means the scene name in the if block does not match the set block. = ignores capital letters, but not extra spaces: cave with a space at the end is not cave."}],quiz:[{q:"The scene variable is cave. The reader clicks Choice A. Which message does it send?",blocks:"when this sprite clicked\nbroadcast (hide choices v)\nif <(scene) = [start]> then\nbroadcast (go cave v)\nend\nif <(scene) = [cave]> then\nbroadcast (go treasure v)\nend",choices:["go cave","go treasure","Both go cave and go treasure","No go message at all"],answer:1,explain:"scene is cave, so the first if is false and the second if is true. It sends hide choices and go treasure."},{q:"Your map has 3 choice points with 2 choices each, and every path splits. What keeps the story small enough to finish?",choices:["Add more choices to each scene","Use only one backdrop for everything","Delete the story map","Let some choices go straight to an ending or join another path"],answer:3,explain:"Paths that end early or join together keep the number of scenes small."}],challenge:{title:"Typed password",short:"At one choice point, the reader types a password instead of clicking.",text:"Add a scene where a guard asks the reader for a password. Do not show the buttons in this scene. If the reader types the right password, go to one scene. Any other answer goes to a different scene. Use ask and wait and the answer block from Sensing. Test the right password, a wrong one, and the right one in capital letters.",answer:"when I receive [go gate v]\nswitch backdrop to (gate v)\nset [scene v] to [gate]\nask [The guard says: What is the password?] and wait\nif <(answer) = [dragonfruit]> then\nbroadcast (go castle v)\nelse\nbroadcast (go dungeon v)\nend"},checklist:["A story map with at least 3 endings","At least 2 choice points with buttons that show and hide","Every button click goes to the right scene","Every path reaches an ending, and I tested each one","The green flag always starts the story from the beginning"],submit:'Share your adventure with at least 2 choice points and 3 endings. Name your project anything you like (idea: "The Glowing Door"). Paste the link. Next quest you will add sound and mood.',teacherNotes:"Pacing (45 min): warmup 3, story map 8, backdrops 5, buttons 5, opening 4, wire buttons 6, build paths 10, test 4. Students who need more time can finish paths at the start of s4.<br>Students arrive from Games, Stories, or Apps at fork 1, plus m1 and m2. Games students may not have used variables to hold words (textStrings primer). The challenge uses ask and answer, so Games and Stories students see the askAnswer primer.<br><b>Common misconceptions</b>: the same button means different things in different scenes (that is why it checks scene); using broadcast and wait in the button, which makes the story jump two scenes; a misspelled scene name in set vs. if; forgetting that an ending must not show the buttons; a say block with seconds makes the question disappear before the reader picks.<br><b>Accommodations</b>: writing-heavy. Each scene needs only one sentence plus the choices. Students may use the example map (start, cave, forest, treasure, home) and only write their own sentences. Allow speech-to-text in a doc for scene text, then copy and paste. For IEP writing goals, 1 choice point and 2 endings is a fair core. English learners may write scene text in their home language. Keep the paper map on the desk: it is the best debugging tool for this lesson."},s4:{title:"Sound and Atmosphere",tagline:"Make your audience feel the story with music, sound, and effects.",mission:"Add music, sound effects, and mood to your adventure.",minutes:"45",skill:"Sound, Music and Visual Effects",goal:"By the end of this quest you will give each scene its own music that changes cleanly, add a voice line or sound effect, fade a character in, and use graphic effects to set the mood.",vocab:[{term:"Atmosphere",def:"The mood or feeling of a scene, like spooky, happy, or tense.",icon:"🌫️"},{term:"Sound effect",def:"A short sound for an action, like a door creak or a splash.",icon:"🔊"},{term:"Music loop",def:"A song that plays again and again in the background.",icon:"🔁"},{term:"Fade",def:"Slowly appearing or disappearing, made with the ghost effect.",icon:"👻"},{term:"stop other scripts in sprite",def:"Stops every other script in this sprite, but keeps this one running.",icon:"✋"}],warmup:"Close your eyes. Imagine a spooky scene. What sounds do you hear? Now imagine a beach. How do the sounds change the mood? Think alone or share with a neighbor.",needs:[],starter:{name:"Interactive Adventure starter",contains:"Backdrops named start, cave, forest, treasure, and home; a Narrator sprite; Choice A and Choice B button sprites that hide on the green flag and show or hide on the messages show choices and hide choices; a scene variable (For all sprites); and Narrator scripts for the messages go cave, go forest, go treasure, and go home. forest, treasure, and home are endings."},project:{name:"Interactive Adventure",part:"Part 2 of 2"},steps:[{title:"Plan the mood",short:"Open your adventure. Pick a mood and sounds for each scene.",text:"Open your Interactive Adventure from last quest (or the starter project). For each scene on your story map, choose a mood, a music loop, and maybe one sound effect or visual effect. You will not have time for everything, so start with the start scene and one ending.",frame:"Scene ____ feels ____.\n  Music: ____\n  Sound effect: ____\n  Visual effect: darker / colorful / ghost fade / none\n\nScene ____ feels ____.\n  Music: ____\n  Sound effect: ____\n  Visual effect: ____",tip:"Use headphones if you have them, so you can hear your own project."},{title:"Make a Music sprite",short:"Add a hidden Music sprite that plays a loop in the start scene.",text:"All music will live in one sprite, so it is easy to control. Add any sprite and rename it <b>Music</b>. In its <b>Sounds</b> tab, click Choose a Sound and pick a song from the <b>Loops</b> category. Add this code. When the backdrop switches to start, it first stops any other song script in this sprite and any sound that is playing, then plays the loop forever.",where:"Music (new sprite)",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nhide\n\nwhen backdrop switches to [start v]\nstop [other scripts in sprite v]\nstop all sounds\nset volume to (50) %\nforever\nplay sound (Dance Around v) until done\nend",shots:["choose-sprite","sounds-tab"],tip:"Use play sound until done inside forever. With start sound, the loop starts the song again many times every second and it sounds broken."},{title:"Change the music for each scene",short:"Give each scene its own song script in the Music sprite.",text:"Add a song for each scene to the Music sprite. Each scene gets its own <b>when backdrop switches to</b> script with the same pattern. Here is why <b>stop other scripts in sprite</b> matters: <b>stop all sounds</b> stops the song for a moment, but the old forever loop is still running, so it starts the old song again on top of the new one. Stopping the other scripts ends the old loop for real.",where:"Music",change:"new",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when backdrop switches to [cave v]\nstop [other scripts in sprite v]\nstop all sounds\nforever\nplay sound (Spooky String v) until done\nend",tip:"Keep all song scripts in the Music sprite only. If you put stop other scripts in sprite on the Stage or the Narrator, it would also stop their story scripts."},{title:"Add a voice line",short:"Record one dramatic line, or pick a sound, and play it in a scene.",text:"Pick a character or the Narrator. In its <b>Sounds</b> tab, point to the Choose a Sound button and click <b>Record</b>. Record one short line, like Who goes there? Then play it when that scene starts. The wait lets the music change first, because the music change uses stop all sounds.",where:"Narrator or a character",change:"new",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when I receive [go cave v]\nwait (1) seconds\nplay sound (recording1 v) until done",shots:["sounds-tab"],tip:"No microphone, or too noisy to record? Choose a sound from the library instead, like a growl, a scream, or a laugh, and add a say block with the words. Keep recordings school-appropriate."},{title:"Fade a character in",short:"Make a My Block called fade in and use it when a scene starts.",text:"Pick a character who appears in one scene, like a dragon in the cave. Make a block in <b>My Blocks</b> named <b>fade in</b>. Ghost 100 is invisible. Changing ghost by minus 5, twenty times, makes it slowly appear. Also add <b>hide</b> and <b>clear graphic effects</b> to this character's green flag script, so it starts hidden every time.",where:"A character who appears in one scene",change:"new",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"define fade in\nset [ghost v] effect to (100)\nshow\nrepeat (20)\nchange [ghost v] effect by (-5)\nend\n\nwhen backdrop switches to [cave v]\nfade in",shots:["make-block"],tip:"A My Block only works in the sprite where you made it. To make the character leave when the reader clicks a choice, add: when I receive hide choices, then hide."},{title:"Set the mood with effects",short:"Make spooky scenes darker and happy endings colorful, on the Stage.",text:"Build this on the Stage by yourself. When the backdrop switches to a spooky scene, make the Stage darker. When it switches to a happy ending, make the colors change a few times. Every scene script should start with clear graphic effects, so effects from the last scene do not pile up. The green flag should also clear effects.<br>Blocks that help (Looks and Events): when backdrop switches to, clear graphic effects, set effect to, change effect by, repeat, wait.",where:"Stage",change:"new",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nclear graphic effects\n\nwhen backdrop switches to [cave v]\nclear graphic effects\nset [brightness v] effect to (-40)\n\nwhen backdrop switches to [treasure v]\nclear graphic effects\nrepeat (10)\nchange [color v] effect by (25)\nwait (0.1) seconds\nend",shots:["select-stage","cat-looks"],tip:"Brightness minus 40 is darker. Plus 40 is lighter. Try other effects too, like whirl or fisheye for a dream."},{title:"Polish and test every path",short:"Give each ending a sound and message. Then test every path.",text:"Give each ending its own final sound and a message like THE END: you found 1 of 3 endings. Then play every path. Listen carefully: when the scene changes, does only the new song play? Click the green flag in the middle of a story: does everything reset? Save and share.",frame:"Path: start, then ____, then ____\nOnly one song plays at a time? yes / no\nEffects look right? yes / no\nWhat I fixed: ____",tip:"Two songs at once? That scene's song script is missing stop other scripts in sprite, or it is not in the Music sprite."}],quiz:[{q:"This is the Music sprite. The backdrop switches from start to cave. What do you hear?",blocks:"when backdrop switches to [start v]\nforever\nplay sound (Dance Around v) until done\nend\n\nwhen backdrop switches to [cave v]\nstop all sounds\nforever\nplay sound (Spooky String v) until done\nend",choices:["Only Spooky String","Silence","Both songs, because the start loop is still running","Only Dance Around"],answer:2,explain:"stop all sounds stops the sound, not the script. The old forever loop plays Dance Around again on top."},{q:"This music sounds broken and restarts very fast. What should you change?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nforever\nstart sound (Dance Around v)\nend",choices:["Use play sound until done inside the forever","Add more forever loops","Set the volume to 100 %","Move the script to the Stage"],answer:0,explain:"start sound does not wait, so forever starts the song again many times a second. play sound until done waits."}],challenge:{title:"Ending tracker",short:"Show how many different endings the reader has found.",text:"Make a variable <b>endings found</b> and one variable for each ending, like <b>found treasure</b>. When the reader reaches an ending for the first time, add 1 to endings found. Reaching the same ending again must not add more. Show the endings found monitor on the Stage at every ending. Do not reset these variables on the green flag, so they last between plays. Use a key, like r, to reset them.",answer:"when I receive [go treasure v]\nif <(found treasure) = [0]> then\nset [found treasure v] to (1)\nchange [endings found v] by (1)\nend\nshow variable [endings found v]\n\nwhen [r v] key pressed\nset [found treasure v] to (0)\nset [found home v] to (0)\nset [found forest v] to (0)\nset [endings found v] to (0)"},checklist:["Each scene has its own music","Only one song plays at a time when scenes change","One voice line or sound effect in a scene","A visual effect or fade that sets the mood","Every path still works and resets on the green flag"],submit:'Share your finished adventure with music, sound, and effects. Name your project anything you like (idea: "The Spooky Cave"). Paste the link.',teacherNotes:"Pacing (45 min): warmup 3, plan 4, Music sprite 6, scene music 7, voice line 6, fade in 7, effects 7, polish and test 5. Absent students start from the starter project.<br><b>The music bug</b>: stop all sounds silences the current sound but does not stop a forever loop, so the old loop starts its song again over the new one. The fix used here: all music lives in one hidden Music sprite, and each scene's music script starts with stop other scripts in sprite and stop all sounds. Keeping music in its own sprite matters: stop other scripts in sprite on the Stage or Narrator would kill story scripts. stop all sounds also cuts sound effects that are playing when a scene changes, which is why the voice line waits 1 second.<br><b>Common misconceptions</b>: start sound inside forever (restarts constantly); effects piling up (set vs change, no clear graphic effects); a My Block used in another sprite; a fading character left invisible on the next run (needs clear graphic effects on the green flag).<br><b>Accommodations</b>: recording is optional; library sounds plus a say block are a full alternative for students without a microphone or who are uncomfortable recording. Set a headphones rule and a quiet recording corner. Students with hearing differences can focus on visual mood (effects, fades) and check music with a peer or by watching the Sounds tab. Typing is light in this lesson; the frames can be filled with single words."},t1:{title:"Hello, Sprite!",tagline:"Step through the portal and take control of your first sprite.",mission:"Make a sprite move, turn, and glide around the Stage.",minutes:"40",skill:"Sprites, the Stage & Motion",goal:"By the end of this quest you will know your way around Scratch, move a sprite with motion blocks, use x and y to place it on the Stage, and save and share your project.",vocab:[{term:"Sprite",def:"A character or object in your project. Sprites can move, talk, and run code.",icon:"🐱"},{term:"Stage",def:"The big rectangle where your project plays.",icon:"🎭"},{term:"Block",def:"A puzzle piece of code. You snap blocks together.",icon:"🧩"},{term:"Script",def:"A stack of blocks snapped together. They run from top to bottom.",icon:"📜"},{term:"x and y",def:"Numbers for a spot on the Stage. x is left-right. y is up-down.",icon:"📍"}],warmup:"If you could program any character to do anything, what would it be? Say it in one sentence to a neighbor, or write it down. Then think: what is the very first instruction you would give it?",needs:[],steps:[{title:"Open Scratch and sign in",short:"Go to the Scratch website, sign in, and click Create.",text:"Go to <b>scratch.mit.edu</b> and click <b>Sign in</b>. Use your class account. Then click <b>Create</b> at the top. You now have a new project with one sprite: the cat, called <b>Sprite1</b>.",tip:"Always sign in first. If you are not signed in, Scratch cannot save your work to your account. Look for your username in the top right corner."},{title:"Tour the map",short:"Find the four parts: blocks, code area, Stage, and sprite list.",text:"Find the four main areas:<br><b>1. Block palette</b> (left side): all the blocks, sorted by color.<br><b>2. Code area</b> (middle): where you build scripts.<br><b>3. Stage</b> (top right): where your project plays.<br><b>4. Sprite list</b> (bottom right): all your sprites.<br>Click each color circle on the far left (Motion, Looks, Sound, Events) and watch the palette jump to that color.",tip:"Every block color is a category. Blue is Motion, purple is Looks, yellow is Events, orange is Control.",shots:["tour"]},{title:"Your first move",short:"Drag a move block into the code area and click it.",text:"Click <b>Motion</b>. Drag a <code>move (10) steps</code> block into the code area. <b>Click the block</b>. The sprite moves! Click it again and again. Now change 10 to 50 and click it. Then try a negative number, like -50. Which way does it go now?",where:"Sprite1",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"move (10) steps",tip:"Lost your sprite off the edge of the Stage? Drag it back with the mouse, or click a go to x: 0 y: 0 block to bring it to the center."},{title:"Turn and spin",short:"Snap a turn block under your move block and click the stack.",text:"Drag a <code>turn cw (15) degrees</code> block into the code area. Snap it <b>under</b> your move block. You will feel it click into place. Now the two blocks are one <b>script</b>. Click the script: both blocks run, top to bottom. Change 15 to 90 and click it four times. What shape did the sprite make?",where:"Sprite1",change:"add",changeNote:"Snap the turn block under the move block from the last step.",scaffold:"full",blocks:"move (50) steps\nturn cw (90) degrees",tip:"If only one block runs, the blocks are not snapped together. Drag the bottom block away and drop it again, right under the first block."},{title:"The x and y treasure map",short:"Use a go to block to send your sprite to each corner.",text:"Drag your sprite around the Stage with the mouse. Watch the <b>x</b> and <b>y</b> numbers under the Stage change.<br>Now use <code>go to x: () y: ()</code> to jump your sprite to a spot. Type the numbers, then click the block.<br>Can you find all four corners? Hint: x is 240 or -240. y is 180 or -180. The center is x: 0, y: 0.",where:"Sprite1",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"go to x: (0) y: (0)\ngo to x: (-200) y: (150)",tip:"Positive x goes right, negative x goes left. Positive y goes up, negative y goes down. If your sprite is stuck on an edge, click go to x: 0 y: 0 to bring it home.",shots:["xy"]},{title:"Glide like a pro",short:"Build a script that glides your sprite to three different spots.",text:"<b>go to</b> jumps right away. <b>glide</b> moves smoothly, so you can watch it. Build this script and click it. The first block puts the sprite at its starting spot every time.",where:"Sprite1",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"go to x: (-200) y: (0)\nglide (1) secs to x: (0) y: (100)\nglide (1) secs to x: (200) y: (0)\nglide (1) secs to x: (0) y: (-100)",tip:"Make the first number bigger to glide slower. Try 3 seconds."},{title:"Corner tour",short:"Build your own script that visits all four corners and returns home.",text:"Now you build it with no picture. Make one script that:<br>1. Starts the sprite at the center (x: 0, y: 0).<br>2. Glides to all four corners of the Stage, one after another.<br>3. Glides back to the center at the end.<br>Use the <b>go to</b> and <b>glide</b> blocks from Motion. Click your script to test it. Does the sprite end at x: 0, y: 0?",where:"Sprite1",change:"new",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"go to x: (0) y: (0)\nglide (1) secs to x: (-200) y: (150)\nglide (1) secs to x: (200) y: (150)\nglide (1) secs to x: (200) y: (-150)\nglide (1) secs to x: (-200) y: (-150)\nglide (1) secs to x: (0) y: (0)",tip:"Use numbers a little smaller than the edges, like 200 and 150, so you can still see the sprite in the corner."},{title:"Name it, save it, share it",short:"Name your project, save it, and click Share.",text:"Click the title box above the code area and type a name for your project. Name it anything you like. Click <b>File</b>, then <b>Save now</b>. Then click the orange <b>Share</b> button. Copy the link from the address bar at the top of your browser. That link is what you turn in.",tip:"If the Share button is grey and you cannot click it, your account email may not be confirmed yet. Tell your teacher. Never put your last name or other personal information in a project."}],quiz:[{q:"You click this script. Where does the sprite end up?",blocks:"go to x: (0) y: (0)\ngo to x: (-150) y: (100)",choices:["Up and to the left","Down and to the left","Up and to the right","Back in the center"],answer:0,explain:"The sprite stops at the last spot. Negative x is left. Positive y is up. So the sprite ends up and to the left."},{q:"Blocks are snapped together into a script. What happens when you click it?",choices:["Only the top block runs","The blocks run in order, top to bottom","The blocks run bottom to top","Nothing. You must click each block."],answer:1,explain:"A script runs from the top block down to the bottom block, in order."}],challenge:{title:"Walk a square",short:"Make your sprite walk a square and end where it started.",text:"Make your sprite walk in a square path and stop exactly where it started, facing the same way. Use only <b>move</b> and <b>turn</b> blocks. How many of each do you need? How many degrees is each turn?",answer:"move (100) steps\nturn cw (90) degrees\nmove (100) steps\nturn cw (90) degrees\nmove (100) steps\nturn cw (90) degrees\nmove (100) steps\nturn cw (90) degrees"},checklist:["My sprite moves and turns with a script.","I used go to x: y: to put my sprite in a spot.","My corner tour script visits four corners and comes back to the center.","My project has a name and is shared."],submit:"Share your project and paste the link. Name your project anything you like (idea: My First Sprite).",teacherNotes:"PACING (40 min): sign in + tour 8 min, move/turn 7 min, x/y treasure map 8 min, glide 5 min, corner tour (goal step) 7 min, save/share 5 min. Check that every student is signed in during the first 5 minutes; the most common lost-work problem is building while signed out. Share is disabled on accounts whose email is not confirmed (teacher student accounts are normally fine) - have a fallback such as File > Save to your computer or adding students to a class studio.\nMISCONCEPTIONS: (1) x and y mixed up - have students point left/right for x and up/down for y. (2) Thinking blocks that sit near each other are one script; they must be snapped. (3) Thinking go to and glide 'add' to the position; they go to an exact spot. (4) Sprite lost off-Stage - go to x: 0 y: 0 or drag it back.\nACCOMMODATIONS: Students with fine-motor needs may struggle to drop blocks exactly; show that a block snaps when the white shadow appears, and allow a trackpad-to-mouse swap. Students new to coordinates or with math anxiety: give a printed Stage grid with the four corners labeled. English learners: point to the four areas on screen while saying the vocabulary words; the read-aloud and the screenshot help. Students who finish early go to the square challenge."},t2:{title:"Lights, Camera, Events!",tagline:"Make things happen when YOU say so: flags, keys, and clicks.",mission:"Start your code with the green flag, keys, and clicks.",minutes:"40",skill:"Events & Sequences",goal:"By the end of this quest you will start scripts with event blocks (green flag, key presses, sprite clicks), put blocks in the right order, add speech and sound, and reset your sprites when the green flag is clicked.",vocab:[{term:"Event",def:"Something that happens, like a click or a key press, that starts a script.",icon:"⚡"},{term:"Hat block",def:"A block with a round top. It sits on top of a script and waits.",icon:"🎩"},{term:"Sequence",def:"The order that steps happen in. The order matters!",icon:"🔢"},{term:"Green flag",def:"The button above the Stage that starts a project.",icon:"🚩"},{term:"Reset",def:"Put things back to how they start, so the project works the same every time.",icon:"🔄"}],warmup:"Write the steps to make a sandwich, in order, in 4 or 5 short lines. Then swap two of the steps. What goes wrong? You can do this alone, or read your steps to a neighbor and let them act it out exactly.",needs:[],steps:[{title:"Start a new project",short:"Make a new project and look at the yellow Events blocks.",text:"Click <b>File</b>, then <b>New</b>. Click the title box and give the project a name. Then click the yellow <b>Events</b> circle on the left. Look at the blocks with round tops. These are <b>hat blocks</b>. Each one waits for something to happen.",shots:["cat-events"]},{title:"The green flag",short:"Build a script that starts when the green flag is clicked.",text:"Build this script. Then click the <b>green flag</b> above the Stage to run it. You do not need to click the script anymore. The hat block starts it.<br>The first two blocks are a <b>reset</b>. They put the sprite back in the center, facing right, every time you click the flag.",where:"Sprite1",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (0) y: (0)\npoint in direction (90)\nsay [Welcome to my party!] for (2) seconds\nsay [Press the arrow keys!] for (2) seconds",tip:"Get into the habit: almost every project starts with a green flag script that resets things. Hat blocks only go on top. Nothing can snap above them.",shots:["green-flag"]},{title:"Key presses",short:"Make four scripts so the arrow keys move your sprite.",text:"Make the sprite move with keys. Build four small scripts, one for each arrow key. Each script has its own hat block. Then press the arrow keys to test.",where:"Sprite1",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when [right arrow v] key pressed\nchange x by (10)\n\nwhen [left arrow v] key pressed\nchange x by (-10)\n\nwhen [up arrow v] key pressed\nchange y by (10)\n\nwhen [down arrow v] key pressed\nchange y by (-10)",tip:"Keys not working? Click once on the Stage first. Scratch only hears the keys when the Stage is selected, not when you are typing in a block."},{title:"Click the sprite",short:"Make your sprite play a sound when someone clicks it.",text:"Add a script that runs when someone clicks the sprite on the Stage. Find <b>start sound</b> in the pink <b>Sound</b> category. Click the sprite on the Stage to test.",where:"Sprite1",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when this sprite clicked\nstart sound (Meow v)\nsay [Hey, that tickles!] for (1) seconds",tip:"No sound? Check that your headphones are plugged in and the volume is up. Want a different sound? Open the Sounds tab and choose one.",shots:["cat-sound","sounds-tab"]},{title:"Add a second sprite",short:"Add a second sprite and give it its own scripts.",text:"Click the <b>Choose a Sprite</b> button (the cat face at the bottom right) and pick a second sprite. Now <b>click the new sprite in the sprite list</b>. The code area is empty! Every sprite has its own code area. Give the new sprite a script for the space key.",where:"Your second sprite",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when [space v] key pressed\nturn cw (15) degrees\nstart sound (pop v)",tip:"Code not working? Look at the sprite list. The sprite with the blue box around it is the one you are coding. If you put code on the wrong sprite, drag the script onto the right sprite in the sprite list to copy it, then delete the old one.",shots:["choose-sprite"]},{title:"Sequence detective",short:"Put the mixed up blocks in order so the story makes sense.",text:"The blocks for a tiny show are mixed up. Before you build it, read each block and <b>predict</b>: what should happen first, next, and last? Then put them in order on your first sprite and click the green flag. Does the story make sense? Try swapping two say blocks. What changes?",where:"Sprite1",change:"new",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (-150) y: (0)\nsay [Hello!] for (2) seconds\nglide (2) secs to x: (150) y: (0)\nsay [I made it across!] for (2) seconds\nsay [Goodbye!] for (2) seconds",tip:"Clues: the hat block is always first. The reset goes right under it. You say Goodbye at the end, not the beginning."},{title:"Your own event",short:"Make your second sprite react to a key you choose.",text:"Now build one with no picture. Pick a key, like <b>b</b> or <b>w</b>. When that key is pressed, your <b>second sprite</b> should:<br>1. Say something.<br>2. Play a sound.<br>3. Move somewhere new.<br>Also give the second sprite a <b>green flag</b> script that resets it to a starting spot. Use blocks from Events, Looks, Sound, and Motion.",where:"Your second sprite",change:"new",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (100) y: (-100)\n\nwhen [b v] key pressed\nsay [You found my secret key!] for (2) seconds\nstart sound (pop v)\nglide (1) secs to x: (0) y: (100)",tip:"Test the reset: press your key, then click the green flag. Does the sprite go back to its starting spot?"},{title:"Save and share",short:"Save your project and click Share.",text:"Click <b>File</b>, then <b>Save now</b>. Then click <b>Share</b> and copy the link from the address bar."}],quiz:[{q:"The sprite is at x: 0. You click the Stage, then press the space key three times. What is x now?",blocks:"when [space v] key pressed\nchange x by (10)",choices:["10","0","30","3"],answer:2,explain:"The script runs once for every press. 10 plus 10 plus 10 is 30."},{q:"Why do many green flag scripts start with a go to x: y: block?",choices:["To make the sprite talk","To reset the sprite to its starting spot","To delete the sprite","It makes the project load faster"],answer:1,explain:"A reset puts the sprite back, so the project starts the same way every time."}],challenge:{title:"Costume and backdrop keys",short:"Make one key change the costume and another key change the backdrop.",text:"Make the <b>c</b> key change the sprite's costume, and the <b>n</b> key change the backdrop. You will need to add at least one backdrop first. Look in the purple Looks category for the blocks. Bonus: make the green flag reset both the costume and the backdrop.",answer:"when [c v] key pressed\nnext costume\n\nwhen [n v] key pressed\nnext backdrop\n\nwhen green flag clicked\nswitch costume to (costume1 v)\nswitch backdrop to (backdrop1 v)"},checklist:["My project has a green flag script that resets a sprite.","My project uses at least three different event hat blocks.","My project has two sprites, each with its own scripts.","My project uses speech and a sound."],submit:"Share your project and paste the link. Name your project anything you like (idea: Event Party).",teacherNotes:"PACING (40 min): warmup 3 min, new project + green flag 7 min, key presses 6 min, click sprite 4 min, second sprite 6 min, sequence detective 5 min, your own event (goal) 7 min, save/share 2 min.\nMISCONCEPTIONS: (1) Trying to put a hat block in the middle or bottom of a script - hats only go on top. (2) Code built on the wrong sprite - teach students to look for the blue highlight in the sprite list; scripts can be dragged onto another sprite to copy. (3) Keys 'not working' because focus is in a text field or block input - click the Stage first. (4) Thinking the reset happens by itself; it only happens because the script says so.\nACCOMMODATIONS: Students with reading difficulty can use read-aloud on each card; the sequence detective step is a good place to pair a student with you for think-aloud tracing. Students who struggle with keyboard/mouse coordination may find four arrow scripts tiring to build; let them build two (right and left) and duplicate with right-click > duplicate. The warmup is written so it can be done alone. Students with hearing impairments or no headphones can replace sounds with say blocks."},t3:{title:"Loop-de-Loop",tagline:"Why build it 100 times when a loop can do it for you?",mission:"Paint your own sprite and bring it to life with loops.",minutes:"45",skill:"Loops, Costumes & the Paint Editor",goal:"By the end of this quest you will use repeat and forever loops, paint your own sprite with two costumes, animate it with next costume and wait, and use graphic effects that reset each time.",vocab:[{term:"Loop",def:"A block that runs the blocks inside it again and again.",icon:"🔁"},{term:"repeat",def:"A loop that runs a set number of times, then stops.",icon:"🔢"},{term:"forever",def:"A loop that never stops, until you click the red stop sign.",icon:"♾️"},{term:"Costume",def:"One look of a sprite. Switching costumes fast makes animation.",icon:"👕"},{term:"Paint editor",def:"The drawing tool in the Costumes tab. You draw sprites and backdrops there.",icon:"🎨"}],warmup:"Think of things in real life that repeat: brushing your teeth, the chorus of a song, the days of the week. Which ones repeat a set number of times? Which ones repeat forever? Write one of each, or share with a neighbor.",needs:[],steps:[{title:"The long way",short:"Build move and turn four times in a row and click it.",text:"Start a new project and give it a name. Build this script on the cat: move and turn, four times in a row. Click it. It works, but it is long! Now imagine building it 100 times.",where:"Sprite1",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"move (10) steps\nturn cw (15) degrees\nmove (10) steps\nturn cw (15) degrees\nmove (10) steps\nturn cw (15) degrees\nmove (10) steps\nturn cw (15) degrees",tip:"Right-click a block and choose Duplicate to copy it and all the blocks under it."},{title:"The loop way",short:"Put one move and one turn inside a repeat block instead.",text:"Delete the long script. Now put ONE move and ONE turn inside a <code>repeat</code> block from the orange <b>Control</b> category. Click the green flag. The sprite spins in a full circle.",where:"Sprite1",change:"replace",changeNote:"Replaces the long script from step 1. Drag the old script back to the block palette to delete it.",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nrepeat (24)\nmove (10) steps\nturn cw (15) degrees\nend",tip:"24 times 15 degrees is 360 degrees. That is one full circle! The blocks must be INSIDE the repeat, in the mouth of the C shape.",shots:["cat-control"]},{title:"Paint your own sprite",short:"Use the paint editor to draw your own sprite.",text:"Move your mouse over the <b>Choose a Sprite</b> button (bottom right). Click the <b>Paint</b> brush. The paint editor opens in the <b>Costumes</b> tab.<br>Check the button at the bottom says <b>Convert to Bitmap</b>. That means you are in <b>Vector</b> mode, which is easy to fix and move.<br>Try these tools:<br><b>Rectangle</b> and <b>Circle</b>: drag to draw a shape.<br><b>Fill</b>: pick a color at the top, then click a shape to color it.<br><b>Text</b>: click and type words.<br><b>Eraser</b>: rub out a part.<br><b>Select</b> (the arrow): click a shape to move it or make it bigger.<br>Draw a simple creature, robot, or face. Give your sprite a name in the box above the sprite list.",tip:"Keep the drawing in the middle of the canvas. The middle is the <b>costume center</b>: the point your sprite turns around. Select all your shapes and drag them so the drawing sits on the center. It snaps when you get close. Fine-motor tip: you can also pick a library sprite and change its colors with Fill.",shots:["choose-sprite","costumes-tab"]},{title:"Make a second costume",short:"Duplicate your costume and change one small thing.",text:"In the Costumes tab, right-click your costume and choose <b>duplicate</b>. Click the new costume (costume2). Change ONE small thing: move an arm, close the eyes, open the mouth, or tilt a shape. Click between costume1 and costume2. Can you see it move a little? That small change is how animation works.",tip:"Small changes look better than big changes. If the sprite jumps around when costumes switch, the drawings are not both on the costume center."},{title:"Bring it to life",short:"Put the blocks in order so your sprite walks and bounces forever.",text:"Click your painted sprite in the sprite list. Put these blocks in order to make it walk back and forth forever. <code>next costume</code> switches between your two costumes. <code>wait</code> slows it down so your eyes can see each costume.",where:"Your painted sprite",change:"new",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset rotation style [left-right v]\nforever\nmove (10) steps\nnext costume\nif on edge, bounce\nwait (0.2) seconds\nend",tip:"The set rotation style block goes BEFORE the forever, so it runs one time. Without it, your sprite walks upside down after it bounces. Nothing can go under a forever: it never finishes.",shots:["cat-control"]},{title:"Color party",short:"Make a loop that changes a color effect, and reset it first.",text:"Loops can repeat any block, even effects. Put these blocks in order on your painted sprite. The <code>clear graphic effects</code> block is the reset. It removes old effects every time you click the green flag.",where:"Your painted sprite",change:"new",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nclear graphic effects\nforever\nchange [color v] effect by (5)\nwait (0.1) seconds\nend",tip:"Effects stay on the sprite after you click stop. If you skip clear graphic effects, they pile up and the sprite starts the next run with old colors.",shots:["cat-looks"]},{title:"Spin trick",short:"Make your sprite spin once and shrink back when clicked.",text:"Now build it with no picture. When someone <b>clicks</b> your painted sprite, it should:<br>1. Spin around one full circle (use a <b>repeat</b> loop with a turn block).<br>2. Grow a little each time around the loop.<br>3. After the loop, go back to its normal size of 100.<br>Hint: look in Looks for <b>change size by</b> and <b>set size to</b>. How many times 36 degrees makes 360?",where:"Your painted sprite",change:"new",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"when this sprite clicked\nrepeat (10)\nturn cw (36) degrees\nchange size by (5)\nend\nset size to (100) %",tip:"Does the sprite wobble when it spins? Go back to the Costumes tab and move the drawing onto the costume center."},{title:"Save and share",short:"Check your loops, then save and share.",text:"Make sure your project has at least one <b>repeat</b> and one <b>forever</b>, and your own painted sprite. Click <b>File</b>, then <b>Save now</b>. Click <b>Share</b> and copy the link."}],quiz:[{q:"You click the green flag. What is the sprite's x at the end?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (0) y: (0)\npoint in direction (90)\nrepeat (4)\nmove (50) steps\nend",choices:["50","4","54","200"],answer:3,explain:"The loop runs move 50 four times. 50 times 4 is 200. Direction 90 is right, so x grows."},{q:"Each time you click the green flag, your sprite starts with stranger colors. What is missing?",choices:["A wait block","A clear graphic effects block at the start","Another forever loop","A second costume"],answer:1,explain:"Effects stay after you click stop. clear graphic effects at the start resets them every run."}],challenge:{title:"Spiral tunnel",short:"Make your sprite fly in a spiral and grow bigger each time.",text:"Make a spiral. When the green flag is clicked, start the sprite small. Then, inside a repeat loop, make it move a little, turn a little, and grow a little each time. It should look like it is flying out of a tunnel. Use only blocks you know from Motion, Looks, and Control. Remember to reset the size and position first.",answer:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (0) y: (0)\nset size to (20) %\nrepeat (36)\nmove (15) steps\nturn cw (10) degrees\nchange size by (3)\nend"},checklist:["My project has a sprite I painted myself, with two costumes.","My sprite walks with forever, next costume, and wait.","My project uses a repeat loop.","My color effect starts with clear graphic effects."],submit:"Share your project and paste the link. Name your project anything you like (idea: Loop Lab).",teacherNotes:"PACING (45 min): warmup 3 min, long way + loop way 7 min, paint your sprite 12 min (set a visible timer: students can spend the whole period painting), second costume 5 min, bring it to life 6 min, color party 4 min, spin trick (goal) 6 min, save/share 2 min.\nMISCONCEPTIONS: (1) Putting the green flag hat inside a loop, or blocks below forever (they never run - forever has no bottom notch for this reason). (2) Blocks next to the loop instead of inside its mouth. (3) Effects pile up between runs - clear graphic effects on the green flag is the reset. (4) Bitmap vs vector: if a student clicked Convert to Bitmap, the Fill and Select tools act differently; click Convert to Vector to go back. (5) Wobbly spinning means the drawing is off the costume center.\nACCOMMODATIONS: Painting is hard for students with fine-motor needs or limited mouse control. Allow them to start from a library sprite and only recolor it with Fill, or draw with large rectangles and circles only (no freehand). Students who struggle with the parsons steps can say each block aloud and ask 'what should happen first?'. Students who are perfectionists about art: remind them the goal is two costumes that animate, and they can polish the drawing later. English learners: the tool names in the paint editor appear when you hover over each tool; point and name them together."},t4:{title:"If This, Then That",tagline:"Teach your sprite to make decisions in a maze.",mission:"Build a maze game with walls and a goal.",minutes:"45",skill:"Conditionals & Sensing",goal:"By the end of this quest you will use if-then blocks with sensing conditions, move a sprite smoothly with key pressed checks in a forever loop, react to wall colors and goal sprites, and stop the game with stop all.",vocab:[{term:"Condition",def:"A true or false question. In Scratch these are the pointy blocks.",icon:"❓"},{term:"if then",def:"A block that runs the code inside only when the condition is true.",icon:"🚦"},{term:"Sensing",def:"Blocks that check things, like a key press or touching something.",icon:"👀"},{term:"Eyedropper",def:"A tool that copies a color by clicking on it.",icon:"💧"}],warmup:'Finish these sentences: "IF the bell rings, THEN ..." and "IF it is raining, THEN ...". Write three of your own. You can say them to a neighbor or write them down. You use conditions all day!',needs:[],steps:[{title:"Paint the maze",short:"Paint a maze backdrop with thick walls all in one color.",text:"Start a new project and give it a name. Click the <b>Stage</b> (bottom right), then the <b>Backdrops</b> tab. You are in the same paint editor you used for your sprite last time.<br>Use the <b>Rectangle</b> tool with a fill color to draw thick walls. Use ONE color for all walls, like dark blue. Leave a wide path from a start corner to an end corner.",tip:"Make walls thick, at least as wide as your finger on the screen. Thin walls can be skipped by a fast sprite. Hard to draw? Choose a backdrop from the library and use one color in it as the walls, or ask your teacher for a ready-made maze.",shots:["select-stage","backdrops"]},{title:"Add a player",short:"Add a small player sprite and make it fit on the path.",text:"Click <b>Choose a Sprite</b> and pick a small player, like the Ball or the Beetle. You can also use the sprite you painted last lesson. Make it smaller: under the Stage, change <b>Size</b> to about 30. It must fit on the path with room to spare.",tip:"Use the wall color ONLY for walls. If your path, your goal, or another sprite uses that color, touching it will count as touching a wall.",shots:["choose-sprite"]},{title:"Smooth arrow keys",short:"Use a forever loop with if blocks to move with the arrow keys.",text:'In t2 you used "when key pressed" hats. They are a little jumpy. This way is smoother: a forever loop that keeps checking each key. Find <code>key space pressed?</code> in the light blue <b>Sensing</b> category and drop it in the hole of an <code>if then</code> block.<br>Change the go to numbers to your maze\'s start spot.',where:"Player",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\ngo to x: (-200) y: (-150)\nforever\nif <key (right arrow v) pressed?> then\nchange x by (3)\nend\nif <key (left arrow v) pressed?> then\nchange x by (-3)\nend\nif <key (up arrow v) pressed?> then\nchange y by (3)\nend\nif <key (down arrow v) pressed?> then\nchange y by (-3)\nend\nend",tip:"All four if blocks go INSIDE the forever, so the sprite checks the keys again and again. Click the Stage first so the keys work."},{title:"Walls send you back",short:"Use touching color so walls send your player back to start.",text:"Add a new script to the player. Use the <code>touching color</code> block from Sensing. To set the color: click the color square in the block, click the <b>eyedropper</b> at the bottom, then click a wall on the Stage.",where:"Player",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nforever\nif <touching color [#1e3a8a]?> then\ngo to x: (-200) y: (-150)\nsay [Ouch! Back to start!] for (1) seconds\nend\nend",tip:"Click the MIDDLE of a thick wall with the eyedropper. The edges of shapes have soft, mixed colors, and those will not match.",shots:["touching-color"]},{title:"Add a goal",short:"Add a goal sprite, then build the winning script in order.",text:"Add a second sprite as the goal, like the Star. Drag it to the end of the maze. Then click the <b>player</b> and put these blocks in order. <code>stop all</code> is in Control. It ends the whole game.",where:"Player",change:"new",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nforever\nif <touching (Star v)?> then\nsay [I made it!] for (2) seconds\nstop [all v]\nend\nend",tip:"Check that this script is on the player, not the Star. In the touching block, pick the goal's name from the dropdown.",shots:["choose-sprite"]},{title:"Danger zone",short:"Add spots of a new color that send the player back with a message.",text:'Build this one with no picture. Go back to your backdrop and paint a few spots of a NEW color, like red lava or green slime. When the player touches that color, it should:<br>1. Say a different message, like "Too hot!".<br>2. Go back to the start.<br>You already know every block you need: look at your wall script for ideas.',where:"Player",change:"new",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nforever\nif <touching color [#ff0000]?> then\nsay [Too hot!] for (1) seconds\ngo to x: (-200) y: (-150)\nend\nend",tip:"The danger color must be different from the wall color, and must not be on the player or the goal."},{title:"Playtest",short:"Let someone play your maze, then fix one thing.",text:"Ask a neighbor to play your maze while you watch. Do not help them! Notice: Is it too easy or too hard? Can the player get through a wall? Is the start spot right? Then fix ONE thing.",solo:"Play your maze three times as if you have never seen it. Try to break it: push into every wall and corner. Write one thing that went wrong, then fix it. You can also ask your teacher to play.",frame:"When I played, I noticed ____.\nOne thing that was hard was ____.\nI fixed it by ____."},{title:"Save and share",short:"Save your maze and click Share.",text:"Click <b>File</b>, then <b>Save now</b>. Click <b>Share</b> and copy the link."}],quiz:[{q:"You click the green flag. Three seconds later, you press space. What happens?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nif <key (space v) pressed?> then\nsay [Jump!]\nend",choices:["The sprite says Jump!","Nothing happens","The sprite says Jump! forever","The project stops"],answer:1,explain:"There is no forever loop, so the key is checked only once, at the start. Nobody was pressing space then."},{q:"Your fast player sometimes goes right through a wall. What is the best fix?",choices:["Make the walls thicker","Delete the forever loop","Use a different wall color for each wall","Add more say blocks"],answer:0,explain:"A sprite can jump over a thin wall between checks. Thick walls are always caught."}],challenge:{title:"Moving enemy",short:"Add an enemy that glides back and forth and sends you home.",text:"Add an enemy sprite that glides back and forth across the path forever. If the player touches the enemy, the player goes back to the start. Think: which sprite needs which script? You will need one script on the enemy and one on the player.",answer:"when green flag clicked\nforever\nglide (2) secs to x: (100) y: (0)\nglide (2) secs to x: (-100) y: (0)\nend\n\nwhen green flag clicked\nforever\nif <touching (Enemy v)?> then\ngo to x: (-200) y: (-150)\nend\nend"},checklist:["My player moves with the arrow keys using if blocks in a forever loop.","My walls send the player back to the start.","My player wins and the game stops at the goal.","My maze has a danger zone or another change after playtesting."],submit:"Share your maze and paste the link. Name your project anything you like (idea: Maze Runner).",teacherNotes:"PACING (45 min): warmup 3 min, paint the maze 10 min (timer! keep it simple), add player 3 min, smooth arrow keys 7 min, walls 6 min, goal 5 min, danger zone (goal step) 5 min, playtest 4 min, save/share 2 min. If/else is NOT in this lesson anymore; it is taught in t5.\nMISCONCEPTIONS: (1) if blocks placed outside the forever loop, so they check only once (quiz 1 targets this). (2) Thinking the hexagon condition block is a command; it must sit inside an if. (3) Eyedropper picked an anti-aliased edge pixel, so the color never matches - click the middle of a thick wall. (4) Wall color used elsewhere (goal, path, other sprites) so they act as walls. Note: touching color ignores the sprite's own costume, but a player in the wall color is hard to see. (5) Fast players skip thin walls. (6) Scripts on the goal sprite instead of the player.\nACCOMMODATIONS: Drawing a maze is the biggest barrier for students with fine-motor needs or limited time: have a teacher-made maze project ready to remix, or let them pick a library backdrop and use one of its colors as walls. Students who find the long arrow-key script tiring can build one if block, then right-click > duplicate it three times and change the key and numbers. Students with anxiety about others playing their game can use the solo playtest. English learners: model the sentence 'IF I touch the wall, THEN I go back' while pointing to the blocks."},t5:{title:"Power-Up Pack",tagline:"Unlock the tools every big project needs: memory, messages, and choices.",mission:"Build a virtual pet that gets hungry and tells you how it feels.",minutes:"45",skill:"Variables, Broadcasts & Decisions",goal:"By the end of this quest you will make variables and use set and change, show a variable on the Stage, compare numbers with = < and >, choose between two actions with if-else, send messages with broadcast and when I receive, and wait until something is true.",vocab:[{term:"Variable",def:"A named box that holds a number or word. It can change.",icon:"📦"},{term:"Broadcast",def:"A message one sprite sends. Other sprites can hear it and react.",icon:"📣"},{term:"if else",def:"If the condition is true, do the first part. If not, do the second part.",icon:"🔀"},{term:"Compare",def:"Check if one number is equal to, less than, or greater than another.",icon:"⚖️"},{term:"Monitor",def:"The little box on the Stage that shows a variable's value.",icon:"📺"}],warmup:"Think about a pet or a video game character. What numbers about it change over time? (Ideas: energy, health, coins, score.) Write two, and what makes each one go up or down. You can do this alone or share with a neighbor.",needs:[],steps:[{title:"Choose your pet and its food",short:"Start a new project and add a pet sprite and a food sprite.",text:"Start a new project and give it a name. Add a <b>pet</b> sprite: any animal, monster, or robot from Choose a Sprite, or paint your own. Then add a <b>food</b> sprite, like the Apple, Donut, or a food you paint. Rename them <b>Pet</b> and <b>Food</b> in the box above the sprite list. You can delete the cat if you do not use it.<br>Pick a backdrop for your pet's home.",tip:"Clear names help a lot today. Blocks will show sprite names in their dropdowns.",shots:["choose-sprite","delete-sprite","backdrops"]},{title:"Make an energy variable",short:"Make a variable called energy and set it when the flag is clicked.",text:"Click the <b>Pet</b>. Open the orange <b>Variables</b> category and click <b>Make a Variable</b>. Type <b>energy</b>. Choose <b>For all sprites</b> and click OK.<br>Now a new <code>energy</code> block appears. The checkbox next to it shows the <b>monitor</b> on the Stage. Keep it checked so you can watch the number.<br>Build this script. <code>set</code> puts an exact number in the box. Every game needs this reset at the start.",where:"Pet",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [energy v] to (10)\nshow variable [energy v]\nsay [Hi! I am your new pet!] for (2) seconds",tip:"<b>For all sprites</b>: every sprite and the Stage can read and change it. Use this for things like score, time, or energy that other sprites need. <b>For this sprite only</b>: only this sprite can use it. Use this when each sprite needs its own, like its own speed. If you are not sure, choose For all sprites.",shots:["make-variable"]},{title:"Getting hungry",short:"Make energy go down by one every two seconds.",text:"Pets get hungry over time. Add a forever loop under your say block. <code>change energy by -1</code> takes one away from whatever is in the box. Click the green flag and watch the monitor count down.<br><b>set</b> puts in an exact number. <b>change</b> adds to the number that is already there. A negative number takes away.",where:"Pet",change:"add",changeNote:"Add the forever loop to the bottom of your green flag script from step 2, under the say block.",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [energy v] to (10)\nshow variable [energy v]\nsay [Hi! I am your new pet!] for (2) seconds\nforever\nwait (2) seconds\nchange [energy v] by (-1)\nend",tip:"If energy goes up instead of down, check for the minus sign: it must be -1, not 1. If energy starts at the old number, the set block is missing or below the forever.",shots:["cat-control"]},{title:"Feed with a message",short:"Click the food to send a message, and make the pet eat.",text:"The food and the pet are different sprites. They talk with <b>broadcast</b> messages.<br>1. Click the <b>Food</b>. Drag in <code>when this sprite clicked</code> and <code>broadcast</code> from Events. Click the dropdown in broadcast, choose <b>New message</b>, and type <b>feed</b>.<br>2. Click the <b>Pet</b>. Add the <code>when I receive feed</code> script.<br>Click the food on the Stage. Does energy go up?",where:"Food, then Pet",change:"new",scaffold:"full",blocks:"when this sprite clicked\nbroadcast (feed v)\n\nwhen I receive [feed v]\nchange [energy v] by (3)\nsay [Yum!] for (1) seconds",tip:"The first script goes on the Food. The second goes on the Pet. <b>broadcast</b> sends the message and keeps going right away. <b>broadcast and wait</b> sends it, then waits until every when I receive script for it is finished. Use broadcast and wait when the next block must happen after the other sprite is done.",shots:["cat-events"]},{title:"Show its mood",short:"Put the blocks in order so the pet shows a hungry or happy mood.",text:"Now the pet makes a choice with <code>if else</code>. If energy is <b>less than 4</b>, it thinks I am hungry. Else (any other time), it thinks I feel great. Find the <code>( ) &lt; ( )</code> block in the green <b>Operators</b> category. Drop the <code>energy</code> block in the left side and type 4 in the right side.<br>Put these blocks in order on the Pet.",where:"Pet",change:"new",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nforever\nif <(energy) < (4)> then\nthink [I am hungry...]\nelse\nthink [I feel great!]\nend\nend",tip:"The forever is needed so the pet checks its energy again and again. Read the less than sign like a mouth: it opens toward the bigger number.",shots:["cat-operators"]},{title:"Not too full",short:"Put the blocks in order so energy never goes above ten.",text:"Right now you can click the food again and again, and energy goes up to 50! Let's add a limit. Change your <b>when I receive feed</b> script: after energy goes up, if it is <b>greater than 10</b>, set it back to 10.",where:"Pet",change:"replace",changeNote:"Replaces the when I receive feed script on the Pet from step 4.",scaffold:"parsons",blocks:"when I receive [feed v]\nchange [energy v] by (3)\nif <(energy) > (10)> then\nset [energy v] to (10)\nsay [I am so full!] for (1) seconds\nend\nsay [Yum!] for (1) seconds",tip:"Use the greater than block, not less than. Test it: click the food many times. The monitor should stop at 10."},{title:"Fall asleep",short:"When energy reaches zero, make the pet fall asleep and stop the game.",text:"Build this one with no picture. Make a new green flag script on the Pet that:<br>1. Waits until energy <b>equals 0</b>.<br>2. Says a sleepy message, like Zzz.<br>3. Stops the whole game.<br>Helpful blocks: <b>wait until</b> (Control), the <b>=</b> block (Operators), your <b>energy</b> variable, <b>say</b>, and <b>stop all</b>.",where:"Pet",change:"new",scaffold:"goal",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nwait until <(energy) = (0)>\nsay [Zzz... I fell asleep.] for (2) seconds\nstop [all v]",tip:"wait until pauses this script, but other scripts keep running. Test by not feeding the pet for 20 seconds."},{title:"Make it yours, then share",short:"Change one thing to make the pet yours, then save and share.",text:"Make the pet your own. Pick at least one:<br>Change how fast energy drops.<br>Change what the pet says or thinks.<br>Add a sound to the feed script.<br>Make the food go to a new spot after each click.<br>Then click <b>File</b>, then <b>Save now</b>. Click <b>Share</b> and copy the link.",tip:"Test everything again after you change it: start, feed, and let it fall asleep."}],quiz:[{q:"You click the green flag. What number does the score monitor show at the end?",blocks:"when green flag clicked\nset [score v] to (5)\nchange [score v] by (2)\nset [score v] to (1)\nchange [score v] by (3)",choices:["11","4","3","8"],answer:1,explain:"set puts in an exact number. change adds to it. 5, then 7, then set to 1, then 1 plus 3 is 4."},{q:"Energy is exactly 4. What does the pet think?",blocks:"if <(energy) < (4)> then\nthink [I am hungry...]\nelse\nthink [I feel great!]\nend",choices:["I am hungry...","Both messages","I feel great!","Nothing"],answer:2,explain:"4 is not less than 4, so the condition is false. The else part runs."}],challenge:{title:"Play time",short:"Add a toy and a happiness variable that drops and goes up.",text:"Add a second variable called <b>happiness</b> for all sprites, and a <b>toy</b> sprite. Happiness should start at 10 and drop by 1 every 3 seconds. When someone clicks the toy, it sends a <b>play</b> message, and the pet gets 2 more happiness and spins around. Bonus: if happiness is greater than 8, the pet says I love you! else it says Play with me! Use only blocks from this quest.",answer:"when green flag clicked\nset [happiness v] to (10)\nforever\nwait (3) seconds\nchange [happiness v] by (-1)\nend\n\nwhen this sprite clicked\nbroadcast (play v)\n\nwhen I receive [play v]\nchange [happiness v] by (2)\nrepeat (10)\nturn cw (36) degrees\nend"},checklist:["My energy variable is set at the start and shows on the Stage.","Energy goes down over time with change.","Clicking the food sends a broadcast and the pet eats.","My pet shows its mood with if else."],submit:"Share your pet project and paste the link. Name your project anything you like (idea: My Pocket Pet).",teacherNotes:"PACING (45 min): warmup 3 min, choose pet and food 5 min (do not let students paint today - library sprites are fine), energy variable 6 min, getting hungry 5 min, feed with a message 7 min, show its mood 6 min, not too full 5 min, fall asleep (goal step) 5 min, make it yours + share 3 min. This lesson is the toolkit every later lesson relies on, so prioritize steps 2-7 over decoration.\nMISCONCEPTIONS: (1) set vs change: students use set when they mean change (energy jumps to 3 instead of going up by 3) - the quiz trace targets this. (2) No reset: forgetting set at the green flag, so the old value carries over between runs. (3) Choosing 'For this sprite only' and then not finding the variable on another sprite; explain the choice in step 2 and say 'when unsure, For all sprites'. (4) Typing the word 'energy' into the comparison instead of dragging in the energy reporter block. (5) Reading < and > backwards. (6) Thinking broadcast targets one sprite; every sprite with a matching when I receive hears it. (7) Boundary: 4 < 4 is false.\nACCOMMODATIONS: This is the most abstract trunk lesson. Students with working-memory or attention needs: keep the monitor visible and have them say the value out loud after each test click; a physical 'variable box' (a cup labeled energy with counters) helps show set vs change. Students who read below grade level: the read-aloud plus the screenshots for Make a Variable and Operators carry the step. English learners: post 'less than' and 'greater than' with the symbols and a number line. Students with fine-motor needs may have trouble dropping a reporter into a small operator slot - drop it on the left white oval until it highlights. Fast finishers go to the Play time challenge."}},SQ_RULES=function(){var e=[{id:"t1",branch:"trunk",tier:1,x:500,y:100,requires:[]},{id:"t2",branch:"trunk",tier:2,x:420,y:210,requires:["t1"]},{id:"t3",branch:"trunk",tier:3,x:580,y:320,requires:["t2"]},{id:"t4",branch:"trunk",tier:4,x:420,y:430,requires:["t3"]},{id:"t5",branch:"trunk",tier:5,x:500,y:540,requires:["t4"]},{id:"g1",branch:"games",tier:6,x:180,y:740,requires:["t5"]},{id:"s1",branch:"stories",tier:6,x:500,y:740,requires:["t5"]},{id:"a1",branch:"apps",tier:6,x:820,y:740,requires:["t5"]},{id:"g2",branch:"games",tier:7,x:180,y:860,requires:["g1"]},{id:"s2",branch:"stories",tier:7,x:500,y:860,requires:["s1"]},{id:"a2",branch:"apps",tier:7,x:820,y:860,requires:["a1"]},{id:"m1",branch:"merge",tier:8,x:500,y:1040,requiresAny:["g2","s2","a2"]},{id:"m2",branch:"merge",tier:9,x:500,y:1150,requires:["m1"]},{id:"g3",branch:"games",tier:10,x:180,y:1375,requires:["m2"]},{id: